(window.RK_DATA = window.RK_DATA || {})["base-en"] = {"modules":[{"id":"m00","title":"Start","sub":"What a program is and how to talk to a computer","lessons":[{"id":"m00-1","title":"What programming is","min":4,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A program is a recipe","md":"A **program** is a list of commands for a computer.\n\nAnalogy: a recipe says \"take, mix, put in the oven\". A computer also does the commands one by one, from top to bottom.\n\nA computer is very fast, but it cannot guess. It does **exactly** what is written. Nothing more."},{"t":"text","title":"A programming language","md":"A computer understands only zeros and ones. Writing with them is hard.\n\nSo people invented **programming languages**. You write text that looks a bit like English. A special program translates it into zeros and ones.\n\n**Rust** is one of these languages."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does a computer do if a program has a mistake in its logic?","options":["It understands what you meant and fixes it","It does exactly what is written","It refuses to work and shuts down"],"answer":1,"why":["A computer cannot guess. It does not know what you \"meant\".","Yes. That is why programs are written precisely and carefully.","Usually not. It just does what is written, even if that makes no sense."]},{"t":"text","title":"Why Rust is good","md":"- **Fast.** Rust programs run as fast as programs in C and C++.\n- **Reliable.** It catches many mistakes **before** the program runs.\n- **Popular.** People use it for browsers, games, servers, tools, and even parts of Windows and Linux.\n\n> People say Rust is hard. That is only half true. It is strict, but it explains every complaint it makes. We will go in small steps."},{"t":"text","title":"How the course works","md":"- One lesson takes 5–10 minutes.\n- One screen holds one idea.\n- Press \"Next\" when you are ready. There are no timers.\n- You can make as many mistakes as you want. A mistake is also a step.\n- Tired? Close the tab. The course remembers where you stopped."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What should you do if a step becomes boring or hard?","options":["Force yourself to finish the whole module","Take a break: progress saves itself","Start the course again"],"answer":1,"why":["Usually this only makes it worse. Short breaks help more.","Yes. When you come back, you continue from the same step.","Not needed. Everything you finished is already saved."]},{"t":"deep","title":"Where Rust is really used","md":"- **Firefox.** Its style engine, Stylo, is written in Rust.\n- **Windows and Linux.** Both kernels have parts written in Rust.\n- **Android.** Google writes more and more new system code in Rust.\n- **Discord, Dropbox, Cloudflare.** Servers that need speed.\n- **Developer tools.** For example, `ripgrep` (fast file search) and `uv` (a Python package manager).\n\nRust started in 2010 at the company Mozilla. Version 1.0 came out in 2015. A new compiler version comes out every 6 weeks."},{"t":"recap","items":["A program is a list of commands. The computer runs them in order.","The computer does exactly what is written.","Rust is a fast and strict language. It catches mistakes before the program runs."]}],"goals":["What a program is","What Rust is and why it exists","How this course works"]},{"id":"m00-2","title":"Your first program","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Hello, world!","md":"Almost every programmer's first program prints \"Hello, world!\".\n\nBelow is a real Rust program. Press **\"▶ Run\"**. The code goes to the Rust Playground server, gets compiled, and the result comes back."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    println!(\"Hello, world!\");\n}","out":"Hello, world!","after":"Did it work? You just ran a Rust program."},{"t":"text","title":"Word by word","md":"```\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"Hello, world!\");\n}\n```\n- `fn` is short for **function**. A function is a named piece of a program.\n- `main` is the name of the function. A program **always** starts with the function `main`.\n- `{` and `}` are curly braces. The commands of the function go inside them.\n- `println!` is the command \"print a line\"."},{"t":"text","title":"Small details that matter","md":"```\nprintln!(\"Hello, world!\");\n```\n- Text goes inside double quotes: `\"...\"`.\n- A command ends with a semicolon `;`.\n- `println!` has an exclamation mark. It means this is a **macro** — a special kind of command. More about this at the end of the course. For now, just remember: `println!` is written with `!`."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Which function does every Rust program start with?","options":["`start`","`main`","`println!`"],"answer":1,"why":["There is no such rule. Try again.","Correct. `main` is the main function, the entry point of the program.","`println!` prints text. The program does not start with it."]},{"t":"order","explain":"First the function header, then the command inside, then the closing brace.","md":"Build a program that prints \"Hi\".","lines":["fn main() {","    println!(\"Hi\");","}"]},{"t":"task","md":"Change the program so that it prints **My name is Rust**.","code":"fn main() {\n    println!(\"Hello, world!\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    println!(\"My name is Rust\");\n}","expect":"My name is Rust","hints":["Change only the text inside the quotes.","The line should be: `println!(\"My name is Rust\");`"]},{"t":"recap","items":["A program starts with `fn main() { ... }`.","`println!(\"text\");` prints a line.","Text goes in quotes, and a command ends with `;`."]}],"goals":["Write and run your first program","Learn what `fn main` and `println!` are"]},{"id":"m00-3","title":"The compiler is your strict friend","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"What a compiler is","md":"A **compiler** is a program that translates your code into zeros and ones.\n\nRust's compiler is called `rustc`. Before translating, it checks the code carefully. If it finds a problem, it does not translate. It writes a message instead.\n\n> Analogy: a teacher who checks an essay before it goes to print. Strict, but always explains what is wrong."},{"t":"text","title":"Errors are normal","md":"**Everyone** sees compile errors: beginners and professionals with 20 years of experience.\n\nAn error does not mean \"I am not trying hard enough\". It means \"the compiler found a place that needs to be more precise\". It is a hint, not a grade."},{"t":"code","fails":true,"md":"A semicolon is missing here. Run it and see what the compiler says.","code":"fn main() {\n    println!(\"One\")\n    println!(\"Two\");\n}","after":"The compiler writes `expected ;` and shows with an arrow exactly where."},{"t":"text","title":"How to read an error","md":"An error message usually has three parts:\n1. `error` and a short description: what is wrong.\n2. A line number and a piece of code with an arrow `^`: where it is.\n3. Sometimes `help`: how to fix it.\n\nRead **from top to bottom** and fix the first error first. Often the others disappear when the first one is fixed."},{"t":"quiz","md":"A program has five errors. Which one should you start with?","options":["The first one","The last one","The shortest one"],"answer":0,"why":["Yes. One error often causes a whole chain of other errors.","The last ones are often an echo of the first one. Start at the top.","The length of the message does not matter. The order does."]},{"t":"task","md":"Fix the program: the compiler complains. Press \"Run\", read the message, and fix it.","code":"fn main() {\n    println(\"I fixed the program!\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    println!(\"I fixed the program!\");\n}","expect":"I fixed the program!","hints":["Look carefully at the name of the command. Something is missing.","`println` needs an exclamation mark: `println!`."]},{"t":"deep","title":"What the compiler does, step by step","md":"In simple terms, compiling goes like this:\n1. **Parsing.** The compiler reads the code and builds a tree from it: where a function is, where a command is, where text is.\n2. **Name checking.** Do all variables and functions exist?\n3. **Type checking.** Are you adding a number to text?\n4. **Borrow checking.** A special Rust check about who uses data and when. Modules 5 and 6 are about it.\n5. **Optimization.** The compiler makes the program faster. A part called LLVM does this.\n6. **Machine code.** The result is a file that the processor runs directly. On Windows it is an `.exe` file.\n\nErrors appear in steps 1–4. If those pass, the program almost always works the way it is written."},{"t":"recap","items":["The compiler checks the code before it runs.","Errors are hints, not failures.","Read errors from top to bottom and start with the first one."]}],"goals":["What a compiler is","How to read error messages"]},{"id":"m00-4","title":"Several lines and comments","min":5,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Commands run in order","md":"You can write many commands inside `main`. They run from top to bottom, one after another."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    println!(\"Step 1: wake up\");\n    println!(\"Step 2: drink water\");\n    println!(\"Step 3: learn Rust\");\n}","out":"Step 1: wake up\nStep 2: drink water\nStep 3: learn Rust"},{"t":"text","title":"Comments","md":"A **comment** is a note for a person. The computer skips it.\n\nA comment starts with two slashes `//` and goes to the end of the line.\n\n```\nfn main() {\n    // This is a comment. It does nothing.\n    println!(\"And this is a command\"); // a comment can also be at the end of a line\n}\n```"},{"t":"quiz","md":"What will the program print?\n```\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"A\");\n    // println!(\"B\");\n    println!(\"C\");\n}\n```","options":["A, B, C","A, C","Only C"],"answer":1,"why":["The line with \"B\" is commented out. The computer skips it.","Correct. A commented-out line does not run.","The first line is a normal command. It runs."]},{"t":"text","title":"An empty line and `print!`","md":"- `println!()` with no text prints an empty line.\n- `print!` prints **without** moving to a new line.\n\n```\nfn main() {\n    print!(\"One, \");\n    print!(\"two, \");\n    println!(\"three!\");\n    println!();\n    println!(\"The end\");\n}\n```"},{"t":"task","outputCheck":"exact","md":"Write a program that prints three lines:","code":"fn main() {\n    // write three commands here\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    println!(\"Rust\");\n    println!(\"is\");\n    println!(\"fun\");\n}","expect":"Rust\nis\nfun","hints":["You need three `println!` commands.","The first one: `println!(\"Rust\");`"]},{"t":"text","title":"Quotes and curly braces inside text","md":"Sometimes the text itself needs quotes or curly braces. There are special ways to write them:\n- `\\\"` — a quote inside text;\n- `\\n` — a new line;\n- `{{` and `}}` — curly braces in `println!`."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    println!(\"He said: \\\"Hello\\\"\");\n    println!(\"First line\\nSecond line\");\n    println!(\"Curly braces: {{ and }}\");\n}","out":"He said: \"Hello\"\nFirst line\nSecond line\nCurly braces: { and }","after":"The backslash `\\` means \"the next character is special\". This is called **escaping**."},{"t":"recap","items":["Commands run from top to bottom.","`// ...` is a comment. The computer does not read it.","`println!` moves to a new line, `print!` does not."]}],"goals":["Print several lines","Write comments for people"]},{"id":"m00-p","title":"Practice: first programs","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"How practice works","md":"\"Practice\" lessons have almost nothing new. They only have tasks about what you already learned.\n\nEvery task has a \"Hint\" button. When the hints run out, the button shows the solution. Looking at the solution is fine."},{"t":"task","md":"Print a three-line business card. Keep the labels `Name:`, `Age:`, and `Favorite:` in that order. Use your own name and favorite activity, and write your age as a whole number zero or greater.","code":"fn main() {\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    println!(\"Name: Rust\");\n    println!(\"Age: 11\");\n    println!(\"Favorite: speed\");\n}","expect":"Name: Rust\nAge: 11\nFavorite: speed","expectFormat":"Name: {{text}}\nAge: {{int}}\nFavorite: {{text}}","expectPattern":"Name[ \\t]*:[ \\t]*\\S[^\\r\\n]*\\nAge[ \\t]*:[ \\t]*[0-9]+\\nFavorite[ \\t]*:[ \\t]*\\S[^\\r\\n]*","hints":["Try three `println!` commands, one per line.","For the first line, try `println!(\"Name: Rust\");` with a name of your choice."]},{"t":"task","outputCheck":"exact","md":"The program has three errors. Fix it. It must print `One` and `Two` on separate lines.","code":"fn main()\n    println(\"One\");\n    println!(\"Two\")\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    println!(\"One\");\n    println!(\"Two\");\n}","expect":"One\nTwo","hints":["The errors: the opening brace `{` is missing, the first command has no `!`, the second has no `;`.","The first line must be `fn main() {`"]},{"t":"task","outputCheck":"exact","md":"Using `print!` and one `println!`, print `1 2 3` on one line.","code":"fn main() {\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    print!(\"1 \");\n    print!(\"2 \");\n    println!(\"3\");\n}","expect":"1 2 3","hints":["`print!` does not move to a new line. Do not forget the spaces inside the quotes.","`print!(\"1 \"); print!(\"2 \"); println!(\"3\");`"]},{"t":"task","outputCheck":"exact","md":"Print this sentence with quotes exactly: `Rust says: \"Don't panic\"`.","code":"fn main() {\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    println!(\"Rust says: \\\"Don't panic\\\"\");\n}","expect":"Rust says: \"Don't panic\"","hints":["A quote inside text is written as `\\\"`.","`println!(\"Rust says: \\\"Don't panic\\\"\");`"]},{"t":"recap","items":["If something does not work, read the compiler error first.","Small details matter: `!`, `;`, braces, quotes."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Practice `fn main`, `println!` and `print!`","Fix broken programs"]}]},{"id":"m01","title":"Variables and types","sub":"How a program remembers data","lessons":[{"id":"m01-1","title":"A variable is a labeled box","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A box with a label","md":"Analogy: a **variable** is a box with a label. The label is the name. The value is inside.\n\nIn Rust you create the box with the word `let`:\n\n```\nlet age = 25;\n```\nRead it like this: \"let `age` be 25\"."},{"t":"code","md":"To print a variable, put its name in curly braces inside the text.","code":"fn main() {\n    let age = 25;\n    let name = \"Anna\";\n    println!(\"{name}, you are {age} years old\");\n}","out":"Anna, you are 25 years old","after":"Try changing the name and the age, then run it again."},{"t":"text","title":"Another way to print","md":"You can leave the braces empty `{}` and list the variables after the text, separated by commas. They are inserted in order.\n\n```\nlet a = 2;\nlet b = 3;\nprintln!(\"{} plus {} equals {}\", a, b, a + b);\n```\nIt prints: `2 plus 3 equals 5`."},{"t":"fill","explain":"`let` creates a variable, and `{cat}` inserts its value into the text.","md":"Fill in the blanks so the program prints `Cat: Tom`.","code":"fn main() {\n    ___ cat = \"Tom\";\n    println!(\"Cat: {___}\");\n}","answers":[["let"],["cat"]]},{"t":"text","title":"How to name variables","md":"- Only Latin letters, digits and `_`.\n- Separate words with an underscore: `user_name`, `max_speed`.\n- The name should explain what is inside. `price` is better than `p`.\n\n> This style is called snake_case. All Rust programmers write names this way."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Which variable name follows the Rust style?","options":["`UserName`","`user_name`","`user-name`"],"answer":1,"why":["In Rust this style is used for types, not variables.","Correct. Lowercase letters and an underscore.","A dash means minus. The compiler reads this as \"user minus name\"."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`let name = value;` creates a variable.","`{name}` inside text inserts the value.","Names are written in snake_case: `my_value`."]}],"goals":["What a variable is","How to create one with `let`","How to print its value"]},{"id":"m01-2","title":"Changing variables: mut","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"The box is sealed","md":"In Rust a variable is **immutable** by default. Once you put a value in, the box is sealed.\n\nWhy? So that a value does not change by accident somewhere in the middle of a big program."},{"t":"code","fails":true,"md":"Run it and read the error.","code":"fn main() {\n    let score = 10;\n    score = 20;\n    println!(\"{score}\");\n}","after":"The compiler says `cannot assign twice to immutable variable`. It even suggests a fix in `help`."},{"t":"text","title":"The word mut","md":"To allow changes, add `mut` (short for mutable — changeable):\n\n```\nlet mut score = 10;\nscore = 20;\n```\nNow it works. `mut` is an honest label: \"this variable will change\"."},{"t":"task","md":"Fix the program. It must print `Points: 15`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let points = 10;\n    points = points + 5;\n    println!(\"Points: {points}\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let mut points = 10;\n    points = points + 5;\n    println!(\"Points: {points}\");\n}","expect":"Points: 15","hints":["The compiler complains that the variable cannot be changed.","Add `mut` after `let`."]},{"t":"text","title":"Short forms","md":"Instead of `x = x + 5` you can write `x += 5`. The same works with `-=`, `*=`, `/=`.\n\n```\nlet mut lives = 3;\nlives -= 1; // now 2\n```"},{"t":"quiz","md":"What will the program print?\n```\nfn main() {\n    let mut coins = 5;\n    coins += 10;\n    coins -= 3;\n    println!(\"{coins}\");\n}\n```","options":["5","12","A compile error"],"answer":1,"why":["The value changed twice. Count: 5 + 10 − 3.","Correct: 5 + 10 − 3 = 12.","There is `mut`, so changing is allowed."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Variables cannot be changed by default.","`let mut` allows changes.","`x += 1` is a short form of `x = x + 1`."]}],"goals":["Why you cannot simply change a variable","How to allow changes with `mut`"]},{"id":"m01-3","title":"Numbers","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Every value has a type","md":"A **type** says what is in the box: a number, text, yes/no.\n\nUsually Rust works out the type by itself. But you can write it explicitly, after a colon:\n\n```\nlet age: i32 = 30;\nlet price: f64 = 99.5;\n```"},{"t":"text","title":"Whole numbers","md":"`i32` is a signed whole number: it can be negative. This is the default type.\n\n- The letter `i` means integer, a whole number.\n- `u` means unsigned: only zero and above. For example `u8`, `u32`, `u64`.\n- The digits say how many bits the number uses. More bits means a bigger maximum.\n\n`u8` holds 0 to 255. `i32` holds about ±2 billion. Usually `i32` is enough."},{"t":"text","title":"Fractional numbers","md":"Fractional numbers use a **dot**: `3.14`.\n\nThe default type is `f64` (f means float, \"floating point\").\n\n!> You cannot add a whole number and a fractional number directly. `5 + 2.5` is an error. Write `5.0 + 2.5`."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let a = 7;\n    let b = 2;\n    println!(\"Sum: {}\", a + b);\n    println!(\"Difference: {}\", a - b);\n    println!(\"Product: {}\", a * b);\n    println!(\"Integer division: {}\", a / b);\n    println!(\"Remainder: {}\", a % b);\n    println!(\"Fractional division: {}\", 7.0 / 2.0);\n}","out":"Sum: 9\nDifference: 5\nProduct: 14\nInteger division: 3\nRemainder: 1\nFractional division: 3.5","after":"Notice: `7 / 2` with whole numbers gives `3`. The fractional part is simply dropped."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What is `10 % 3`?","options":["3","1","3.33"],"answer":1,"why":["`%` is not division. It is the remainder of division.","Correct. 10 = 3 × 3 + 1. The remainder is 1.","Whole numbers have no fractional part."]},{"t":"text","title":"Turning a whole number into a fractional one","md":"To mix whole and fractional numbers, convert the type with the word `as`:\n\n```\nlet apples = 7;\nlet half = apples as f64 / 2.0; // 3.5\n```"},{"t":"task","md":"You have a temperature in degrees Celsius. Convert it to Fahrenheit with the formula `F = C × 1.8 + 32` and print `Fahrenheit: 77`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let celsius = 25.0;\n    let fahrenheit = 0.0; // fix this line\n    println!(\"Fahrenheit: {fahrenheit}\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let celsius = 25.0;\n    let fahrenheit = celsius * 1.8 + 32.0;\n    println!(\"Fahrenheit: {fahrenheit}\");\n}","expect":"Fahrenheit: 77","hints":["Multiplication is `*`, addition is `+`.","Remember: the fractional number is written `32.0`, not `32`."]},{"t":"text","title":"Writing big numbers","md":"You can put underscores in a number to make it easier to read. The computer ignores them:\n\n```\nlet million = 1_000_000;\n```\nYou can also write the type at the end of the number: `5u8`, `10i64`, `2.5f32`."},{"t":"deep","title":"What happens when a number does not fit","md":"Every whole-number type has a limit. `u8` holds at most 255. What happens if you add 1 to 255?\n\n- In a normal run (debug mode) the program **panics**: `attempt to add with overflow`.\n- If you need different behavior, you choose it explicitly with special methods.\n\n- `checked_add` returns \"nothing\" (`None`) if the result does not fit;\n- `wrapping_add` goes around in a circle: after 255 comes 0;\n- `saturating_add` stops at the limit.","code":"fn main() {\n    let x: u8 = 255;\n    println!(\"{:?}\", x.checked_add(1));\n    println!(\"{}\", x.wrapping_add(1));\n    println!(\"{}\", x.saturating_add(1));\n    println!(\"u8 limit: {}, i32 limit: {}\", u8::MAX, i32::MAX);\n}","out":"None\n0\n255\nu8 limit: 255, i32 limit: 2147483647"},{"t":"recap","items":["`i32` is a whole number, `f64` is a fractional number. These are the default types.","Integer division drops the fraction: `7 / 2 == 3`. `%` is the remainder.","Whole to fractional: `x as f64`."]}],"goals":["Whole numbers and fractional numbers","What `i32` and `f64` mean","Arithmetic"]},{"id":"m01-4","title":"Yes, no, and characters","min":5,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"bool — true or false","md":"The `bool` type holds one of two values: `true` or `false`.\n\n```\nlet is_raining = true;\nlet has_umbrella = false;\n```\nAnalogy: a light switch is either on or off."},{"t":"text","title":"Comparisons give a bool","md":"- `==` equal (two signs! one `=` is assignment)\n- `!=` not equal\n- `<`, `>` less, greater\n- `<=`, `>=` less or equal, greater or equal\n\n```\nlet adult = 20 >= 18; // true\n```"},{"t":"text","title":"And, or, not","md":"- `&&` means \"and\": true if both parts are true.\n- `||` means \"or\": true if at least one part is true.\n- `!` means \"not\": it flips the value.\n\n```\nlet go_outside = !is_raining || has_umbrella;\n```\nRead it as: \"we go outside if it is not raining or if we have an umbrella\"."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What is `5 > 3 && 2 > 4`?","options":["true","false"],"answer":1,"why":["The first part is true, but the second is not. `&&` needs both.","Correct. `2 > 4` is false, so the whole expression is false."]},{"t":"text","title":"char — one character","md":"The `char` type holds **one** character. It is written in **single** quotes:\n\n```\nlet letter = 'Z';\nlet smile = '☺';\n```\n!> `'a'` is a character (char). `\"a\"` is text (a string). They are different types."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Which one is a `char`?","options":["`\"B\"`","`'B'`","`B`"],"answer":1,"why":["Double quotes mean a string, even with one letter.","Correct. Single quotes mean one character.","Without quotes the compiler thinks it is a variable name."]},{"t":"deep","title":"How much space a char takes","md":"A `char` in Rust takes **4 bytes**. It can hold any Unicode character: letters of all alphabets, Chinese characters, emoji.\n\nEvery character has a number in the Unicode table. You can get it with `as u32`:","code":"fn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", 'A' as u32);\n    println!(\"{}\", 'Я' as u32);\n    println!(\"{}\", char::from_u32(1071).unwrap());\n}","out":"65\n1071\nЯ"},{"t":"recap","items":["`bool`: `true` or `false`.","`==` compares, `=` assigns.","`&&` and, `||` or, `!` not.","`char` is one character in single quotes."]}],"goals":["The `bool` type: true or false","The `char` type: one character","Comparisons"]},{"id":"m01-5","title":"Constants and shadowing","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A constant","md":"A **constant** is a value that never changes. For example, the number of seconds in a minute.\n\n```\nconst SECONDS_IN_MINUTE: u32 = 60;\n```\nRules:\n- It is written in capital letters.\n- The type is **required**.\n- `mut` is not allowed."},{"t":"text","title":"Shadowing","md":"You can write `let` again with the same name. This creates a **new** variable, and the old one becomes hidden. This is called **shadowing**.\n\n```\nlet x = 5;\nlet x = x * 2; // a new x = 10\n```\n> Analogy: a new label stuck over the old label. The old box still exists, but we cannot see it anymore."},{"t":"code","md":"Shadowing even lets you change the type:","code":"fn main() {\n    let input = \"42\";\n    let input: i32 = input.parse().unwrap();\n    println!(\"The number plus one: {}\", input + 1);\n}","out":"The number plus one: 43","after":"Here `parse()` turns text into a number. For now `unwrap()` just means \"we trust that it worked\". Details are in the module about errors."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What will the program print?\n```\nfn main() {\n    let a = 3;\n    let a = a + 1;\n    let a = a * 10;\n    println!(\"{a}\");\n}\n```","options":["3","40","An error: the variable has no mut"],"answer":1,"why":["Each `let` creates a new `a` from the old one. Count step by step.","Correct: 3 → 4 → 40.","Shadowing is not changing. It is a new variable. `mut` is not needed."]},{"t":"task","md":"Create a constant `MAX_LIVES` of type `u32` with the value 3 and print `Lives: 3`.","code":"// create the constant here\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"Lives: {}\", MAX_LIVES);\n}","solution":"const MAX_LIVES: u32 = 3;\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"Lives: {}\", MAX_LIVES);\n}","expect":"Lives: 3","hints":["The format: `const NAME: type = value;`","`const MAX_LIVES: u32 = 3;`"]},{"t":"deep","title":"Why changing is not allowed by default","md":"In big programs, a lot of code reads the same data. If data can change at any moment, it is hard to find where a bug comes from.\n\nRust makes immutability the normal case. Then `mut` works as a marker: \"attention, the value changes here\". Such code is easier to read.\n\nThere is a second reason: immutable data can be safely given to several parts of a program at once, and even to several threads. Threads are in module 17."},{"t":"recap","items":["`const NAME: type = value;` is a constant forever.","Writing `let` again with the same name is shadowing: a new variable.","Shadowing can change the type, `mut` cannot."]}],"goals":["Constants with `const`","Shadowing: a new variable with an old name"]},{"id":"m01-p","title":"Practice: variables and numbers","min":10,"cards":[{"t":"fill","md":"Fill in the types: a signed whole number, a fractional number, a boolean, a character.","code":"let a: ___ = -5;\nlet b: ___ = 2.5;\nlet c: ___ = true;\nlet d: ___ = 'z';","answers":[["i32","i64","i8","i16","i128","isize"],["f64","f32"],["bool"],["char"]]},{"t":"task","outputCheck":"exact","md":"Calculate the area and the perimeter of a 7 by 3 rectangle. Print two lines: `Area: 21` and `Perimeter: 20`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let width = 7;\n    let height = 3;\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let width = 7;\n    let height = 3;\n    println!(\"Area: {}\", width * height);\n    println!(\"Perimeter: {}\", 2 * (width + height));\n}","expect":"Area: 21\nPerimeter: 20","hints":["The area is `width * height`. The perimeter is `2 * (width + height)`.","`println!(\"Area: {}\", width * height);`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Convert 135 seconds into minutes and seconds. Print `2 min 15 sec`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let total = 135;\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let total = 135;\n    let minutes = total / 60;\n    let seconds = total % 60;\n    println!(\"{minutes} min {seconds} sec\");\n}","expect":"2 min 15 sec","hints":["Integer division `/` gives the minutes. The remainder `%` gives the seconds.","`let minutes = total / 60; let seconds = total % 60;`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Fix the program. It must print `Average: 2.5`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let sum = 10;\n    let count = 4;\n    let avg = sum / count;\n    println!(\"Average: {avg}\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let sum = 10;\n    let count = 4;\n    let avg = sum as f64 / count as f64;\n    println!(\"Average: {avg}\");\n}","expect":"Average: 2.5","hints":["Right now the division is integer division, and the fractional part is lost.","Convert both numbers to `f64`: `sum as f64 / count as f64`."]},{"t":"task","md":"A player has a score of 15. Add 50 points, then subtract 20, then double it. Print `Score: 90`. Use `+=`, `-=`, `*=`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let score = 15;\n    println!(\"Score: {score}\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let mut score = 15;\n    score += 50;\n    score -= 20;\n    score *= 2;\n    println!(\"Score: {score}\");\n}","expect":"Score: 90","hints":["The variable must be `mut`.","15 + 50 = 65, 65 − 20 = 45, 45 × 2 = 90."]},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `println!(\"{}\", 17 / 5 * 5 + 17 % 5);` print?","options":["17","17.0","3"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct. 17 / 5 = 3, 3 × 5 = 15, 17 % 5 = 2, 15 + 2 = 17.","All numbers are whole, so there is no decimal point.","That is only the first part: 17 / 5."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Whole numbers divide without a remainder; the remainder comes from `%`.","For a fractional result you need `as f64`.","If you change a variable, you need `mut`."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Create variables and change them","Calculate with whole and fractional numbers"]}]},{"id":"m02","title":"Functions","sub":"How to split a program into small pieces","lessons":[{"id":"m02-1","title":"Your own function","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A function is a small program","md":"A **function** is a piece of code with a name. You write it once and call it as many times as you want.\n\n> Analogy: a button on a remote control. You press \"louder\" and do not think about what happens inside.\n\n`main` is also a function. It is special: everything starts with it."},{"t":"code","code":"fn greet() {\n    println!(\"Hello!\");\n    println!(\"Nice to see you.\");\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    greet();\n    greet();\n}","out":"Hello!\nNice to see you.\nHello!\nNice to see you.","after":"`greet();` is a **call**: \"run the function greet\". The parentheses are required."},{"t":"quiz","md":"How many times is \"Hello!\" printed if `main` has `greet();` three times?","options":["1","3","6"],"answer":1,"why":["Every call runs the function again.","Correct. One call means one run.","`greet` has one \"Hello!\" line. Three calls means three times."]},{"t":"order","md":"Build the program: the function `hello` and its call from `main`.","lines":["fn hello() {","    println!(\"hello\");","}","fn main() {","    hello();","}"]},{"t":"text","title":"Comments for a function","md":"Before a function you can write a comment with **three** slashes `///`. This is a **documentation comment**. The editor shows it when you hover the mouse over the function name.\n\n```\n/// Prints a greeting.\nfn greet() {\n    println!(\"Hello!\");\n}\n```\nFunction names are written like variable names: lowercase letters with `_`. For example `print_report`."},{"t":"recap","items":["`fn name() { ... }` declares a function.","`name();` calls it.","The order of functions in a file does not matter."]}],"goals":["Why functions are needed","How to write your own function and call it"]},{"id":"m02-2","title":"Parameters","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Functions need data","md":"The function `greet` always says the same thing. Boring. We want to pass it a name.\n\nFor that there are **parameters** — variables in the parentheses:\n\n```\nfn greet(name: &str) {\n    println!(\"Hello, {name}!\");\n}\n```\n`&str` is the type of text. More about it in the module about borrowing."},{"t":"text","title":"The parameter type is required","md":"Inside a function Rust works out types by itself. For parameters it does **not**. You must always write the type.\n\n```\nfn show_age(age: i32) { ... }\nfn area(width: f64, height: f64) { ... }\n```\nSeveral parameters are separated by commas."},{"t":"code","code":"fn print_sum(a: i32, b: i32) {\n    println!(\"{a} + {b} = {}\", a + b);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    print_sum(2, 3);\n    print_sum(10, -4);\n}","out":"2 + 3 = 5\n10 + -4 = 6"},{"t":"fill","explain":"A parameter is written as `name: type`.","md":"Complete the function so it takes a number `n` of type `i32`.","code":"fn print_double(___: ___) {\n    println!(\"{}\", n * 2);\n}","answers":[["n"],["i32"]]},{"t":"task","md":"Write a function `say_twice` that prints the given text two times. The call is already in `main`.","code":"// write the function say_twice here\n\nfn main() {\n    say_twice(\"Rust\");\n}","solution":"fn say_twice(text: &str) {\n    println!(\"{text}\");\n    println!(\"{text}\");\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    say_twice(\"Rust\");\n}","expect":"Rust\nRust","hints":["Start with `fn say_twice(text: &str) {`","Inside — two lines `println!(\"{text}\");`"]},{"t":"recap","items":["Parameters are the input data of a function.","Always write the parameter type: `fn f(x: i32)`.","When calling, pass values: `f(5)`."]}],"goals":["Pass data into a function","Write the types of parameters"]},{"id":"m02-3","title":"Returning a value","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A function can give something back","md":"A function can not only print, but also **return** a result. Analogy: a calculator. You give it numbers, you get an answer.\n\nThe result type is written after an arrow `->`:\n\n```\nfn double(x: i32) -> i32 {\n    x * 2\n}\n```"},{"t":"text","title":"The last line has no semicolon","md":"Notice: `x * 2` is written **without** `;`. This is not a typo.\n\nIn Rust, the last line of a function without `;` is the result. It is called an **expression**: it has a value.\n\nA line with `;` is a **statement**. It does something, but gives no value."},{"t":"code","code":"fn square(n: i32) -> i32 {\n    n * n\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let result = square(7);\n    println!(\"7 squared = {result}\");\n    println!(\"3 squared = {}\", square(3));\n}","out":"7 squared = 49\n3 squared = 9"},{"t":"code","fails":true,"md":"Now with an extra `;`. Run it and look at the error.","code":"fn square(n: i32) -> i32 {\n    n * n;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", square(3));\n}","after":"The compiler writes `mismatched types`: the function promised to return `i32`, but returned \"nothing\" `()`. It also suggests: `remove this semicolon`."},{"t":"text","title":"The word return","md":"You can also return a value explicitly with `return`. Usually this is used to leave **before** the end of the function:\n\n```\nfn safe_div(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {\n    if b == 0 {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    a / b\n}\n```"},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does this function return for `add_one(5)`?\n```\nfn add_one(x: i32) -> i32 {\n    x + 1\n}\n```","options":["6","5","Nothing, because there is no `return`"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct. The last line without `;` is the result.","The function adds one.","`return` is not needed: the last expression is returned automatically."]},{"t":"task","md":"Write a function `max` that returns the larger of two numbers. The program must print `9`.","code":"fn max(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", max(4, 9));\n}","solution":"fn max(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {\n    if a > b { a } else { b }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", max(4, 9));\n}","expect":"9","hints":["You need `if a > b { ... } else { ... }`. Details about `if` are in the next module.","`if` also returns a value: `if a > b { a } else { b }` — without semicolons."]},{"t":"deep","title":"What () is","md":"A function without `->` also returns something: the value `()`. It is called **unit**. It is the value \"nothing useful\".\n\nThat is why the error with an extra `;` says `expected i32, found ()`: a line with `;` gives `()`, not a number.","code":"fn nothing() {}\n\nfn main() {\n    let x = nothing();\n    println!(\"{:?}\", x);\n}","out":"()"},{"t":"recap","items":["`-> type` says what the function returns.","The last expression without `;` is the result.","An extra `;` at the end turns the result into \"nothing\" `()`.","`return` leaves the function early."]}],"goals":["How a function returns a result","The difference between an expression and a statement","Why the last line without `;` matters"]},{"id":"m02-4","title":"Blocks and scope","min":5,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A block is also an expression","md":"Any curly braces `{ }` form a **block**. The last expression of a block is the value of the block.\n\n```\nlet total = {\n    let a = 2;\n    let b = 3;\n    a + b\n}; // total = 5\n```"},{"t":"text","title":"A variable lives inside its block","md":"**Scope** is the part of the code where a variable is visible. Usually it is the block where the variable was created.\n\n```\nfn main() {\n    {\n        let secret = 42;\n        println!(\"{secret}\"); // visible here\n    }\n    // println!(\"{secret}\"); // not here anymore — an error\n}\n```\n> Analogy: a room. A thing left inside is not visible from the hallway."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What happens if you uncomment the last line in the example above?","options":["It prints 42","A compile error: `secret` is not found","It prints 0"],"answer":1,"why":["The variable `secret` disappeared together with its block.","Correct. After the closing `}` the variable no longer exists.","Rust does not invent values. It just does not find the variable."]},{"t":"deep","title":"The call stack","md":"When `main` calls a function `a`, and `a` calls `b`, the computer remembers the chain: `main → a → b`. This chain is called the **call stack**.\n\n- Each call puts its own \"frame\" on the stack: parameters and local variables.\n- When a function ends, its frame is removed, and its variables disappear.\n\nThat is why a function's variables are not visible outside: their frame is already gone. If a program panics, Rust can show this stack — it is called a **backtrace**."},{"t":"recap","items":["`{ ... }` is a block. Its value is the last expression.","A variable is visible only inside the block where it was created."]}],"goals":["What a block `{ }` is","Where a variable \"lives\""]},{"id":"m02-p","title":"Practice: functions","min":10,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Write a function `is_even` that returns `true` for an even number.","code":"fn is_even(n: i32) -> bool {\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{} {}\", is_even(4), is_even(7));\n}","solution":"fn is_even(n: i32) -> bool {\n    n % 2 == 0\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{} {}\", is_even(4), is_even(7));\n}","expect":"true false","hints":["A number is even if the remainder of dividing by 2 is 0.","The body is one line without `;`: `n % 2 == 0`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Write a function `average3` that returns the average of three fractional numbers.","code":"// write the function average3\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", average3(2.0, 4.0, 9.0));\n}","solution":"fn average3(a: f64, b: f64, c: f64) -> f64 {\n    (a + b + c) / 3.0\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", average3(2.0, 4.0, 9.0));\n}","expect":"5","hints":["The header: `fn average3(a: f64, b: f64, c: f64) -> f64`","The body: `(a + b + c) / 3.0`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Fix the function. It must print `Total: 30`.","code":"fn total(price: i32, count: i32) {\n    price * count;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"Total: {}\", total(10, 3));\n}","solution":"fn total(price: i32, count: i32) -> i32 {\n    price * count\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"Total: {}\", total(10, 3));\n}","expect":"Total: 30","hints":["There are two mistakes: the function does not promise to return a number, and the last line has an extra `;`.","You need `-> i32` in the header and `price * count` without `;`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Write a function `greet(name: &str, times: i32)` that prints `Hello, name!` the given number of times.","code":"// write the function greet\n\nfn main() {\n    greet(\"Anna\", 2);\n}","solution":"fn greet(name: &str, times: i32) {\n    for _ in 0..times {\n        println!(\"Hello, {name}!\");\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    greet(\"Anna\", 2);\n}","expect":"Hello, Anna!\nHello, Anna!","hints":["The loop `for _ in 0..times { ... }` repeats code. Loops are explained in the next module.","Inside the loop: `println!(\"Hello, {name}!\");`"]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `clamp_score` must return the number, but not less than 0 and not more than 100. Use `return` to leave early.","code":"fn clamp_score(x: i32) -> i32 {\n    x\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{} {} {}\", clamp_score(-5), clamp_score(50), clamp_score(140));\n}","solution":"fn clamp_score(x: i32) -> i32 {\n    if x < 0 {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    if x > 100 {\n        return 100;\n    }\n    x\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{} {} {}\", clamp_score(-5), clamp_score(50), clamp_score(140));\n}","expect":"0 50 100","hints":["Two checks: `if x < 0 { return 0; }` and `if x > 100 { return 100; }`.","At the end return `x` without `;`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`-> type` is needed if the function returns a value.","The last expression without `;` is the result.","`return` leaves early."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Write functions with parameters and results","Fix mistakes in functions"]}]},{"id":"m03","title":"Conditions and loops","sub":"How a program chooses and repeats","lessons":[{"id":"m03-1","title":"if and else","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"If… then…","md":"`if` runs a block only when the condition is true.\n\n```\nif temperature > 30 {\n    println!(\"It is hot!\");\n}\n```\nThe condition must be of type `bool`. Parentheses around the condition are not needed."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let hour = 14;\n    if hour < 12 {\n        println!(\"Good morning\");\n    } else if hour < 18 {\n        println!(\"Good afternoon\");\n    } else {\n        println!(\"Good evening\");\n    }\n}","out":"Good afternoon","after":"Rust checks the conditions from top to bottom and runs the **first** matching branch. Change `hour` and run it again."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does the program print with `hour = 9`?","options":["Good morning","Good afternoon","Good morning and Good afternoon"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct. The very first condition `9 < 12` is true.","The second branch is never reached: the first one already matched.","Only one branch runs."]},{"t":"text","title":"if returns a value","md":"Like a block, `if` also gives a value. You can put it into a variable:\n\n```\nlet label = if age >= 18 { \"adult\" } else { \"child\" };\n```\n!> Both branches must return **the same type**."},{"t":"task","md":"Write a program: if `number` is even, print `even`, otherwise print `odd`. Right now `number = 7`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let number = 7;\n    // your code\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let number = 7;\n    if number % 2 == 0 {\n        println!(\"even\");\n    } else {\n        println!(\"odd\");\n    }\n}","expect":"odd","hints":["A number is even if the remainder of dividing by 2 is 0.","The condition: `number % 2 == 0`."]},{"t":"deep","title":"Why Rust has no \"?:\" operator","md":"Many languages have a short form of a condition: `condition ? yes : no`. Rust does not.\n\nIt is not needed: in Rust, `if` itself returns a value. So the same thing is written like this:\n\n```\nlet price = if is_member { 90 } else { 100 };\n```\nThis is one of Rust's ideas: almost everything is an **expression** and gives a value. That means fewer special rules."},{"t":"recap","items":["`if condition { } else if condition { } else { }`.","The first matching branch runs.","`if` can return a value; the branch types must match."]}],"goals":["Run code only when a condition holds","Choose between options"]},{"id":"m03-2","title":"The loop loop","min":5,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Repeat until I say stop","md":"A **loop** repeats code. The simplest one is `loop`. It repeats forever until it reaches `break`."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut count = 1;\n    loop {\n        println!(\"Round {count}\");\n        if count == 3 {\n            break;\n        }\n        count += 1;\n    }\n    println!(\"Done!\");\n}","out":"Round 1\nRound 2\nRound 3\nDone!"},{"t":"text","title":"continue — skip a step","md":"`continue` jumps straight to the next repetition and skips the rest of the block.\n\n> Analogy: `break` means leaving the room. `continue` means leaving and immediately coming back in."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What happens if a `loop` has no `break`?","options":["The loop runs once","The loop runs forever","A compile error"],"answer":1,"why":["`loop` repeats the block again and again.","Yes. The program hangs until someone stops it.","The compiler allows infinite loops: sometimes they are really needed."]},{"t":"text","title":"break can return a value","md":"```\nlet mut n = 1;\nlet result = loop {\n    n *= 2;\n    if n > 100 {\n        break n;\n    }\n};\n// result = 128\n```"},{"t":"deep","title":"Loop labels","md":"If one loop is inside another, `break` leaves only the **inner** one. To leave the outer loop directly, give the loop a **label** — a name with an apostrophe:\n\n`continue 'outer` works the same way: it goes to the next step of the outer loop.","code":"fn main() {\n    'outer: for a in 1..=3 {\n        for b in 1..=3 {\n            if a * b == 4 {\n                println!(\"Found: {a} × {b}\");\n                break 'outer;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}","out":"Found: 2 × 2"},{"t":"recap","items":["`loop { }` repeats forever.","`break` leaves, `break value` leaves with a result.","`continue` goes straight to the next repetition."]}],"goals":["Repeat code forever","Leave a loop with `break`"]},{"id":"m03-3","title":"while and for loops","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"while — as long as it is true","md":"`while` repeats a block as long as the condition is true.\n\n```\nlet mut fuel = 3;\nwhile fuel > 0 {\n    println!(\"Flying! Fuel: {fuel}\");\n    fuel -= 1;\n}\n```"},{"t":"text","title":"for and ranges","md":"`for` goes through values one by one. Most often these are numbers from a **range**:\n\n- `1..5` means 1 to 4. The right end is **not included**.\n- `1..=5` means 1 to 5. The `=` sign includes the right end."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    for i in 1..=3 {\n        println!(\"Push-up number {i}\");\n    }\n    for i in (1..=3).rev() {\n        println!(\"{i}...\");\n    }\n    println!(\"Go!\");\n}","out":"Push-up number 1\nPush-up number 2\nPush-up number 3\n3...\n2...\n1...\nGo!","after":"`.rev()` reverses the range: we count backwards."},{"t":"quiz","md":"How many times does `for i in 0..10` run?","options":["11","10","9"],"answer":1,"why":["The right end `10` is not included in `0..10`.","Correct: the numbers from 0 to 9.","Zero also counts. From 0 to 9 there are ten numbers."]},{"t":"task","md":"Calculate the sum of the numbers from 1 to 100 inclusive and print it.","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut sum = 0;\n    // the loop goes here\n    println!(\"{sum}\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let mut sum = 0;\n    for i in 1..=100 {\n        sum += i;\n    }\n    println!(\"{sum}\");\n}","expect":"5050","hints":["You need `for i in 1..=100`.","Inside the loop: `sum += i;`"]},{"t":"text","title":"A step bigger than one","md":"The method `.step_by(n)` goes through the numbers in steps of `n`:\n\n```\nfor i in (0..=10).step_by(5) {\n    println!(\"{i}\"); // 0, 5, 10\n}\n```\n`for` can go through more than numbers. For example, the letters of a string or the items of a list. This comes in the next modules."},{"t":"recap","items":["`while condition { }` runs as long as the condition is true.","`for x in 1..=n { }` goes through values.","`a..b` excludes the right end, `a..=b` includes it."]}],"goals":["The `while` loop with a condition","Going through numbers with `for` and ranges"]},{"id":"m03-4","title":"Practice: FizzBuzz","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"The FizzBuzz task","md":"This is a famous job interview task. The rules for the numbers from 1 to 15:\n\n- divisible by 3 and by 5 — print `FizzBuzz`;\n- divisible only by 3 — `Fizz`;\n- divisible only by 5 — `Buzz`;\n- otherwise — the number itself.\n\n> Hint: put the check \"by 3 and by 5\" **first**. Otherwise it is never reached."},{"t":"fill","explain":"A number is divisible by both 3 and 5 only if it is divisible by 15.","md":"Fill in the conditions.","code":"fn main() {\n    for n in 1..=15 {\n        if n % 15 == 0 {\n            println!(\"FizzBuzz\");\n        } else if n % ___ == 0 {\n            println!(\"Fizz\");\n        } else if n % ___ == 0 {\n            println!(\"Buzz\");\n        } else {\n            println!(\"{n}\");\n        }\n    }\n}","answers":[["3"],["5"]]},{"t":"task","md":"Now on your own. Write FizzBuzz for the numbers from 1 to 15.","code":"fn main() {\n    for n in 1..=15 {\n        // your code\n    }\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    for n in 1..=15 {\n        if n % 15 == 0 {\n            println!(\"FizzBuzz\");\n        } else if n % 3 == 0 {\n            println!(\"Fizz\");\n        } else if n % 5 == 0 {\n            println!(\"Buzz\");\n        } else {\n            println!(\"{n}\");\n        }\n    }\n}","expect":"1\n2\nFizz\n4\nBuzz\nFizz\n7\n8\nFizz\nBuzz\n11\nFizz\n13\n14\nFizzBuzz","hints":["First check `n % 15 == 0`.","Then `else if n % 3 == 0`, then `else if n % 5 == 0`, and finally `else`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["The order of conditions matters: the narrowest one goes first.","`%` helps check divisibility."]}],"goals":["Combine `for`, `if` and `%`","Solve a classic task"]},{"id":"m03-p","title":"Practice: conditions and loops","min":12,"cards":[{"t":"task","outputCheck":"exact","md":"Print a row of the multiplication table for 7: from `7 × 1 = 7` to `7 × 5 = 35`, each on its own line.","code":"fn main() {\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    for i in 1..=5 {\n        println!(\"7 × {i} = {}\", 7 * i);\n    }\n}","expect":"7 × 1 = 7\n7 × 2 = 14\n7 × 3 = 21\n7 × 4 = 28\n7 × 5 = 35","hints":["The loop `for i in 1..=5`.","`println!(\"7 × {i} = {}\", 7 * i);`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Calculate the sum of only the even numbers from 1 to 20.","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut sum = 0;\n    println!(\"{sum}\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let mut sum = 0;\n    for i in 1..=20 {\n        if i % 2 == 0 {\n            sum += i;\n        }\n    }\n    println!(\"{sum}\");\n}","expect":"110","hints":["Inside the loop add `i` only if `i % 2 == 0`.","Or like this: `for i in (2..=20).step_by(2) { sum += i; }`"]},{"t":"task","md":"With `while`, count down from 5 to 1, and at the end print `Launch!`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut n = 5;\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let mut n = 5;\n    while n > 0 {\n        println!(\"{n}\");\n        n -= 1;\n    }\n    println!(\"Launch!\");\n}","expect":"5\n4\n3\n2\n1\nLaunch!","hints":["The loop condition: `while n > 0`.","Do not forget to decrease `n` inside the loop: `n -= 1;`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Using `loop` and `break` with a value, find the first number greater than 50 that is divisible by both 7 and 4. Print it.","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut n = 51;\n    let found = 0; // replace with a loop\n    println!(\"{found}\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let mut n = 51;\n    let found = loop {\n        if n % 7 == 0 && n % 4 == 0 {\n            break n;\n        }\n        n += 1;\n    };\n    println!(\"{found}\");\n}","expect":"56","hints":["`let found = loop { ... };` — a `;` is needed after `}`.","Inside: if it fits — `break n;`, otherwise `n += 1;`"]},{"t":"task","outputCheck":"exact","md":"Print a triangle of stars with a height of 4. You need two loops: the outer one for rows, the inner one for stars.","code":"fn main() {\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    for row in 1..=4 {\n        for _ in 0..row {\n            print!(\"*\");\n        }\n        println!();\n    }\n}","expect":"*\n**\n***\n****","hints":["The outer loop is `for row in 1..=4`. Inside it, print `*` with `print!` `row` times.","After the inner loop — `println!();` to move to a new line."]},{"t":"recap","items":["A loop with an `if` inside is a common pattern.","`loop` with `break value` is handy for searching.","Nested loops: the outer one goes over rows, the inner one over the items of a row."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Combine loops and conditions","Write nested loops"]}]},{"id":"m04","title":"Tuples, arrays, vectors","sub":"How to store many values at once","lessons":[{"id":"m04-1","title":"Tuples","min":5,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A tuple is a set of different things","md":"A **tuple** is several values in one pair of parentheses. The types can be different.\n\n```\nlet person = (\"Anna\", 25, true);\n```\n> Analogy: a backpack holds a book, a bottle and keys. Different things, but together."},{"t":"code","md":"To get an element, write a dot and a number. Numbering starts from **zero**.","code":"fn main() {\n    let person = (\"Anna\", 25, true);\n    println!(\"Name: {}\", person.0);\n    println!(\"Age: {}\", person.1);\n\n    let (name, age, student) = person;\n    println!(\"{name}, {age}, student: {student}\");\n}","out":"Name: Anna\nAge: 25\nAnna, 25, student: true","after":"The second part is **unpacking**: three variables at once from one tuple."},{"t":"text","title":"A function can return a tuple","md":"This is a handy way to return two results at once:\n\n```\nfn min_max(a: i32, b: i32) -> (i32, i32) {\n    if a < b { (a, b) } else { (b, a) }\n}\n```"},{"t":"quiz","md":"How do you get `true` out of `let t = (10, \"yes\", true);`?","options":["`t.3`","`t.2`","`t[2]`"],"answer":1,"why":["Numbering starts from zero: 0, 1, 2.","Correct. The third element has the number 2.","Square brackets are for arrays. A tuple uses a dot."]},{"t":"deep","title":"The empty tuple","md":"Remember the value `()` from the module about functions? It is an **empty tuple** — a tuple with zero elements.\n\nSo a function \"without a result\" really returns an empty tuple. The type and the value are written the same way: `()`."},{"t":"recap","items":["`(a, b, c)` is a tuple; the types can differ.","`t.0`, `t.1` give access by number, starting from zero.","`let (x, y) = t;` unpacks."]}],"goals":["Keep several different values together","Get them by number and by unpacking"]},{"id":"m04-2","title":"Arrays","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"An array is a row of identical cells","md":"An **array** is a row of values of **one** type. The length is fixed: it cannot change.\n\n```\nlet days = [\"mon\", \"tue\", \"wed\", \"thu\", \"fri\"];\nlet zeros = [0; 10]; // ten zeros\n```\n> Analogy: an egg carton has ten identical cells, no more and no fewer."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let scores = [7, 9, 4, 10];\n    println!(\"First score: {}\", scores[0]);\n    println!(\"Number of scores: {}\", scores.len());\n    for s in scores {\n        println!(\"Score {s}\");\n    }\n}","out":"First score: 7\nNumber of scores: 4\nScore 7\nScore 9\nScore 4\nScore 10"},{"t":"code","md":"What happens if you ask for a cell that does not exist? Run it.","code":"fn main() {\n    let a = [1, 2, 3];\n    for i in 0..=3 {\n        println!(\"{}\", a[i]);\n    }\n}","after":"Three numbers are printed, and at `a[3]` the program **panics**: `index out of bounds`. Rust does not let a program read someone else's memory. This is protection, not breakage."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What is the index of the last element of an array with 5 elements?","options":["5","4","-1"],"answer":1,"why":["Indexes start from zero, so the last one is one less than the length.","Correct: 0, 1, 2, 3, 4.","Rust has no negative indexes."]},{"t":"task","outputCheck":"number","md":"Find the highest score in the array and print it.","code":"fn main() {\n    let scores = [7, 9, 4, 10, 6];\n    let mut best = scores[0];\n    // go through scores with a loop and update best\n    println!(\"Best: {best}\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let scores = [7, 9, 4, 10, 6];\n    let mut best = scores[0];\n    for s in scores {\n        if s > best {\n            best = s;\n        }\n    }\n    println!(\"Best: {best}\");\n}","expect":"Best: 10","hints":["`for s in scores { ... }`","Inside: if `s > best`, then `best = s;`"]},{"t":"text","title":"The type of an array","md":"The type of an array is written like this: `[type; length]`.\n\n```\nlet days: [&str; 7] = [\"mon\", \"tue\", \"wed\", \"thu\", \"fri\", \"sat\", \"sun\"];\n```\nThe length is part of the type. `[i32; 3]` and `[i32; 4]` are **different** types."},{"t":"deep","title":"A table: an array of arrays","md":"An array can hold other arrays. This makes a table:\n\nThe type of `grid` is `[[i32; 3]; 2]`: two rows of three numbers.","code":"fn main() {\n    let grid = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]];\n    println!(\"Row 1, column 2: {}\", grid[1][2]);\n    for row in grid {\n        for cell in row {\n            print!(\"{cell} \");\n        }\n        println!();\n    }\n}","out":"Row 1, column 2: 6\n1 2 3\n4 5 6"},{"t":"recap","items":["`[a, b, c]` is an array of one type; the length is fixed.","`a[0]` is the first element, `a.len()` is the length.","Going out of bounds is a panic, not a silent error."]}],"goals":["Store many values of one type","Go through an array with a loop"]},{"id":"m04-3","title":"A first look at Vec","min":5,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Vec is a growing list","md":"An array cannot grow. But often you need to add elements as you go. For that there is the **vector** `Vec`.\n\n```\nlet mut list = Vec::new();\nlist.push(\"bread\");\nlist.push(\"milk\");\n```\nThere is also a short form: `let v = vec![1, 2, 3];`"},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut todo = vec![\"wake up\", \"wash\"];\n    todo.push(\"learn Rust\");\n    println!(\"Tasks: {}\", todo.len());\n    for task in &todo {\n        println!(\"- {task}\");\n    }\n}","out":"Tasks: 3\n- wake up\n- wash\n- learn Rust","after":"The `&` before `todo` means \"just look\". Why it is needed, you will learn in the module about ownership. It is the most important topic in Rust."},{"t":"quiz","md":"How is `Vec` different from an array?","options":["A Vec can grow and shrink","A Vec can hold values of different types","A Vec is numbered from one"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct. Analogy: an array is a fixed box, a vector is a rubber one.","No, a vector also holds values of one type.","It is also numbered from zero, like an array."]},{"t":"deep","title":"Length and capacity of a Vec","md":"A vector has two numbers:\n- `len()` — how many elements it holds right now;\n- `capacity()` — how many elements it already has space for.\n\nWhen there is not enough space, the vector takes a new piece of memory, usually twice as big, and moves the elements there. This happens rarely, so `push` is fast on average.\n\nIf you know in advance how many elements there will be, you can ask for the space right away: `Vec::with_capacity(100)`."},{"t":"recap","items":["`Vec::new()` or `vec![...]` creates a vector.","`push` adds to the end, `len` gives the length.","A vector must be `mut` to add to it."]}],"goals":["A list that can grow","Add elements with `push`"]},{"id":"m04-p","title":"Practice: tuples, arrays, vectors","min":10,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Calculate the sum of all the numbers in the array.","code":"fn main() {\n    let nums = [4, 8, 15, 16, 23, 42];\n    let mut sum = 0;\n    println!(\"{sum}\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let nums = [4, 8, 15, 16, 23, 42];\n    let mut sum = 0;\n    for n in nums {\n        sum += n;\n    }\n    println!(\"{sum}\");\n}","expect":"108","hints":["`for n in nums { ... }`","Inside: `sum += n;`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Count how many negative numbers the array has.","code":"fn main() {\n    let temps = [3, -2, 0, -7, 5, -1];\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let temps = [3, -2, 0, -7, 5, -1];\n    let mut count = 0;\n    for t in temps {\n        if t < 0 {\n            count += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    println!(\"{count}\");\n}","expect":"3","hints":["You need a counter: `let mut count = 0;`.","In the loop: if `t < 0`, then `count += 1;`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Write a function `swap` that takes a tuple `(i32, i32)` and returns it with the elements in reverse order.","code":"// write the function swap\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = swap((1, 2));\n    println!(\"{} {}\", p.0, p.1);\n}","solution":"fn swap(pair: (i32, i32)) -> (i32, i32) {\n    (pair.1, pair.0)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = swap((1, 2));\n    println!(\"{} {}\", p.0, p.1);\n}","expect":"2 1","hints":["The header: `fn swap(pair: (i32, i32)) -> (i32, i32)`","The body: `(pair.1, pair.0)`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Find the position (index) of the number 23 in the array. Go through the indexes from 0 to the length of the array.","code":"fn main() {\n    let nums = [4, 8, 15, 16, 23, 42];\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let nums = [4, 8, 15, 16, 23, 42];\n    for i in 0..nums.len() {\n        if nums[i] == 23 {\n            println!(\"{i}\");\n        }\n    }\n}","expect":"4","hints":["`for i in 0..nums.len()` goes through the indexes.","Inside: `if nums[i] == 23 { println!(\"{i}\"); }`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Build a vector of the squares of the numbers from 1 to 5 and print it with `{:?}`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut squares = Vec::new();\n    println!(\"{:?}\", squares);\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let mut squares = Vec::new();\n    for i in 1..=5 {\n        squares.push(i * i);\n    }\n    println!(\"{:?}\", squares);\n}","expect":"[1, 4, 9, 16, 25]","hints":["The loop `for i in 1..=5`.","Inside: `squares.push(i * i);`"]},{"t":"recap","items":["`for x in array` goes through the values, `for i in 0..a.len()` goes through the indexes.","`Vec::new()` + `push` collects a list as you go.","`{:?}` prints whole arrays and vectors."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Go through arrays with loops","Collect data into a Vec"]}]},{"id":"m05","title":"Ownership","sub":"The main idea of Rust. Slowly, step by step","lessons":[{"id":"m05-1","title":"Where data lives: the stack and the heap","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Why this module exists","md":"This is **the most important** topic in Rust. It is what makes Rust fast and reliable.\n\nIt is also the topic that feels the strangest. That is why the lessons here are especially small. Do not rush. If something does not make sense the first time, that is normal."},{"t":"text","title":"Program memory","md":"While a program runs, its data lives in **memory**. Memory has two places:\n\n- the **stack** — a fast shelf for things of known size;\n- the **heap** — a big storage room for things whose size can change."},{"t":"text","title":"The stack is a pile of plates","md":"Numbers, `bool`, `char` always have the same size. They live on the **stack**.\n\n> Analogy: a pile of plates. You put a plate on top and take one from the top. Very fast and simple.\n\nWhen a function ends, its plates are removed from the pile automatically."},{"t":"text","title":"The heap is a storage room","md":"Text that can grow is stored in the **heap**. For example, the type `String`.\n\n> Analogy: a storage room. The storekeeper finds free space, puts the item there and gives you a **ticket**. The ticket lives on the stack, and the item itself is in the storage room.\n\nThe main question: **who frees the space in the storage room, and when?** Ownership answers this question."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Where is a `String` that can grow stored?","options":["Only on the stack","In the heap, with a pointer to it on the stack","Nowhere, it is computed on the fly"],"answer":1,"why":["Only the \"ticket\" is on the stack: the address, the length, the capacity.","Correct. Like an item in storage and a ticket in your pocket.","Data is always stored somewhere in memory."]},{"t":"text","title":"How other languages clean the storage room","md":"- In C the programmer frees memory **by hand**. Forget it — memory leaks. Free it twice — the program crashes.\n- In Python, Java, C# a **garbage collector** works. It walks around and cleans up by itself. Convenient, but it takes time.\n- In Rust the cleanup is planned **in advance**, while compiling. This is **ownership**."},{"t":"deep","title":"What a String is made of","md":"A variable of type `String` itself is small. It lives on the stack and holds three numbers:\n1. a **pointer** — the address of the text in the heap;\n2. the **length** — how many bytes of text there are now;\n3. the **capacity** — how many bytes are reserved in the heap.\n\n24 bytes are three numbers of 8 bytes each. The text itself lives separately, in the heap.","code":"fn main() {\n    let s = String::from(\"hello\");\n    println!(\"Bytes of text: {}\", s.len());\n    println!(\"Size of the variable itself: {} bytes\", std::mem::size_of::<String>());\n}","out":"Bytes of text: 5\nSize of the variable itself: 24 bytes"},{"t":"recap","items":["The stack is a fast pile for data of known size.","The heap is storage for data that can grow.","Ownership decides who frees heap space and when."]}],"goals":["What the stack and the heap are","Why this matters at all"]},{"id":"m05-2","title":"The three rules of ownership","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"The three rules","md":"The whole ownership system is just three rules:\n\n1. Every value has an **owner** — a variable.\n2. There is **only one** owner at a time.\n3. When the owner goes out of scope, the value is **deleted**.\n\n> Analogy: a book has one owner. If the owner leaves forever, the book goes to recycling."},{"t":"code","md":"Rule 3 in action. Let us create a `String` inside a block.","code":"fn main() {\n    {\n        let s = String::from(\"hello\");\n        println!(\"{s}\");\n    } // here s goes out of scope — the memory is freed\n    println!(\"the block has ended\");\n}","out":"hello\nthe block has ended","after":"`String::from(\"...\")` creates a string in the heap. At `}` Rust does the cleanup by itself. You do not need to write anything."},{"t":"quiz","md":"When is the memory of the string `s` in the example above freed?","options":["At the end of `main`","At the closing `}` of the block where `s` was created","Never, while the program runs"],"answer":1,"why":["Earlier: `s` lives only inside its block.","Correct. The owner is gone — the value is deleted.","In Rust memory is freed as soon as the owner is gone."]},{"t":"fill","explain":"This is all that ownership is built on.","md":"Remember the rules. Fill in each blank with one word.","code":"// 1. Every value has an ___.\n// 2. There is only ___ owner at a time.\n// 3. When the owner is gone, the value is ___.","answers":[["owner"],["one","1"],["deleted","dropped","freed","removed"]]},{"t":"deep","title":"How to see the moment of deletion","md":"In Rust you can ask a type to report when it is deleted. For that there is the trait `Drop`. Traits come in module 12; here just look at the result.\n\nYou can see rule 3: a value is deleted exactly when its owner leaves its block.","code":"struct Noisy(&'static str);\n\nimpl Drop for Noisy {\n    fn drop(&mut self) {\n        println!(\"Deleting {}\", self.0);\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let _a = Noisy(\"first\");\n    {\n        let _b = Noisy(\"second\");\n        println!(\"End of the inner block\");\n    }\n    println!(\"End of main\");\n}","out":"End of the inner block\nDeleting second\nEnd of main\nDeleting first"},{"t":"recap","items":["A value has one owner.","When the owner leaves the block, the value is deleted.","The compiler writes the cleanup, not you."]}],"goals":["Learn the three rules of ownership","Understand when a value is deleted"]},{"id":"m05-3","title":"Move","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"You gave the book away — you no longer have it","md":"What happens if you write `let b = a;` where `a` is a `String`?\n\nRule 2: there is one owner. So the book **moves** to `b`. And `a` no longer has it.\n\nThis is called a **move**."},{"t":"code","fails":true,"md":"Run it. What does the compiler say?","code":"fn main() {\n    let a = String::from(\"book\");\n    let b = a;\n    println!(\"{a}\");\n}","after":"The error is `E0382: borrow of moved value: a`. The value moved into `b`. The variable `a` is now empty."},{"t":"text","title":"Why it works this way","md":"If both `a` and `b` owned the string, it would be deleted **twice** at `}`. In C this is a classic crash.\n\nRust simply forbids it. The error is caught while compiling, not on the user's computer."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does the program print?\n```\nfn main() {\n    let a = String::from(\"cat\");\n    let b = a;\n    println!(\"{b}\");\n}\n```","options":["cat","A compile error","An empty string"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct. Now the owner is `b`, and you can print it.","The error would happen when using `a`. `b` is the rightful owner.","The data did not go anywhere; only the owner changed."]},{"t":"text","title":"A function also takes ownership","md":"Passing a value into a function is also a move. The function becomes the owner.\n\n```\nfn eat(food: String) {\n    println!(\"Ate {food}\");\n} // here food is deleted\n\nfn main() {\n    let apple = String::from(\"apple\");\n    eat(apple);\n    // apple can no longer be used\n}\n```"},{"t":"quiz","md":"After `eat(apple);` can you print `apple`?","options":["Yes, the function only looked at it","No, the value moved into the function"],"answer":1,"why":["Without `&` a function does not look, it takes.","Correct. And it was deleted at the end of `eat`."]},{"t":"deep","title":"What is really copied in a move","md":"With `let b = a;` for a `String`, only the three numbers on the stack are copied: the pointer, the length, the capacity. The text in the heap stays where it is.\n\nSo `a` and `b` point to the same text. To avoid deleting the text twice, Rust declares `a` invalid. That is why a move is fast: however long the text is, only 24 bytes are copied."},{"t":"recap","items":["`let b = a;` with a `String` moves the value.","After a move the old variable cannot be used: error E0382.","Passing into a function is also a move."]}],"goals":["What happens with `let b = a;` for a string","Why the old variable stops working"]},{"id":"m05-4","title":"clone and Copy","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"clone — a photocopy","md":"Do you need the data in both `a` and `b`? Make a **copy**: `.clone()`.\n\n```\nlet a = String::from(\"text\");\nlet b = a.clone();\nprintln!(\"{a} and {b}\"); // works\n```\n> Analogy: a photocopy of a book. Now everyone has their own. But copying a big book takes time, so do it on purpose."},{"t":"code","md":"With numbers everything works even without `clone`. Why?","code":"fn main() {\n    let x = 5;\n    let y = x;\n    println!(\"x = {x}, y = {y}\");\n}","out":"x = 5, y = 5"},{"t":"text","title":"The Copy type","md":"Small simple values live on the stack. Copying them is cheap. So Rust copies them **automatically**.\n\nSuch types are called **Copy**: all numbers, `bool`, `char` and tuples made of them.\n\n> Analogy: a number is like a note with a digit. It is easy to copy onto another piece of paper. A `String` is a whole book."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Which of these types is NOT copied automatically with `let b = a;`?","options":["`i32`","`bool`","`String`","`char`"],"answer":2,"why":["Whole numbers are Copy.","`bool` is Copy.","Correct. A `String` lives in the heap, so it moves.","`char` is Copy."]},{"t":"task","md":"Fix the program: add a copy so that both strings are printed.","code":"fn main() {\n    let original = String::from(\"Rust\");\n    let copy = original;\n    println!(\"{original} and {copy}\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let original = String::from(\"Rust\");\n    let copy = original.clone();\n    println!(\"{original} and {copy}\");\n}","expect":"Rust and Rust","hints":["Right now the value moves into `copy`. You need a copy.","`let copy = original.clone();`"]},{"t":"deep","title":"Why String cannot be Copy","md":"A type can be `Copy` only if it can be copied **byte by byte** and both copies are independent.\n\nFor a `String`, a byte copy would give two pointers to the same text in the heap. Two variables would own the same text, and that breaks rule 2. So a `String` needs `.clone()`: it reserves **new** memory and copies the text there.\n\nThe rule: if a type owns something in the heap, it is not `Copy`."},{"t":"recap","items":["`.clone()` makes a full copy. It works, but it costs time.","Numbers, `bool`, `char` are Copy types; they are copied automatically.","`String` and `Vec` move."]}],"goals":["How to make a copy with `.clone()`","Why numbers are copied automatically"]},{"id":"m05-5","title":"Giving ownership back","min":5,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A function can give it back","md":"A function took a value. But it can also **return** it:\n\n```\nfn add_world(mut s: String) -> String {\n    s.push_str(\", world\");\n    s\n}\n```\n`push_str` adds text to the end of a string."},{"t":"code","code":"fn add_world(mut s: String) -> String {\n    s.push_str(\", world\");\n    s\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let greeting = String::from(\"Hello\");\n    let greeting = add_world(greeting);\n    println!(\"{greeting}\");\n}","out":"Hello, world"},{"t":"text","title":"Inconvenient, right?","md":"Giving a value away and taking it back every time is tiring. It is like giving a friend a book only so that they can tell you how many pages it has.\n\nWe want to let someone **look** without giving the value away. Rust has this. It is called **borrowing** — the topic of the next module."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why does `add_world` return `s`?","options":["To give ownership of the string back to `main`","To print it","Because the syntax of every function requires it"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct. Otherwise the string would be deleted at the end of the function.","`main` prints, not the function.","Functions do not have to return anything."]},{"t":"recap","items":["A function can give ownership back through its result.","It works, but it is inconvenient.","The convenient way is borrowing (`&`). It comes next."]}],"goals":["How a function can give ownership back","Why this is inconvenient and what comes next"]},{"id":"m05-p","title":"Practice: ownership","min":12,"cards":[{"t":"quiz","md":"Which program compiles?","options":["`let a = String::from(\"x\"); let b = a; println!(\"{a}\");`","`let a = 5; let b = a; println!(\"{a}\");`","`let a = vec![1]; let b = a; println!(\"{:?}\", a);`"],"answer":1,"why":["After `let b = a;` the string moved into `b`. You cannot use `a`.","Correct. A number is a Copy type; it was copied.","A `Vec` also moves, like a `String`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Way 1: a copy. Fix the program with `.clone()`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let name = String::from(\"Rust\");\n    let other = name;\n    println!(\"{name} {other}\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let name = String::from(\"Rust\");\n    let other = name.clone();\n    println!(\"{name} {other}\");\n}","expect":"Rust Rust","hints":["`let other = name.clone();`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Way 2: the order. Fix the program just by moving lines around. Do not use `clone`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let text = String::from(\"book\");\n    let moved = text;\n    println!(\"Before: {text}\");\n    println!(\"After: {moved}\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let text = String::from(\"book\");\n    println!(\"Before: {text}\");\n    let moved = text;\n    println!(\"After: {moved}\");\n}","expect":"Before: book\nAfter: book","hints":["Use `text` **before** it moves into `moved`.","Put `println!(\"Before: {text}\");` before `let moved = text;`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Way 3: give ownership back. The function `add_exclaim` takes the string. Make it return the string with `!` at the end.","code":"fn add_exclaim(mut s: String) {\n    s.push('!');\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let word = String::from(\"Hooray\");\n    add_exclaim(word);\n    println!(\"{word}\");\n}","solution":"fn add_exclaim(mut s: String) -> String {\n    s.push('!');\n    s\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let word = String::from(\"Hooray\");\n    let word = add_exclaim(word);\n    println!(\"{word}\");\n}","expect":"Hooray!","hints":["The function needs `-> String` and `s` as the last line.","In `main`: `let word = add_exclaim(word);`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Here the string moves inside a loop. Fix it so the program prints `Rust` three times. `clone` works.","code":"fn print_it(s: String) {\n    println!(\"{s}\");\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let s = String::from(\"Rust\");\n    for _ in 0..3 {\n        print_it(s);\n    }\n}","solution":"fn print_it(s: String) {\n    println!(\"{s}\");\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let s = String::from(\"Rust\");\n    for _ in 0..3 {\n        print_it(s.clone());\n    }\n}","expect":"Rust\nRust\nRust","hints":["On the first step of the loop `s` moves into the function. On the second step it is gone.","Pass a copy: `print_it(s.clone());`. The next module shows a better way — a reference."]},{"t":"recap","items":["E0382 is fixed in three ways: `clone`, a different order of lines, giving ownership back.","In a loop a value moves on the very first step.","The most convenient way is references. They are in the next module."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Fix error E0382 in different ways","Predict where a value will move"]}]},{"id":"m06","title":"Borrowing and slices","sub":"Let someone look without giving it away","lessons":[{"id":"m06-1","title":"References: let someone look","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A reference means \"look, but do not take\"","md":"A **reference** gives access to a value without ownership. It is written with `&`.\n\n```\nlet book = String::from(\"War and Peace\");\nlet r = &book; // r looks at book\n```\nCreating a reference is called **borrowing**.\n\n> Analogy: you lend a friend a book to read. The book is still yours. The friend gives it back after reading."},{"t":"code","code":"fn count_letters(s: &String) -> usize {\n    s.chars().count()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let word = String::from(\"hello\");\n    let n = count_letters(&word);\n    println!(\"The word \\\"{word}\\\" has {n} letters\");\n}","out":"The word \"hello\" has 5 letters","after":"`word` is still ours: the function only looked. `usize` is the type for sizes and counts."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `&word` mean in a function call?","options":["Give `word` to the function forever","Let the function look at `word`","Make a copy of `word`"],"answer":1,"why":["That would be without `&`. With `&` ownership stays.","Correct. The function gets a reference; the owner does not change.","`.clone()` makes a copy. A reference copies nothing."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function takes the string, so it cannot be printed afterwards. Fix it with a reference.","code":"fn shout(s: String) {\n    println!(\"{}!!!\", s);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let msg = String::from(\"Hooray\");\n    shout(msg);\n    println!(\"{msg}\");\n}","solution":"fn shout(s: &String) {\n    println!(\"{}!!!\", s);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let msg = String::from(\"Hooray\");\n    shout(&msg);\n    println!(\"{msg}\");\n}","expect":"Hooray!!!\nHooray","hints":["You need to change two places: the parameter type and the call.","`fn shout(s: &String)` and `shout(&msg);`"]},{"t":"deep","title":"What is inside a reference","md":"A reference is just the **address** of a value in memory. It takes 8 bytes (on a 64-bit computer), however big the value itself is.\n\nYou can print the address with `{:p}` (p means pointer):\n\nThe address is different on every run. The size of the reference is always `8 bytes`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let big = String::from(\"a very long text\");\n    let r = &big;\n    println!(\"Address: {:p}\", r);\n    println!(\"Size of the reference: {} bytes\", std::mem::size_of::<&String>());\n}"},{"t":"recap","items":["`&x` is a reference: access without ownership.","A function with a parameter `&T` only looks.","After such a call the variable is still yours."]}],"goals":["What a reference `&` is","How to pass a value into a function without giving it away"]},{"id":"m06-2","title":"Mutable references","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"&mut — permission to write notes in the margins","md":"A normal reference `&` can only read. For a function to **change** a value, you need a mutable reference `&mut`.\n\n```\nfn add_smile(s: &mut String) {\n    s.push_str(\" :)\");\n}\n```\nThe variable itself must also be `mut`."},{"t":"code","code":"fn add_smile(s: &mut String) {\n    s.push_str(\" :)\");\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut text = String::from(\"Hello\");\n    add_smile(&mut text);\n    add_smile(&mut text);\n    println!(\"{text}\");\n}","out":"Hello :) :)"},{"t":"text","title":"The main rule","md":"At any moment you can have **one** of these two:\n\n- any number of **readers** `&`;\n- or exactly **one writer** `&mut`.\n\n> Analogy: a document in a shared folder. Everyone can read it at the same time. But while someone edits it, the others wait, so they do not see a mess.\n\nThis rule prevents a whole class of bugs where data changes under your feet."},{"t":"code","fails":true,"md":"Two mutable references at the same time. Run it.","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut s = String::from(\"text\");\n    let a = &mut s;\n    let b = &mut s;\n    a.push_str(\"1\");\n    b.push_str(\"2\");\n}","after":"Error `E0499`: you cannot take `&mut` twice while the first reference is still in use."},{"t":"text","title":"A reference \"lives\" until its last use","md":"Good news: a reference ends after the **last** place where it is used. Not at `}`.\n\n```\nlet mut s = String::from(\"a\");\nlet r1 = &s;\nprintln!(\"{r1}\"); // r1 is not needed anymore\nlet r2 = &mut s;  // now this is allowed\nr2.push_str(\"b\");\n```"},{"t":"quiz","md":"Which set of references is allowed at the same time?","options":["Three `&` references","Two `&mut` references","One `&` and one `&mut`"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct. There can be any number of readers.","There can be only one writer.","While someone reads, nobody may write."]},{"t":"deep","title":"Which bug the rule prevents","md":"Imagine: you hold a reference to the first element of a vector, and at the same time you add a new element to the vector.\n\n```\nlet mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];\nlet first = &v[0];\nv.push(4);            // error E0502\nprintln!(\"{first}\");\n```\nOn `push` the vector may run out of space. Then it moves to new memory, and the old memory is freed. The reference `first` would point to freed memory.\n\nIn C++ such a bug compiles and then breaks the program at a random moment. Rust does not allow it: while the reader `first` exists, `v` cannot be changed."},{"t":"recap","items":["`&mut x` is a reference through which you can change the value.","Either many `&`, or one `&mut`.","A reference lives until its last use."]}],"goals":["How to let something change a value: `&mut`","The main rule of references"]},{"id":"m06-3","title":"There are no dangling references","min":5,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A reference to nowhere","md":"A **dangling reference** points to a value that no longer exists. Analogy: the address of a house that was demolished.\n\nIn C such references are a common cause of crashes and security holes. In Rust they **do not exist**: the compiler watches."},{"t":"code","fails":true,"md":"The function tries to return a reference to its local string. Run it.","code":"fn make() -> &String {\n    let s = String::from(\"hello\");\n    &s\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let r = make();\n}","after":"The compiler does not allow it. The string `s` is deleted at the `}` of the function, and the reference would point to nothing. The error talks about a **lifetime**. There is a separate module about lifetimes."},{"t":"text","title":"How to fix it","md":"Return the string itself, not a reference. Then ownership simply moves out:\n\n```\nfn make() -> String {\n    String::from(\"hello\")\n}\n```"},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why can you not return `&s` when `s` was created inside the function?","options":["References can never be returned","`s` is deleted at the end of the function, and the reference would dangle","Strings cannot be created in functions"],"answer":1,"why":["They can, if the value lives longer than the function.","Correct. A reference cannot outlive its value.","They can; you just need to return the string itself."]},{"t":"recap","items":["A reference cannot outlive its value.","If you created something inside a function, return the value, not a reference."]}],"goals":["What a dangling reference is","How Rust prevents it"]},{"id":"m06-4","title":"Slices and &str","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A slice is a window into part of the data","md":"A **slice** is a reference to a piece of a sequence. Not a copy, but a window.\n\n```\nlet nums = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50];\nlet middle = &nums[1..4]; // [20, 30, 40]\n```\n> Analogy: a frame laid on top of a photo. You see only part of it, but it is the same photo."},{"t":"text","title":"String and &str","md":"- `String` is an **owning** string in the heap. It can grow.\n- `&str` is a **string slice**: a window into someone else's text.\n\nText in quotes, like `\"hello\"`, is a `&str`. It is stored right inside the program.\n\n> The rule: write `&str` in function parameters. Both `&String` and `\"text\"` fit there."},{"t":"code","code":"fn greet(name: &str) {\n    println!(\"Hello, {name}!\");\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let owned = String::from(\"Anna\");\n    greet(&owned);\n    greet(\"Bob\");\n    let name = String::from(\"Яна\");\n    let part = &name[0..2];\n    println!(\"First bytes: {part}\");\n}","out":"Hello, Anna!\nHello, Bob!\nFirst bytes: Я","after":"Wait. The Russian name \"Яна\" and `0..2` gave **one** letter \"Я\", not two. Why?"},{"t":"text","title":"Some letters take 2 or more bytes","md":"Strings in Rust are stored in **UTF-8**. An English letter takes 1 byte. A Russian letter takes 2 bytes. Some other characters take 3 or 4.\n\nThe slice `[0..2]` takes **bytes**, not letters. If you cut a letter in half, the program panics.\n\n!> For text that is not pure English, do not cut a string by numbers. Use `.chars()` — going through the string letter by letter."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let word = \"привет\";\n    println!(\"Bytes: {}\", word.len());\n    println!(\"Letters: {}\", word.chars().count());\n    let first: String = word.chars().take(3).collect();\n    println!(\"The first three letters: {first}\");\n}","out":"Bytes: 12\nLetters: 6\nThe first three letters: при","after":"\"привет\" means \"hello\" in Russian."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `\"мир\".len()` return? (\"мир\" is a Russian word with 3 letters.)","options":["3","6","1"],"answer":1,"why":["`len()` counts bytes. A Russian letter has two.","Correct. Three Russian letters × 2 bytes.","That is the number of words, and `len()` is about something else."]},{"t":"task","md":"Write a function `first_word` that returns the first word of a string (up to the space). If there is no space, return the whole string.","code":"fn first_word(s: &str) -> &str {\n    // hint: s.split(' ').next()\n    s\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", first_word(\"hello world\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", first_word(\"rust\"));\n}","solution":"fn first_word(s: &str) -> &str {\n    match s.find(' ') {\n        Some(i) => &s[..i],\n        None => s,\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", first_word(\"hello world\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", first_word(\"rust\"));\n}","expect":"hello\nrust","hints":["`s.split(' ')` cuts the string at spaces. `.next()` gives the first piece.","A short solution: `s.split(' ').next().unwrap()`. `unwrap` is explained later."]},{"t":"deep","title":"Why &String fits where &str is expected","md":"The function `greet(name: &str)` also accepts `&String`. How, if the types are different?\n\nRust turns `&String` into `&str` by itself. This is called **deref coercion**. A `String` can give out a slice of all its text.\n\nThe same works with vectors: `&Vec<i32>` turns into the slice `&[i32]`. That is why parameters are written as `&[i32]` — then the function accepts both an array and a vector."},{"t":"recap","items":["A slice `&a[1..3]` is a window into part of the data.","`String` owns text, `&str` looks at text.","Write `&str` in function parameters.","`len()` counts bytes. For letters use `.chars()`."]}],"goals":["What a slice is","The difference between `String` and `&str`","A trap with non-English letters"]},{"id":"m06-p","title":"Practice: references and slices","min":12,"cards":[{"t":"fill","md":"Fill in the blanks: the first function only reads, the second one changes.","code":"fn show(v: ___Vec<i32>) {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", v);\n}\n\nfn add_one(v: ___Vec<i32>) {\n    v.push(1);\n}","answers":[["&"],["&mut","& mut"]]},{"t":"task","md":"Write a function `count_vowels` that counts the vowels `a e i o u` in a string.","code":"fn count_vowels(s: &str) -> usize {\n    0\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", count_vowels(\"programming is fun\"));\n}","solution":"fn count_vowels(s: &str) -> usize {\n    let mut n = 0;\n    for c in s.chars() {\n        if \"aeiou\".contains(c) {\n            n += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    n\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", count_vowels(\"programming is fun\"));\n}","expect":"5","hints":["Go through the letters: `for c in s.chars()`.","The vowel check: `\"aeiou\".contains(c)`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Write a function `double_all` that doubles every element of a vector through `&mut`.","code":"fn double_all(v: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut nums = vec![1, 2, 3];\n    double_all(&mut nums);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", nums);\n}","solution":"fn double_all(v: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    for x in v.iter_mut() {\n        *x *= 2;\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut nums = vec![1, 2, 3];\n    double_all(&mut nums);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", nums);\n}","expect":"[2, 4, 6]","hints":["`for x in v.iter_mut()` gives a mutable reference to each element.","The value behind a reference is changed with `*`: `*x *= 2;`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Fix error E0502: printing the reference gets in the way of the change. Reorder the lines so the program prints `1` and `[1, 2, 3, 4]`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];\n    let first = &v[0];\n    v.push(4);\n    println!(\"{first}\");\n    println!(\"{:?}\", v);\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];\n    let first = &v[0];\n    println!(\"{first}\");\n    v.push(4);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", v);\n}","expect":"1\n[1, 2, 3, 4]","hints":["The reference `first` must finish its work **before** `push`.","Move `println!(\"{first}\");` above `v.push(4);`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Write a function `sum` that takes a slice `&[i32]` and returns the sum. It must work with both an array and a vector.","code":"// write the function sum\n\nfn main() {\n    let arr = [1, 2, 3];\n    let v = vec![10, 20];\n    println!(\"{} {}\", sum(&arr), sum(&v));\n    println!(\"{}\", sum(&arr[1..]));\n}","solution":"fn sum(items: &[i32]) -> i32 {\n    let mut total = 0;\n    for x in items {\n        total += x;\n    }\n    total\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let arr = [1, 2, 3];\n    let v = vec![10, 20];\n    println!(\"{} {}\", sum(&arr), sum(&v));\n    println!(\"{}\", sum(&arr[1..]));\n}","expect":"6 30\n5","hints":["The header: `fn sum(items: &[i32]) -> i32`","`&arr[1..]` is a slice from the second element to the end."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`&` reads, `&mut` changes.","While there is a reader, nobody can write: finish with the reference earlier.","A `&[i32]` parameter accepts an array, a vector, and parts of them."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Pass data by reference","Change data through `&mut`","Work with slices"]}]},{"id":"m07","title":"Structs","sub":"Your own data types","lessons":[{"id":"m07-1","title":"A struct is a form","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A form with fields","md":"A **struct** is your own type made of several named fields.\n\n```\nstruct User {\n    name: String,\n    age: u32,\n    active: bool,\n}\n```\n> Analogy: a blank form with the fields \"name\", \"age\", \"active\". The blank form is the type. A filled-in form is a value.\n\nStruct names start with a capital letter: `User`, `GameCharacter`."},{"t":"code","code":"struct User {\n    name: String,\n    age: u32,\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut user = User {\n        name: String::from(\"Anna\"),\n        age: 25,\n    };\n    println!(\"{} is {} years old\", user.name, user.age);\n    user.age += 1;\n    println!(\"Happy birthday! Now {}\", user.age);\n}","out":"Anna is 25 years old\nHappy birthday! Now 26","after":"A field is read with a dot: `user.age`. You can change it only if the whole variable is `mut`."},{"t":"fill","md":"Fill in the blanks to create a point with the coordinates 3 and 4.","code":"struct Point {\n    x: i32,\n    y: i32,\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = ___ { x: 3, ___: 4 };\n    println!(\"{}, {}\", p.x, p.y);\n}","answers":[["Point"],["y"]]},{"t":"text","title":"A short form for fields","md":"If a variable has the same name as a field, you do not need to repeat it:\n\n```\nfn new_user(name: String, age: u32) -> User {\n    User { name, age } // instead of name: name, age: age\n}\n```"},{"t":"text","title":"Tuple structs","md":"Some structs have no field names. They are called **tuple structs**:\n\n```\nstruct Meters(f64);\nstruct Color(u8, u8, u8);\n\nlet height = Meters(1.8);\nlet red = Color(255, 0, 0);\nprintln!(\"{}\", red.0); // 255\n```\nWhy? So you do not mix up similar values. `Meters` and `Seconds` both hold an `f64` inside, but the compiler will not let you add meters to seconds."},{"t":"deep","title":"Creating a struct from another one","md":"There is a short form \"take the other fields from another struct\": `..other`.\n\n`..base` is written **last**. The fields given explicitly come from the new value.","code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Settings {\n    volume: u8,\n    dark: bool,\n    lang: &'static str,\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let base = Settings { volume: 50, dark: false, lang: \"en\" };\n    let mine = Settings { dark: true, ..base };\n    println!(\"{:?}\", mine);\n}","out":"Settings { volume: 50, dark: true, lang: \"en\" }"},{"t":"recap","items":["`struct Name { field: type, ... }` is your own type.","A value: `Name { field: value, ... }`.","A field: `value.field`."]}],"goals":["Create your own type with `struct`","Read and change fields"]},{"id":"m07-2","title":"Printing a struct: Debug","min":5,"cards":[{"t":"code","fails":true,"md":"Let us try to print a struct with `{}`. Run it.","code":"struct Cat {\n    name: String,\n    lives: u8,\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let cat = Cat { name: String::from(\"Tom\"), lives: 9 };\n    println!(\"{}\", cat);\n}","after":"Error `E0277`: Rust does not know **how** to display your struct nicely. It is not a number and not a string."},{"t":"text","title":"derive(Debug) — printing for the developer","md":"Add the line `#[derive(Debug)]` above the struct and print it with `{:?}`.\n\n- `#[derive(Debug)]` means \"teach this type to print itself for debugging\".\n- `{:?}` means \"print the debug view\".\n- `{:#?}` is the same, but pretty, on several lines."},{"t":"code","code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Cat {\n    name: String,\n    lives: u8,\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let cat = Cat { name: String::from(\"Tom\"), lives: 9 };\n    println!(\"{:?}\", cat);\n    println!(\"{cat:#?}\");\n}","out":"Cat { name: \"Tom\", lives: 9 }\nCat {\n    name: \"Tom\",\n    lives: 9,\n}"},{"t":"quiz","md":"What do you need to print a struct with `{:?}`?","options":["Nothing, it just works","Add `#[derive(Debug)]` above the struct","Write a function `print`"],"answer":1,"why":["Without `derive(Debug)` the compiler gives error E0277.","Correct.","You could, but there is a shorter way."]},{"t":"deep","title":"What derive really does","md":"`#[derive(Debug)]` asks the compiler to **write code for you**. The compiler creates an implementation of the trait `Debug`: a function that prints the struct name and all its fields.\n\nWritten by hand, it would be about 10 lines. Traits, and how to write such implementations yourself, are in module 12."},{"t":"recap","items":["`#[derive(Debug)]` + `{:?}` is quick printing for debugging.","`{:#?}` is the same, but in a column."]}],"goals":["How to print a whole struct","What `#[derive(Debug)]` is"]},{"id":"m07-3","title":"Methods: impl","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A method is a function \"inside\" a type","md":"A **method** is a function that belongs to a type. Methods are written in an `impl` block (implementation).\n\n```\nimpl Rectangle {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32 {\n        self.width * self.height\n    }\n}\n```\n`self` means \"the value the method was called on\". The call: `rect.area()`."},{"t":"code","code":"struct Rectangle {\n    width: u32,\n    height: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Rectangle {\n    fn area(&self) -> u32 {\n        self.width * self.height\n    }\n\n    fn grow(&mut self, by: u32) {\n        self.width += by;\n        self.height += by;\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut r = Rectangle { width: 3, height: 4 };\n    println!(\"Area: {}\", r.area());\n    r.grow(1);\n    println!(\"After growing: {}\", r.area());\n}","out":"Area: 12\nAfter growing: 20"},{"t":"text","title":"Three kinds of self","md":"- `&self` — the method only **looks**. The most common.\n- `&mut self` — the method **changes** the struct.\n- `self` — the method **takes** the whole struct. This is rare.\n\nThese are the same rules of ownership and borrowing. Just about the struct itself."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Which `self` does a method `rename`, which changes the field `name`, need?","options":["`&self`","`&mut self`","None, methods do not change fields"],"answer":1,"why":["With `&self` you can only read.","Correct. To change, you need a mutable reference.","They do, if they have `&mut self`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Add a method `can_hold` that returns `true` if the rectangle `self` is bigger than `other` in both width and height.","code":"struct Rectangle {\n    width: u32,\n    height: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Rectangle {\n    // your method can_hold\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let big = Rectangle { width: 10, height: 8 };\n    let small = Rectangle { width: 3, height: 2 };\n    println!(\"{}\", big.can_hold(&small));\n    println!(\"{}\", small.can_hold(&big));\n}","solution":"struct Rectangle {\n    width: u32,\n    height: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Rectangle {\n    fn can_hold(&self, other: &Rectangle) -> bool {\n        self.width > other.width && self.height > other.height\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let big = Rectangle { width: 10, height: 8 };\n    let small = Rectangle { width: 3, height: 2 };\n    println!(\"{}\", big.can_hold(&small));\n    println!(\"{}\", small.can_hold(&big));\n}","expect":"true\nfalse","hints":["The header: `fn can_hold(&self, other: &Rectangle) -> bool`","The body: `self.width > other.width && self.height > other.height`"]},{"t":"deep","title":"What the dot means in a method call","md":"The form `r.area()` is a shortcut. In full it looks like this:\n\n```\nRectangle::area(&r)\n```\nRust inserts `&`, `&mut` or nothing by itself — depending on what `self` says. So you do not need to write `(&r).area()`.\n\nThis is called **automatic referencing**."},{"t":"recap","items":["Methods are written in `impl Type { ... }`.","`&self` reads, `&mut self` changes, `self` takes.","The call: `value.method()`."]}],"goals":["Attach functions to a struct","The difference between `&self`, `&mut self` and `self`"]},{"id":"m07-4","title":"The new constructor","min":5,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Associated functions","md":"In `impl` you can write functions **without** `self`. They are called with `::`, not with a dot.\n\nUsually this is used for a **constructor** — a function that creates a new value. By tradition it is called `new`.\n\n```\nimpl Counter {\n    fn new() -> Counter {\n        Counter { count: 0 }\n    }\n}\nlet c = Counter::new();\n```\nYou have already seen this: `String::new()`, `Vec::new()`."},{"t":"text","title":"Self — the short name of your type","md":"Inside `impl` you can write `Self` with a capital letter instead of the type name:\n\n```\nfn new() -> Self {\n    Self { count: 0 }\n}\n```"},{"t":"task","md":"Complete the constructor `Player::new(name)` that creates a player with 100 health points.","code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Player {\n    name: String,\n    hp: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Player {\n    fn new(name: &str) -> Self {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = Player::new(\"Hero\");\n    println!(\"{} {}\", p.name, p.hp);\n}","solution":"#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Player {\n    name: String,\n    hp: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Player {\n    fn new(name: &str) -> Self {\n        Self { name: name.to_string(), hp: 100 }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = Player::new(\"Hero\");\n    println!(\"{} {}\", p.name, p.hp);\n}","expect":"Hero 100","hints":["Return `Self { name: ..., hp: 100 }`.","Make a `String` from the `&str` with `name.to_string()`."]},{"t":"text","title":"Several impl blocks","md":"One type can have **several** `impl` blocks. For example, constructors in one and calculations in another. The compiler puts them together.\n\nThis is a convenient way to split a big type into meaningful parts."},{"t":"recap","items":["A function in `impl` without `self` is called as `Type::function()`.","The constructor is traditionally called `new`.","`Self` is the short name of the current type."]}],"goals":["Functions without `self` in `impl`","The custom of calling the constructor `new`"]},{"id":"m07-p","title":"Practice: structs","min":12,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Describe a struct `Book` with the fields `title: String` and `pages: u32`. Create a book and print `\"Dune\": 704 pages`.","code":"// describe the struct Book\n\nfn main() {\n    let b = Book { title: String::from(\"Dune\"), pages: 704 };\n    println!(\"\\\"{}\\\": {} pages\", b.title, b.pages);\n}","solution":"struct Book {\n    title: String,\n    pages: u32,\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let b = Book { title: String::from(\"Dune\"), pages: 704 };\n    println!(\"\\\"{}\\\": {} pages\", b.title, b.pages);\n}","expect":"\"Dune\": 704 pages","hints":["`struct Book { title: String, pages: u32 }`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Add a method `is_square` that returns `true` if the width equals the height.","code":"struct Rect {\n    w: u32,\n    h: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Rect {\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Rect { w: 3, h: 3 }.is_square());\n    println!(\"{}\", Rect { w: 3, h: 5 }.is_square());\n}","solution":"struct Rect {\n    w: u32,\n    h: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Rect {\n    fn is_square(&self) -> bool {\n        self.w == self.h\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Rect { w: 3, h: 3 }.is_square());\n    println!(\"{}\", Rect { w: 3, h: 5 }.is_square());\n}","expect":"true\nfalse","hints":["The method only reads the fields, so it needs `&self`.","`fn is_square(&self) -> bool { self.w == self.h }`"]},{"t":"task","md":"A bank account. Complete the methods `deposit` (put money in) and `withdraw` (take money out). If there is not enough money, `withdraw` returns `false` and changes nothing.","code":"struct Account {\n    balance: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Account {\n    fn deposit(&mut self, amount: u32) {\n    }\n\n    fn withdraw(&mut self, amount: u32) -> bool {\n        false\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut acc = Account { balance: 100 };\n    acc.deposit(50);\n    println!(\"{}\", acc.withdraw(120));\n    println!(\"{}\", acc.withdraw(100));\n    println!(\"Left: {}\", acc.balance);\n}","solution":"struct Account {\n    balance: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Account {\n    fn deposit(&mut self, amount: u32) {\n        self.balance += amount;\n    }\n\n    fn withdraw(&mut self, amount: u32) -> bool {\n        if amount > self.balance {\n            return false;\n        }\n        self.balance -= amount;\n        true\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut acc = Account { balance: 100 };\n    acc.deposit(50);\n    println!(\"{}\", acc.withdraw(120));\n    println!(\"{}\", acc.withdraw(100));\n    println!(\"Left: {}\", acc.balance);\n}","expect":"true\nfalse\nLeft: 30","hints":["In `deposit`: `self.balance += amount;`","In `withdraw`: if `amount > self.balance` — `return false;`, otherwise decrease the balance and return `true`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Add a constructor `Point::origin()` (the point 0, 0) and a method `dist` that returns the distance to another point. The formula: the square root of `(x1−x2)² + (y1−y2)²`. The square root is the method `.sqrt()`.","code":"struct Point {\n    x: f64,\n    y: f64,\n}\n\nimpl Point {\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a = Point::origin();\n    let b = Point { x: 3.0, y: 4.0 };\n    println!(\"{}\", a.dist(&b));\n}","solution":"struct Point {\n    x: f64,\n    y: f64,\n}\n\nimpl Point {\n    fn origin() -> Self {\n        Point { x: 0.0, y: 0.0 }\n    }\n\n    fn dist(&self, other: &Point) -> f64 {\n        let dx = self.x - other.x;\n        let dy = self.y - other.y;\n        (dx * dx + dy * dy).sqrt()\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a = Point::origin();\n    let b = Point { x: 3.0, y: 4.0 };\n    println!(\"{}\", a.dist(&b));\n}","expect":"5","hints":["`fn origin() -> Self { Point { x: 0.0, y: 0.0 } }`","`fn dist(&self, other: &Point) -> f64` — calculate `dx`, `dy` and return `(dx * dx + dy * dy).sqrt()`."]},{"t":"task","md":"A tuple struct `Celsius(f64)`. Add a method `to_fahrenheit` that returns degrees Fahrenheit: `C × 1.8 + 32`.","code":"struct Celsius(f64);\n\nimpl Celsius {\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Celsius(100.0).to_fahrenheit());\n}","solution":"struct Celsius(f64);\n\nimpl Celsius {\n    fn to_fahrenheit(&self) -> f64 {\n        self.0 * 1.8 + 32.0\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Celsius(100.0).to_fahrenheit());\n}","expect":"212","hints":["The value inside a tuple struct is `self.0`.","`fn to_fahrenheit(&self) -> f64 { self.0 * 1.8 + 32.0 }`"]},{"t":"recap","items":["Reads — `&self`, changes — `&mut self`.","A constructor without `self` is called as `Type::name()`.","The value of a tuple struct is `self.0`."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Describe your own types","Write methods and constructors"]}]},{"id":"m08","title":"Enums and match","sub":"One of several options","lessons":[{"id":"m08-1","title":"An enum is one option from a list","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Choosing from a list","md":"An **enum** (enumeration) is a type whose value is **one** variant from a list known in advance.\n\n```\nenum Light {\n    Red,\n    Yellow,\n    Green,\n}\nlet now = Light::Red;\n```\n> Analogy: a traffic light shows red, yellow or green. Nothing else is possible."},{"t":"text","title":"Variants can carry data","md":"Each variant can have its own data:\n\n```\nenum Message {\n    Quit,                    // no data\n    Move { x: i32, y: i32 }, // like a struct\n    Write(String),           // one value\n}\n```\nOne type `Message`, but inside it there are different kinds of messages."},{"t":"quiz","md":"How many variants does a value of type `Light` hold at the same time?","options":["Exactly one","All three","Zero or one"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct. That is the point of an enum.","That would be a struct with three fields.","An enum value cannot be empty. But for \"there may be nothing\" there is Option — soon."]},{"t":"text","title":"Enums can have methods too","md":"Like a struct, an `enum` can have an `impl` block:\n\n```\nimpl Light {\n    fn next(&self) -> Light {\n        match self {\n            Light::Red => Light::Green,\n            Light::Green => Light::Yellow,\n            Light::Yellow => Light::Red,\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\nHere `match` checks which light is on now. The next lesson is about it."},{"t":"recap","items":["`enum` is one variant from a list.","A variant is written with `::` — `Light::Red`.","Variants can hold data."]}],"goals":["What an enum is","How to store data in variants"]},{"id":"m08-2","title":"match — checking variants","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"match is a sorting machine","md":"`match` looks at a value and picks the matching branch. This is **pattern matching**.\n\n```\nmatch light {\n    Light::Red => println!(\"Stop\"),\n    Light::Yellow => println!(\"Wait\"),\n    Light::Green => println!(\"Go\"),\n}\n```\n> Analogy: a parcel sorter sends each parcel to the right window by its address."},{"t":"code","code":"enum Coin {\n    Penny,\n    Nickel,\n    Dime,\n}\n\nfn value(c: Coin) -> u32 {\n    match c {\n        Coin::Penny => 1,\n        Coin::Nickel => 5,\n        Coin::Dime => 10,\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let total = value(Coin::Nickel) + value(Coin::Dime);\n    println!(\"Total: {total} cents\");\n}","out":"Total: 15 cents","after":"`match` is also an expression: it returns the value of the chosen branch."},{"t":"text","title":"match checks everything","md":"If you forget a variant, the compiler does not allow it: error `E0004`.\n\nThis is a huge help. When you add a new variant to an enum, the compiler shows **all** places that need updating.\n\nYou do not want to list everything? The branch `_` catches \"everything else\":\n\n```\nmatch number {\n    1 => println!(\"one\"),\n    2 => println!(\"two\"),\n    _ => println!(\"many\"),\n}\n```"},{"t":"code","md":"Data from a variant is taken out right in the `match`:","code":"enum Shape {\n    Circle(f64),\n    Rect(f64, f64),\n}\n\nfn area(s: &Shape) -> f64 {\n    match s {\n        Shape::Circle(r) => 3.14 * r * r,\n        Shape::Rect(w, h) => w * h,\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", area(&Shape::Rect(2.0, 3.0)));\n    println!(\"{}\", area(&Shape::Circle(1.0)));\n}","out":"6\n3.14"},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does the branch `_ =>` do in a `match`?","options":["It catches errors","It matches any value that was not matched earlier","It skips the whole `match`"],"answer":1,"why":["It is not about errors, it is about the remaining variants.","Correct. It is \"everything else\".","The `_` branch runs if nothing matched before it."]},{"t":"task","md":"Write a function `describe` that for a number returns: `0` → `zero`, `1` → `one`, anything else → `other`. Use `match`.","code":"fn describe(n: i32) -> &'static str {\n    // your match\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", describe(0));\n    println!(\"{}\", describe(1));\n    println!(\"{}\", describe(42));\n}","solution":"fn describe(n: i32) -> &'static str {\n    match n {\n        0 => \"zero\",\n        1 => \"one\",\n        _ => \"other\",\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", describe(0));\n    println!(\"{}\", describe(1));\n    println!(\"{}\", describe(42));\n}","expect":"zero\none\nother","hints":["`&'static str` simply means \"text in quotes\". The strange label `'static` is explained later.","The branches: `0 => \"zero\",`, `1 => \"one\",`, `_ => \"other\",`"]},{"t":"text","title":"More complex patterns","md":"In `match` branches you can write more than concrete values:\n\n- `1 | 2` means \"1 or 2\";\n- `3..=9` means \"from 3 to 9 inclusive\";\n- `n if n < 0` means \"any `n`, but only if it is less than zero\". Such a condition is called a **guard**.\n\n```\nlet text = match score {\n    0 => \"zero\",\n    1 | 2 => \"few\",\n    3..=9 => \"medium\",\n    n if n < 0 => \"below zero\",\n    _ => \"many\",\n};\n```"},{"t":"deep","title":"match on a tuple","md":"`match` can check several values at once if you put them into a tuple:","code":"fn main() {\n    for (a, b) in [(true, true), (true, false), (false, false)] {\n        let text = match (a, b) {\n            (true, true) => \"both\",\n            (true, false) | (false, true) => \"one\",\n            (false, false) => \"nobody\",\n        };\n        println!(\"{text}\");\n    }\n}","out":"both\none\nnobody"},{"t":"recap","items":["`match` picks a branch by pattern.","All variants must be covered, otherwise there is an error.","`_` means \"everything else\"."]}],"goals":["Check an enum with `match`","Understand why `match` requires all variants"]},{"id":"m08-3","title":"Option: maybe there is a value, maybe not","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"There may be no value","md":"Often a result may be missing. For example, we look for a user, and there is none.\n\nMany languages have `null` for this — \"nothing\". Its inventor called `null` his \"billion-dollar mistake\": programs kept crashing because someone forgot to check.\n\nRust has no `null`. It has `Option` instead."},{"t":"text","title":"Option is a box that may be empty","md":"```\nenum Option<T> {\n    Some(T), // there is a value inside\n    None,    // empty\n}\n```\n> Analogy: a gift box. There may be a gift inside (`Some`), or it may be empty (`None`). You do not know until you open it. And Rust makes you open it.\n\n`T` means \"any type\". The module about generics explains it in detail."},{"t":"code","code":"fn divide(a: i32, b: i32) -> Option<i32> {\n    if b == 0 {\n        None\n    } else {\n        Some(a / b)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for b in [2, 0] {\n        match divide(10, b) {\n            Some(result) => println!(\"10 / {b} = {result}\"),\n            None => println!(\"You cannot divide by {b}\"),\n        }\n    }\n}","out":"10 / 2 = 5\nYou cannot divide by 0"},{"t":"quiz","md":"Can you add `Option<i32>` and `i32` directly: `Some(5) + 3`?","options":["Yes, Rust takes out the 5 by itself","No, first you have to take the value out of the Option"],"answer":1,"why":["No. First you have to check that the box is not empty.","Correct. This is what protects you from \"forgot to check\"."]},{"t":"text","title":"Handy ways to open the box","md":"- `if let Some(x) = value { ... }` — do something only if there is a value.\n- `value.unwrap_or(0)` — take the value, or use 0 if it is empty.\n- `value.unwrap()` — take the value, and if it is empty — **panic**. Only when you are sure.\n\n```\nlet first = vec![3, 1, 2].first().copied().unwrap_or(0);\n```"},{"t":"code","md":"`if let` is a short `match` for one variant:","code":"fn main() {\n    let names = vec![\"Anna\", \"Bob\"];\n    if let Some(name) = names.get(1) {\n        println!(\"Second: {name}\");\n    }\n    if let Some(name) = names.get(5) {\n        println!(\"Sixth: {name}\");\n    } else {\n        println!(\"There is no sixth\");\n    }\n}","out":"Second: Bob\nThere is no sixth","after":"`get(i)` returns an `Option`: the element may not exist. It is safer than `[i]`."},{"t":"deep","title":"What Option costs","md":"`Option<&T>` takes **the same** amount of memory as a plain `&T`. How?\n\nA reference in Rust is never the zero address. So the compiler uses the zero address as the marker for `None`. No extra space for \"there is / there is not\" is needed.\n\nThe safety of `Option` costs nothing in memory. The same is true for `Option<Box<T>>`.","code":"fn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", std::mem::size_of::<&i32>());\n    println!(\"{}\", std::mem::size_of::<Option<&i32>>());\n}","out":"8\n8"},{"t":"recap","items":["Rust has no `null`. It has `Option<T>`: `Some(x)` or `None`.","Rust makes you check whether there is a value.","`if let Some(x) = ...`, `unwrap_or(fallback)`, `unwrap()` — only when you are sure."]}],"goals":["Why Rust has no `null`","How to work with `Option`"]},{"id":"m08-4","title":"let else and let chains","min":5,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"let else — \"take it out or leave\"","md":"Often you need: \"take the value out, and if there is none — leave the function\". For this there is `let ... else`:\n\n```\nfn greet_first(names: &[&str]) {\n    let Some(first) = names.first() else {\n        println!(\"Nobody is here\");\n        return;\n    };\n    println!(\"Hello, {first}!\");\n}\n```\nAfter this block, `first` is a normal value, without `Some`."},{"t":"text","title":"let chains (Rust 2024)","md":"In the new Rust 2024 edition you can join checks in an `if` with `&&`:\n\n```\nif let Some(age) = maybe_age && age >= 18 {\n    println!(\"Adult\");\n}\n```\nBefore this, you had to put an `if` inside another `if`."},{"t":"code","code":"fn parse_age(text: &str) -> Option<u32> {\n    let Ok(n) = text.parse::<u32>() else {\n        return None;\n    };\n    Some(n)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for t in [\"25\", \"abc\", \"15\"] {\n        if let Some(age) = parse_age(t) && age >= 18 {\n            println!(\"{t}: adult\");\n        } else {\n            println!(\"{t}: no\");\n        }\n    }\n}","out":"25: adult\nabc: no\n15: no","after":"`Ok(n)` is a variant of the type `Result`. It is about errors; there is a separate module about it."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What must be in the `else` block of `let ... else`?","options":["Nothing, it can be empty","A way out: `return`, `break`, `continue` or a panic"],"answer":1,"why":["No: after `else` the program cannot continue without the value.","Correct. The block must \"leave\", because there is no value."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`let Some(x) = opt else { return; };` — take it out or leave.","`if let Some(x) = opt && condition` — a let chain in Rust 2024."]}],"goals":["Leave early with `let ... else`","Several conditions with `&&` in `if let`"]},{"id":"m08-p","title":"Practice: enum and match","min":12,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"There are directions. Complete the function `turn_right`: north → east → south → west → north.","code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nenum Dir {\n    North,\n    East,\n    South,\n    West,\n}\n\nfn turn_right(d: Dir) -> Dir {\n    d\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", turn_right(Dir::North));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", turn_right(Dir::West));\n}","solution":"#[derive(Debug)]\nenum Dir {\n    North,\n    East,\n    South,\n    West,\n}\n\nfn turn_right(d: Dir) -> Dir {\n    match d {\n        Dir::North => Dir::East,\n        Dir::East => Dir::South,\n        Dir::South => Dir::West,\n        Dir::West => Dir::North,\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", turn_right(Dir::North));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", turn_right(Dir::West));\n}","expect":"East\nNorth","hints":["You need `match d { ... }` with four branches.","The first branch: `Dir::North => Dir::East,`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Turn points into a grade with `match` and ranges: 90–100 → `excellent`, 70–89 → `good`, 50–69 → `pass`, everything else → `fail`.","code":"fn grade(score: u32) -> &'static str {\n    \"fail\"\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for s in [95, 75, 50, 10] {\n        println!(\"{}\", grade(s));\n    }\n}","solution":"fn grade(score: u32) -> &'static str {\n    match score {\n        90..=100 => \"excellent\",\n        70..=89 => \"good\",\n        50..=69 => \"pass\",\n        _ => \"fail\",\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for s in [95, 75, 50, 10] {\n        println!(\"{}\", grade(s));\n    }\n}","expect":"excellent\ngood\npass\nfail","hints":["A branch with a range: `90..=100 => \"excellent\",`","The last branch: `_ => \"fail\",`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Write a function `first_negative` that returns the first negative number of a slice in `Some`, or `None` if there is none.","code":"fn first_negative(items: &[i32]) -> Option<i32> {\n    None\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", first_negative(&[3, -4, -1]));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", first_negative(&[1, 2]));\n}","solution":"fn first_negative(items: &[i32]) -> Option<i32> {\n    for &x in items {\n        if x < 0 {\n            return Some(x);\n        }\n    }\n    None\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", first_negative(&[3, -4, -1]));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", first_negative(&[1, 2]));\n}","expect":"Some(-4)\nNone","hints":["In the loop, as soon as you find `x < 0`, return `Some(x)` with `return`.","`for &x in items` takes the number out of the reference right away. After the loop return `None`."]},{"t":"task","md":"With `if let`, print the square of a number only if it exists. For `None` print nothing.","code":"fn main() {\n    let values = [Some(3), None, Some(5)];\n    for v in values {\n        // your if let\n    }\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let values = [Some(3), None, Some(5)];\n    for v in values {\n        if let Some(n) = v {\n            println!(\"{}\", n * n);\n        }\n    }\n}","expect":"9\n25","hints":["`if let Some(n) = v { ... }`","Inside: `println!(\"{}\", n * n);`"]},{"t":"task","md":"An enum of robot commands. Calculate where the robot ends up, starting at position 0: `Forward(n)` adds `n`, `Back(n)` subtracts `n`, `Stop` stops the program.","code":"enum Cmd {\n    Forward(i32),\n    Back(i32),\n    Stop,\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let program = [Cmd::Forward(5), Cmd::Back(2), Cmd::Forward(4), Cmd::Stop, Cmd::Forward(100)];\n    let mut pos = 0;\n    // go through program\n    println!(\"{pos}\");\n}","solution":"enum Cmd {\n    Forward(i32),\n    Back(i32),\n    Stop,\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let program = [Cmd::Forward(5), Cmd::Back(2), Cmd::Forward(4), Cmd::Stop, Cmd::Forward(100)];\n    let mut pos = 0;\n    for cmd in program {\n        match cmd {\n            Cmd::Forward(n) => pos += n,\n            Cmd::Back(n) => pos -= n,\n            Cmd::Stop => break,\n        }\n    }\n    println!(\"{pos}\");\n}","expect":"7","hints":["`for cmd in program { match cmd { ... } }`","The branches: `Cmd::Forward(n) => pos += n,`, `Cmd::Back(n) => pos -= n,`, `Cmd::Stop => break,`"]},{"t":"recap","items":["`match` checks all variants, `_` catches the rest.","Ranges `a..=b` and `|` make branches shorter.","`Option` means \"there is a value or not\", `if let` checks one variant."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Describe variants with `enum`","Check them with `match`, `if let`, `Option`"]}]},{"id":"m09","title":"Collections","sub":"A closer look at Vec, String and HashMap","lessons":[{"id":"m09-1","title":"More about Vec","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"The main operations","md":"```\nlet mut v = vec![10, 20, 30];\nv.push(40);          // add to the end\nlet last = v.pop();  // take the last one: Some(40)\nv[0] = 5;            // replace the first one\nv.remove(1);         // remove by index\nv.insert(0, 1);      // insert at the start\nv.contains(&30);     // is the element there: true\n```"},{"t":"text","title":"[] or get","md":"- `v[i]` is fast, but if the element does not exist — **panic**.\n- `v.get(i)` returns an `Option`. No element — `None`, no panic.\n\n> If the index comes from the user or from a file, use `get`."},{"t":"code","md":"Going through with changes: `&mut` lets you change each element.","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut prices = vec![100, 250, 80];\n    for p in &mut prices {\n        *p = *p * 2;\n    }\n    println!(\"{:?}\", prices);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", prices.get(10));\n}","out":"[200, 500, 160]\nNone","after":"`*p` is **dereferencing**: \"the value that the reference looks at\". Without `*` we would be changing the reference itself."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `vec![1, 2, 3].get(3)` return?","options":["3","None","The program panics"],"answer":1,"why":["Index 3 is the fourth element. It does not exist.","Correct. There is no element, and `get` says so honestly.","`[3]` panics, but `get` does not."]},{"t":"task","md":"Remove all odd numbers from the vector and print the result. Hint: a vector has the method `retain`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut nums = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];\n    // your code\n    println!(\"{:?}\", nums);\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let mut nums = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];\n    nums.retain(|n| n % 2 == 0);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", nums);\n}","expect":"[2, 4, 6]","hints":["`retain` keeps only the elements for which the condition is true.","`nums.retain(|n| n % 2 == 0);` — the form `|n| ...` is called a closure. Details are in the module about iterators."]},{"t":"text","title":"Sorting and order","md":"- `v.sort()` sorts in ascending order.\n- `v.reverse()` reverses.\n- `v.dedup()` removes **neighboring** repeats. That is why you sort first, then `dedup`.\n- `v.len()`, `v.is_empty()` give the length and check \"is it empty\".\n\n```\nlet mut v = vec![3, 1, 3, 2, 1];\nv.sort();   // [1, 1, 2, 3, 3]\nv.dedup();  // [1, 2, 3]\n```\n!> Fractional numbers `f64` cannot be sorted this way: they have a special value NaN (\"not a number\") that cannot be compared. For them, write `v.sort_by(|a, b| a.total_cmp(b))`."},{"t":"recap","items":["`push`, `pop`, `insert`, `remove`, `contains`.","`v[i]` panics out of bounds, `v.get(i)` returns an `Option`.","`for x in &mut v` changes elements; `*x` is the value itself."]}],"goals":["Add, remove and change vector elements","Safe access with `get`"]},{"id":"m09-2","title":"More about String","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"How to build a string","md":"```\nlet mut s = String::from(\"Hello\");\ns.push_str(\", world\"); // add text\ns.push('!');           // add one character\nlet name = \"Anna\";\nlet msg = format!(\"Hello, {name}!\"); // like println!, but into a string\n```\n`format!` is the most convenient way. It prints nothing; it returns a `String`."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let text = \"  Rust makes   code reliable  \";\n    let clean = text.trim();\n    println!(\"[{clean}]\");\n    println!(\"Words: {}\", clean.split_whitespace().count());\n    println!(\"Upper case: {}\", clean.to_uppercase());\n    println!(\"Has \\\"code\\\": {}\", clean.contains(\"code\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", clean.replace(\"reliable\", \"fast\"));\n}","out":"[Rust makes   code reliable]\nWords: 4\nUpper case: RUST MAKES   CODE RELIABLE\nHas \"code\": true\nRust makes   code fast","after":"- `trim` removes spaces at the edges.\n- `split_whitespace` cuts the text into words at spaces."},{"t":"quiz","md":"How is `format!` different from `println!`?","options":["`format!` returns a string instead of printing it","`format!` prints without a new line","There is no difference"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct. You can then store it, pass it on, print it.","That is what `print!` does.","The difference is important: one prints, the other creates a `String`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Reverse each word of the string while keeping the order of the words. For \"hello world\" the result is \"olleh dlrow\".","code":"fn main() {\n    let text = \"hello world\";\n    let mut result = Vec::new();\n    for word in text.split_whitespace() {\n        // reverse word and put it into result\n    }\n    println!(\"{}\", result.join(\" \"));\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let text = \"hello world\";\n    let mut result = Vec::new();\n    for word in text.split_whitespace() {\n        let reversed: String = word.chars().rev().collect();\n        result.push(reversed);\n    }\n    println!(\"{}\", result.join(\" \"));\n}","expect":"olleh dlrow","hints":["`word.chars()` goes through the letters, `.rev()` reverses them.","Collect them back into a string: `let r: String = word.chars().rev().collect();`"]},{"t":"deep","title":"Bytes, characters, and what a person sees","md":"A string in Rust can be read on three levels:\n- `bytes()` — the UTF-8 bytes;\n- `chars()` — the Unicode characters (`char`);\n- what a person sees as one symbol. Sometimes this is **several** `char`s in a row.\n\nOn screen these are 2 symbols: \"é\" and \"👍🏽\". But `é` here is written as `e` plus a separate accent mark (`\\u{301}` is its Unicode number), and 👍🏽 as a thumb plus a skin tone. For working with such \"visible symbols\" there is a separate library, `unicode-segmentation`. It is not in the standard library.","code":"fn main() {\n    let s = \"e\\u{301}👍🏽\"; // e + an accent mark, a thumb + a skin tone\n    println!(\"bytes: {}\", s.len());\n    println!(\"chars: {}\", s.chars().count());\n}","out":"bytes: 11\nchars: 4"},{"t":"recap","items":["`push_str`, `push`, `format!` build a string.","`trim`, `split_whitespace`, `contains`, `replace`, `to_uppercase`.","Letters — through `.chars()`."]}],"goals":["Build strings from pieces","Go through words and letters"]},{"id":"m09-3","title":"HashMap — a dictionary","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A dictionary","md":"A **HashMap** stores \"key → value\" pairs. A value is found quickly by its key.\n\n> Analogy: a phone book. You find the number by the name. You do not need to read through everything.\n\nYou need to bring HashMap in with the line `use std::collections::HashMap;`."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::collections::HashMap;\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut phones = HashMap::new();\n    phones.insert(\"Anna\", \"111-22-33\");\n    phones.insert(\"Bob\", \"444-55-66\");\n\n    match phones.get(\"Anna\") {\n        Some(num) => println!(\"Anna's number: {num}\"),\n        None => println!(\"I do not know this person\"),\n    }\n    println!(\"Is Vic there? {}\", phones.contains_key(\"Vic\"));\n    println!(\"Contacts in total: {}\", phones.len());\n}","out":"Anna's number: 111-22-33\nIs Vic there? false\nContacts in total: 2","after":"!> The order of elements in a HashMap is not guaranteed. If the order matters, use `BTreeMap`: it keeps the keys sorted."},{"t":"text","title":"entry — \"find or create\"","md":"A common task: increase a counter, and if the key is not there yet — start from zero.\n\n```\n*counts.entry(word).or_insert(0) += 1;\n```\nRead it as: \"find the entry `word`; if it is not there, insert 0; add 1\"."},{"t":"task","md":"Count how many times each word appears. We use `BTreeMap` so that the output goes in alphabetical order.","code":"use std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = \"cat dog cat fish cat dog\";\n    let mut counts = BTreeMap::new();\n    for word in text.split_whitespace() {\n        // increase the counter for word\n    }\n    for (word, n) in &counts {\n        println!(\"{word}: {n}\");\n    }\n}","solution":"use std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = \"cat dog cat fish cat dog\";\n    let mut counts = BTreeMap::new();\n    for word in text.split_whitespace() {\n        *counts.entry(word).or_insert(0) += 1;\n    }\n    for (word, n) in &counts {\n        println!(\"{word}: {n}\");\n    }\n}","expect":"cat: 3\ndog: 2\nfish: 1","hints":["Use `entry(word).or_insert(0)`.","In full: `*counts.entry(word).or_insert(0) += 1;`"]},{"t":"text","title":"Going through a dictionary, and HashSet","md":"You can go through all pairs with a loop:\n\n```\nfor (name, phone) in &phones {\n    println!(\"{name}: {phone}\");\n}\n```\nIf you only need keys without values, there is **HashSet** — a set of unique elements:\n\n```\nuse std::collections::HashSet;\nlet mut seen = HashSet::new();\nseen.insert(\"cat\");\nseen.insert(\"cat\"); // a repeat is not added\nprintln!(\"{}\", seen.len()); // 1\n```"},{"t":"deep","title":"How HashMap finds a value so fast","md":"A HashMap turns a key into a number with a **hash function**. This number shows right away which \"box\" (bucket) to put the pair into.\n\nSo a lookup does not go through all elements. It computes the hash of the key and looks into one box. This is about equally fast for 10 elements and for a million.\n\nTwo rules follow from this:\n- a key must be hashable (the trait `Hash`) and comparable (`Eq`);\n- the order of elements depends on the hashes, not on the order of insertion.\n\nBy default, Rust's hash function is protected against specially chosen keys that an attacker could use to slow down a server."},{"t":"recap","items":["`HashMap` is key → value. It needs `use std::collections::HashMap;`.","`insert`, `get` (returns an `Option`), `contains_key`.","`*map.entry(k).or_insert(0) += 1;` is a counter.","`BTreeMap` is the same, but with the keys in order."]}],"goals":["Store \"key → value\" pairs","Count how often words appear"]},{"id":"m09-p","title":"Practice: collections","min":14,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Find the median: sort the numbers and take the one in the middle position. For 5 numbers it is the element with index 2.","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut nums = vec![7, 1, 9, 3, 5];\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let mut nums = vec![7, 1, 9, 3, 5];\n    nums.sort();\n    println!(\"{}\", nums[nums.len() / 2]);\n}","expect":"5","hints":["First `nums.sort();`","The middle index: `nums.len() / 2`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Remove repeats from the list and print the result in ascending order.","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut tags = vec![\"rust\", \"code\", \"rust\", \"games\", \"code\"];\n    println!(\"{:?}\", tags);\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let mut tags = vec![\"rust\", \"code\", \"rust\", \"games\", \"code\"];\n    tags.sort();\n    tags.dedup();\n    println!(\"{:?}\", tags);\n}","expect":"[\"code\", \"games\", \"rust\"]","hints":["`dedup` removes only neighboring repeats. You need `sort` first.","`tags.sort(); tags.dedup();`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Make the first letter of each word a capital letter: `hello world` → `Hello World`.","code":"fn capitalize(word: &str) -> String {\n    word.to_string()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = \"hello world rust\";\n    let mut words = Vec::new();\n    for w in text.split_whitespace() {\n        words.push(capitalize(w));\n    }\n    println!(\"{}\", words.join(\" \"));\n}","solution":"fn capitalize(word: &str) -> String {\n    let mut chars = word.chars();\n    match chars.next() {\n        Some(first) => first.to_uppercase().collect::<String>() + chars.as_str(),\n        None => String::new(),\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = \"hello world rust\";\n    let mut words = Vec::new();\n    for w in text.split_whitespace() {\n        words.push(capitalize(w));\n    }\n    println!(\"{}\", words.join(\" \"));\n}","expect":"Hello World Rust","hints":["Take the first letter: `let mut chars = word.chars(); chars.next()` — this is an `Option<char>`.","The capital letter: `first.to_uppercase().collect::<String>()`. The rest of the word: `chars.as_str()`. Join them with `+`."]},{"t":"task","md":"A phone book. Write a function `lookup` that returns the number or the text `unknown`.","code":"use std::collections::HashMap;\n\nfn lookup(book: &HashMap<&str, &str>, name: &str) -> String {\n    String::new()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut book = HashMap::new();\n    book.insert(\"Anna\", \"111\");\n    book.insert(\"Bob\", \"222\");\n    println!(\"{}\", lookup(&book, \"Bob\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", lookup(&book, \"Vic\"));\n}","solution":"use std::collections::HashMap;\n\nfn lookup(book: &HashMap<&str, &str>, name: &str) -> String {\n    match book.get(name) {\n        Some(phone) => phone.to_string(),\n        None => String::from(\"unknown\"),\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut book = HashMap::new();\n    book.insert(\"Anna\", \"111\");\n    book.insert(\"Bob\", \"222\");\n    println!(\"{}\", lookup(&book, \"Bob\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", lookup(&book, \"Vic\"));\n}","expect":"222\nunknown","hints":["`book.get(name)` returns an `Option`.","Check it with `match`: `Some(phone) => phone.to_string()`, `None => String::from(\"unknown\")`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Group the words by their first letter. Use `BTreeMap<char, Vec<&str>>` so the letters go in alphabetical order.","code":"use std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\nfn main() {\n    let words = [\"cat\", \"apple\", \"cake\", \"avocado\", \"banana\"];\n    let mut groups: BTreeMap<char, Vec<&str>> = BTreeMap::new();\n    // fill groups\n    for (letter, list) in &groups {\n        println!(\"{letter}: {}\", list.join(\", \"));\n    }\n}","solution":"use std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\nfn main() {\n    let words = [\"cat\", \"apple\", \"cake\", \"avocado\", \"banana\"];\n    let mut groups: BTreeMap<char, Vec<&str>> = BTreeMap::new();\n    for w in words {\n        let first = w.chars().next().unwrap();\n        groups.entry(first).or_insert(Vec::new()).push(w);\n    }\n    for (letter, list) in &groups {\n        println!(\"{letter}: {}\", list.join(\", \"));\n    }\n}","expect":"a: apple, avocado\nb: banana\nc: cat, cake","hints":["The first letter: `w.chars().next().unwrap()`.","`groups.entry(first).or_insert(Vec::new()).push(w);`"]},{"t":"recap","items":["`sort` + `dedup` remove repeats.","`chars()` for letters, `split_whitespace()` for words.","`entry(k).or_insert(...)` means \"find or create\" in a dictionary."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Sort and clean vectors","Process strings by words and letters","Build dictionaries"]}]},{"id":"m10","title":"Error handling","sub":"What to do when something goes wrong","lessons":[{"id":"m10-1","title":"panic! — an emergency stop","min":5,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Two kinds of problems","md":"1. **Unrecoverable.** The program is in a state where it cannot continue. For example, a bug in the logic. This is a **panic**.\n2. **Recoverable.** A file is missing, the user typed letters instead of a number. This is normal life. This is `Result`."},{"t":"code","md":"`panic!` stops the program with a message.","code":"fn main() {\n    println!(\"Starting\");\n    let level = 0;\n    if level == 0 {\n        panic!(\"The level cannot be zero!\");\n    }\n    println!(\"We never get here\");\n}","after":"The program prints \"Starting\" and stops with the message `panicked at ... The level cannot be zero!`."},{"t":"quiz","md":"The user typed \"abc\" instead of an age. What is better to do?","options":["`panic!`","Return an error and ask again"],"answer":1,"why":["An input mistake is a normal thing. Crashing because of it is rude.","Correct. It is a recoverable situation — `Result` is for that."]},{"t":"text","title":"When a panic is appropriate","md":"- **An impossible situation.** If it happened, there is a bug in the program, and it is better to stop right away.\n- **Examples and drafts.** While a program is a learning exercise, `unwrap()` saves time.\n- **Tests.** A test that panics counts as failed. This is handy.\n\nIn all other cases — when the problem can happen in normal life — return a `Result`."},{"t":"deep","title":"What happens during a panic","md":"By default a panic **unwinds the stack**: Rust leaves all functions in the call chain one by one and deletes their variables. This frees memory and files.\n\nIf a panic happens in a separate thread, only that thread fails. The main thread can notice this and continue working.\n\nIn `Cargo.toml` you can turn on the mode `panic = \"abort\"`. Then the program stops immediately, without cleanup. The program file becomes smaller. This is done, for example, on microcontrollers."},{"t":"recap","items":["A panic is an emergency stop. It is for bugs and impossible situations.","Expected problems are handled with `Result`."]}],"goals":["What a panic is","When panicking is fine"]},{"id":"m10-2","title":"Result: success or error","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Result is a parcel with a note","md":"```\nenum Result<T, E> {\n    Ok(T),  // all good, the result is inside\n    Err(E), // an error, the description is inside\n}\n```\n> Analogy: a parcel. Inside there is either the thing you ordered (`Ok`), or a note \"out of stock\" (`Err`). It is like `Option`, but instead of emptiness there is an explanation."},{"t":"code","md":"`parse` turns text into a number and returns a `Result`:","code":"fn main() {\n    for input in [\"42\", \"forty two\"] {\n        match input.parse::<i32>() {\n            Ok(n) => println!(\"Number: {n}\"),\n            Err(e) => println!(\"Not a number \\\"{input}\\\": {e}\"),\n        }\n    }\n}","out":"Number: 42\nNot a number \"forty two\": invalid digit found in string","after":"`::<i32>` tells `parse` which number we want."},{"t":"text","title":"Your own Result","md":"A function can return a `Result` itself:\n\n```\nfn check_age(age: i32) -> Result<i32, String> {\n    if age < 0 {\n        Err(String::from(\"An age cannot be negative\"))\n    } else {\n        Ok(age)\n    }\n}\n```"},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `\"7\".parse::<i32>()` return?","options":["`Ok(7)`","`7`","`Some(7)`"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct. Parsing worked — the result is in `Ok`.","`parse` always wraps the answer in a `Result`.","`Some` belongs to `Option`. `parse` returns a `Result`."]},{"t":"text","title":"Useful Result methods","md":"- `r.is_ok()`, `r.is_err()` — check what is inside;\n- `r.unwrap_or(0)` — take the value or a fallback;\n- `r.ok()` — turn it into an `Option`: an error becomes `None`.\n\n```\nlet n = \"12\".parse::<i32>().unwrap_or(0);   // 12\nlet m = \"abc\".parse::<i32>().unwrap_or(0);  // 0\n```"},{"t":"recap","items":["`Result<T, E>`: `Ok(value)` or `Err(error)`.","Check it with `match`, like Option.","`parse::<i32>()` returns a `Result`."]}],"goals":["How `Result` is built","Check it with `match`"]},{"id":"m10-3","title":"unwrap, expect and ?","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"unwrap and expect","md":"- `unwrap()` takes the `Ok` value, and on `Err` it panics.\n- `expect(\"message\")` is the same, but with your own panic text. This makes it easier to find where it failed.\n\n> Use them in examples and where an error is truly impossible. In a real program, be careful."},{"t":"text","title":"The ? operator — \"if it is an error, pass it up\"","md":"The `?` sign after a `Result`:\n- if it is `Ok(x)`, gives `x` and continues;\n- if it is `Err(e)`, immediately **returns** this error from the function.\n\n```\nfn sum(a: &str, b: &str) -> Result<i32, std::num::ParseIntError> {\n    let x = a.parse::<i32>()?;\n    let y = b.parse::<i32>()?;\n    Ok(x + y)\n}\n```\n!> `?` works only in a function that itself returns a `Result` (or an `Option`)."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::num::ParseIntError;\n\nfn sum(a: &str, b: &str) -> Result<i32, ParseIntError> {\n    let x = a.parse::<i32>()?;\n    let y = b.parse::<i32>()?;\n    Ok(x + y)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", sum(\"2\", \"3\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", sum(\"2\", \"three\"));\n}","out":"Ok(5)\nErr(ParseIntError { kind: InvalidDigit })"},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `?` do if the result is `Err`?","options":["It panics","It returns the error from the current function","It skips the line"],"answer":1,"why":["`unwrap` panics. `?` is gentler.","Correct. Let the caller deal with it.","An error cannot simply be skipped: the function ends."]},{"t":"task","md":"Complete the function with `?`: it parses two numbers from strings and returns their product.","code":"use std::num::ParseIntError;\n\nfn multiply(a: &str, b: &str) -> Result<i32, ParseIntError> {\n    // your code with ?\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", multiply(\"6\", \"7\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", multiply(\"6\", \"x\").is_err());\n}","solution":"use std::num::ParseIntError;\n\nfn multiply(a: &str, b: &str) -> Result<i32, ParseIntError> {\n    let x: i32 = a.parse()?;\n    let y: i32 = b.parse()?;\n    Ok(x * y)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", multiply(\"6\", \"7\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", multiply(\"6\", \"x\").is_err());\n}","expect":"Ok(42)\ntrue","hints":["`let x: i32 = a.parse()?;` — the same for `b`.","At the end return `Ok(x * y)`."]},{"t":"deep","title":"How ? converts errors","md":"The `?` operator can not only return an error, but also **convert** it into the function's error type. It uses the trait `From` for this.\n\nIf a function returns `Result<T, MyError>`, and inside there is an error of type `ParseIntError`, then `?` calls `MyError::from(error)`. You only need to write once how to convert:\n\n```\nimpl From<std::num::ParseIntError> for MyError {\n    fn from(_: std::num::ParseIntError) -> Self {\n        MyError::NotANumber\n    }\n}\n```\nAfter that, `let n: i32 = s.parse()?;` works inside functions with `MyError`. Traits and `From` are explained in module 12."},{"t":"recap","items":["`unwrap()` / `expect(\"...\")` — take the value or panic.","`?` — take the value or return the error up.","`?` works in functions that return `Result`/`Option`."]}],"goals":["Quick ways to open a Result","Pass an error up with the `?` operator"]},{"id":"m10-4","title":"Your own errors","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Errors as an enum","md":"A good technique: list all errors of your function in an `enum`. Then the calling code knows exactly what can go wrong.\n\n```\n#[derive(Debug)]\nenum AgeError {\n    NotANumber,\n    Negative,\n    TooOld,\n}\n```"},{"t":"code","code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nenum AgeError {\n    NotANumber,\n    Negative,\n    TooOld,\n}\n\nfn parse_age(s: &str) -> Result<u32, AgeError> {\n    let n: i64 = s.trim().parse().map_err(|_| AgeError::NotANumber)?;\n    if n < 0 {\n        return Err(AgeError::Negative);\n    }\n    if n > 150 {\n        return Err(AgeError::TooOld);\n    }\n    Ok(n as u32)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for s in [\"30\", \"-5\", \"hundred\", \"200\"] {\n        println!(\"{s}: {:?}\", parse_age(s));\n    }\n}","out":"30: Ok(30)\n-5: Err(Negative)\nhundred: Err(NotANumber)\n200: Err(TooOld)","after":"`map_err` turns one error into another — here the `parse` error into our `NotANumber`."},{"t":"text","title":"main can also return a Result","md":"Then you can use `?` inside `main`:\n\n```\nfn main() -> Result<(), String> {\n    let n: i32 = \"5\".parse().map_err(|_| \"not a number\".to_string())?;\n    println!(\"{n}\");\n    Ok(())\n}\n```\n`()` is the \"empty value\": success without a result."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why describe errors with an `enum` instead of just text?","options":["The calling code can check each error with `match`","The program runs faster","The compiler does not allow text errors"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct. Text cannot be checked reliably.","It is not about speed; it is about clarity and reliability.","It does: `Result<T, String>` is completely legal."]},{"t":"deep","title":"An error \"done properly\"","md":"To make your error look like the errors of the standard library, implement two traits for it:\n- `Display` — clear text for a person;\n- `std::error::Error` — a marker \"this is an error\".\n\n`Box<dyn std::error::Error>` means \"any error\". With it, `?` accepts errors of different types. In big programs the libraries `anyhow` and `thiserror` are often used for this.","code":"use std::fmt;\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nenum AppError {\n    Empty,\n}\n\nimpl fmt::Display for AppError {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter) -> fmt::Result {\n        write!(f, \"the string is empty\")\n    }\n}\n\nimpl std::error::Error for AppError {}\n\nfn check(s: &str) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {\n    if s.is_empty() {\n        return Err(Box::new(AppError::Empty));\n    }\n    let _n: i32 = s.parse()?;\n    Ok(())\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for s in [\"\", \"x\", \"5\"] {\n        match check(s) {\n            Ok(()) => println!(\"\\\"{s}\\\": fine\"),\n            Err(e) => println!(\"\\\"{s}\\\": {e}\"),\n        }\n    }\n}","out":"\"\": the string is empty\n\"x\": invalid digit found in string\n\"5\": fine"},{"t":"recap","items":["Errors are conveniently described with your own `enum`.","`map_err` turns someone else's error into yours.","`fn main() -> Result<(), E>` allows `?` in `main`."]}],"goals":["Describe possible errors with an `enum`","Return a `Result` from `main`"]},{"id":"m10-p","title":"Practice: errors","min":14,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Write `safe_divide`: when dividing by zero, return `Err(\"division by zero\")`, otherwise `Ok(result)`.","code":"fn safe_divide(a: i32, b: i32) -> Result<i32, String> {\n    Ok(0)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", safe_divide(10, 2));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", safe_divide(1, 0));\n}","solution":"fn safe_divide(a: i32, b: i32) -> Result<i32, String> {\n    if b == 0 {\n        return Err(String::from(\"division by zero\"));\n    }\n    Ok(a / b)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", safe_divide(10, 2));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", safe_divide(1, 0));\n}","expect":"Ok(5)\nErr(\"division by zero\")","hints":["`if b == 0 { return Err(String::from(\"division by zero\")); }`","At the end `Ok(a / b)`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Add up only the strings that are numbers. Skip the others.","code":"fn main() {\n    let items = [\"10\", \"x\", \"5\", \"\", \"7\"];\n    let mut sum = 0;\n    println!(\"{sum}\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let items = [\"10\", \"x\", \"5\", \"\", \"7\"];\n    let mut sum = 0;\n    for s in items {\n        if let Ok(n) = s.parse::<i32>() {\n            sum += n;\n        }\n    }\n    println!(\"{sum}\");\n}","expect":"22","hints":["`if let Ok(n) = s.parse::<i32>() { ... }`","Inside: `sum += n;`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Parse a string like `\"3,4\"` into a point `(i32, i32)`. Use `?` for both parts. If there is no comma, return the error `\"no comma\"`.","code":"fn parse_point(s: &str) -> Result<(i32, i32), String> {\n    let Some((a, b)) = s.split_once(',') else {\n        return Err(String::from(\"no comma\"));\n    };\n    // parse a and b\n    Ok((0, 0))\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_point(\"3,4\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_point(\"3;4\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_point(\"3,x\"));\n}","solution":"fn parse_point(s: &str) -> Result<(i32, i32), String> {\n    let Some((a, b)) = s.split_once(',') else {\n        return Err(String::from(\"no comma\"));\n    };\n    let x: i32 = a.trim().parse().map_err(|_| format!(\"\\\"{a}\\\" is not a number\"))?;\n    let y: i32 = b.trim().parse().map_err(|_| format!(\"\\\"{b}\\\" is not a number\"))?;\n    Ok((x, y))\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_point(\"3,4\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_point(\"3;4\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_point(\"3,x\"));\n}","expect":"Ok((3, 4))\nErr(\"no comma\")\nErr(\"\\\"x\\\" is not a number\")","hints":["The `parse` error has a different type; turn it into a `String` with `map_err`.","`let x: i32 = a.trim().parse().map_err(|_| format!(\"\\\"{a}\\\" is not a number\"))?;`"]},{"t":"task","md":"`?` also works with `Option`. Write a function that returns the first letter of the second word, or `None`.","code":"fn second_word_initial(s: &str) -> Option<char> {\n    None\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", second_word_initial(\"learn rust\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", second_word_initial(\"single\"));\n}","solution":"fn second_word_initial(s: &str) -> Option<char> {\n    let word = s.split_whitespace().nth(1)?;\n    word.chars().next()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", second_word_initial(\"learn rust\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", second_word_initial(\"single\"));\n}","expect":"Some('r')\nNone","hints":["`s.split_whitespace().nth(1)` gives the second word as `Option<&str>`. Put `?` after it.","The first letter: `word.chars().next()` — this is already an `Option<char>`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Expected problems use `Result`, not a panic.","`?` passes an error up, `map_err` changes its type.","`if let Ok(x)` handles only success."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Return `Result` from your own functions","Use `?` and `map_err`","Handle errors without panicking"]}]},{"id":"m11","title":"Modules and Cargo","sub":"How a real project is organized","lessons":[{"id":"m11-1","title":"Modules: mod, pub, use","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A module is a folder for code","md":"A big program is split into **modules** (`mod`). Analogy: drawers in a cabinet — tools in one, documents in another.\n\n```\nmod math {\n    pub fn double(x: i32) -> i32 {\n        x * 2\n    }\n    fn secret() {} // visible only inside math\n}\n```"},{"t":"text","title":"pub — open to the outside","md":"By default everything inside a module is **closed**. To use it from outside, mark it `pub` (public).\n\nYou access it with `::` — `math::double(5)`.\n\n`use` creates a short name: after `use math::double;` you can just write `double(5)`."},{"t":"code","code":"mod shop {\n    pub struct Item {\n        pub name: String,\n        price: u32,\n    }\n\n    impl Item {\n        pub fn new(name: &str, price: u32) -> Self {\n            Item { name: name.to_string(), price }\n        }\n        pub fn price_with_tax(&self) -> u32 {\n            self.price * 120 / 100\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nuse shop::Item;\n\nfn main() {\n    let it = Item::new(\"tea\", 100);\n    println!(\"{}: {} cents\", it.name, it.price_with_tax());\n}","out":"tea: 120 cents","after":"The field `price` is closed: it cannot be read or broken from outside. Access is only through a method."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What happens if you call `math::secret()` from `main`?","options":["It works","An error: the function is private"],"answer":1,"why":["`secret` has no `pub`, so it is closed.","Correct. Without `pub` it is only for the module itself."]},{"t":"text","title":"Nested modules and paths","md":"Modules can be nested inside each other. A path is written with `::`:\n\n```\nmod game {\n    pub mod score {\n        pub fn reset() -> u32 { 0 }\n    }\n}\n\nlet s = game::score::reset();\n```\nInside modules two words are useful:\n- `self::` means \"in this module\";\n- `super::` means \"in the module one level up\"."},{"t":"deep","title":"Visibility levels","md":"Besides plain `pub` there are more precise variants:\n- `pub(crate)` — visible in the whole crate, but not outside (not to other libraries);\n- `pub(super)` — visible only in the parent module;\n- no `pub` — only in this module and the modules nested inside it.\n\nStruct fields have their own visibility. A struct can be `pub` while its field is not. This is what happened with the field `price` in the example above."},{"t":"recap","items":["`mod name { }` is a module.","Everything is closed until it is marked `pub`.","`use path::name;` creates a short name."]}],"goals":["Organize code into modules","Decide what is visible from outside"]},{"id":"m11-2","title":"Installing Rust on your computer","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Time for your own computer","md":"Until now we ran code in the browser. For real projects, Rust is installed on the computer.\n\nThe official installer is called **rustup**. It installs the compiler `rustc`, the project manager `cargo` and the documentation."},{"t":"text","title":"Windows","md":"1. Open [rust-lang.org/tools/install](https://www.rust-lang.org/tools/install).\n2. Download `rustup-init.exe` and run it.\n3. It offers to install Visual Studio Build Tools — agree. They are needed to build programs.\n4. Press Enter for the default settings.\n\n> Installation takes 5–15 minutes. A good moment for a break."},{"t":"text","title":"macOS and Linux","md":"Open a terminal and paste one command:\n\n```sh\ncurl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf https://sh.rustup.rs | sh\n```\nOn Linux you also need a C compiler: the package `build-essential` (Ubuntu) or a similar one."},{"t":"text","title":"Check that everything works","md":"Open a **new** terminal window and type:\n\n```sh\nrustc --version\ncargo --version\n```\nVersion numbers should appear. For example, `rustc 1.99.1`. New versions come out every 6 weeks. To update: `rustup update`."},{"t":"text","title":"An editor","md":"- **VS Code** + the **rust-analyzer** extension. Free. Highlighting, hints, errors right in the code.\n- **RustRover** by JetBrains. Free for non-commercial use.\n\n> rust-analyzer shows the types of variables in gray right in the code. For a beginner this helps a lot."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What is the official Rust installer called?","options":["rustup","cargo","npm"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct. It installs and updates the whole toolset.","`cargo` is installed together with Rust. It is the project manager.","That is the JavaScript package manager."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Install Rust with rustup from rust-lang.org.","Check: `rustc --version`; update: `rustup update`.","Editor: VS Code + rust-analyzer, or RustRover."]}],"goals":["Install Rust with rustup","Choose an editor"]},{"id":"m11-3","title":"Cargo: your first project","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Cargo is the project manager","md":"**Cargo** creates projects, builds them, runs them, downloads libraries and runs tests. Almost everything is done through it."},{"t":"text","title":"A new project","md":"```sh\ncargo new hello\ncd hello\ncargo run\n```\nYou will see `Hello, world!`. Cargo already wrote the program for you."},{"t":"text","title":"What is inside","md":"```sh\nhello/\n├── Cargo.toml     ← the project description\n└── src/\n    └── main.rs    ← your code\n```\n`Cargo.toml` looks like this:\n\n```sh\n[package]\nname = \"hello\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2024\"\n\n[dependencies]\n```\n`edition = \"2024\"` is the edition of the language. We use 2024 in the whole course."},{"t":"text","title":"The main commands","md":"- `cargo run` — build and run.\n- `cargo check` — only check for errors. Very fast.\n- `cargo build --release` — build the fast version for users.\n- `cargo test` — run the tests.\n- `cargo fmt` — format the code neatly.\n- `cargo clippy` — hints on how to write better code."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Which command shows compile errors the fastest, without running anything?","options":["`cargo run`","`cargo check`","`cargo build --release`"],"answer":1,"why":["It also runs the program. Slower.","Correct. Only checking, without building the program.","This is the longest build: with optimization."]},{"t":"text","title":"Debug and release","md":"Cargo builds a program in two modes:\n- **debug** (`cargo run`, `cargo build`) — builds fast, runs slower, checks for number overflow;\n- **release** (`cargo run --release`) — builds longer, runs many times faster.\n\nThe finished program is in the folder `target/debug` or `target/release`."},{"t":"deep","title":"Cargo.lock and versions","md":"Next to `Cargo.toml` a file `Cargo.lock` appears. It records the **exact** versions of all libraries the project was built with.\n\nLibrary versions follow the **SemVer** rule: `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`, for example `1.4.2`.\n- Patch (`1.4.3`) — fixes, nothing breaks.\n- Minor (`1.5.0`) — new features, old code still works.\n- Major (`2.0.0`) — there may be incompatible changes.\n\nThe entry `rand = \"0.9\"` in `Cargo.toml` means \"any version compatible with 0.9\". `cargo update` updates versions within this rule."},{"t":"recap","items":["`cargo new name` creates a new project.","The code is in `src/main.rs`, the settings in `Cargo.toml`.","`run`, `check`, `build --release`, `test`, `fmt`, `clippy`."]}],"goals":["Create a project with `cargo new`","Build and run it"]},{"id":"m11-4","title":"Crates and libraries","min":5,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A crate is a unit of code","md":"A **crate** is what gets built in one go: a program or a library.\n\nThousands of ready-made libraries are on the site [crates.io](https://crates.io). You do not have to write everything yourself."},{"t":"text","title":"Adding a library","md":"For example, the random numbers library `rand`. In the project folder:\n\n```sh\ncargo add rand\n```\nCargo adds it to `Cargo.toml` and downloads it during the build. Then in the code:\n\n```sh\nuse rand::Rng;\nlet n = rand::rng().random_range(1..=6);\n```\n!> Rust Playground knows only the most popular libraries. That is why the tasks of this course use only the standard library `std`."},{"t":"text","title":"Modules in separate files","md":"In a real project a module often lives in its own file. If you write `mod shop;` (with a semicolon) in `main.rs`, Cargo takes the code from `src/shop.rs`."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Where do you look for ready-made Rust libraries?","options":["crates.io","In Rust Playground","In Cargo.toml"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct. It is the official crate registry.","Playground only runs code. Libraries live on crates.io.","`Cargo.toml` only lists them; they live on crates.io."]},{"t":"recap","items":["A crate is a program or a library.","Libraries are on crates.io; add one with `cargo add name`.","`mod shop;` takes code from the file `src/shop.rs`."]}],"goals":["What a crate is","How to add someone else's library"]},{"id":"m11-p","title":"Practice: modules","min":10,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Create a module `math` with a public function `square`. In `main`, call it through the path.","code":"// the module math goes here\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", math::square(9));\n}","solution":"mod math {\n    pub fn square(x: i32) -> i32 {\n        x * x\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", math::square(9));\n}","expect":"81","hints":["`mod math { pub fn square(x: i32) -> i32 { x * x } }`","Without `pub` the function is not visible from `main`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Fix the visibility error. Do not change `main`.","code":"mod greetings {\n    fn hello() -> &'static str {\n        \"Hello\"\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", greetings::hello());\n}","solution":"mod greetings {\n    pub fn hello() -> &'static str {\n        \"Hello\"\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", greetings::hello());\n}","expect":"Hello","hints":["The compiler says: function `hello` is private.","Add `pub` before `fn hello`."]},{"t":"task","md":"A nested module. Add a `use` so that `main` can simply write `to_km(...)`.","code":"mod units {\n    pub mod length {\n        pub fn to_km(m: f64) -> f64 {\n            m / 1000.0\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", to_km(2500.0));\n}","solution":"mod units {\n    pub mod length {\n        pub fn to_km(m: f64) -> f64 {\n            m / 1000.0\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nuse units::length::to_km;\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", to_km(2500.0));\n}","expect":"2.5","hints":["The path to the function: `units::length::to_km`.","The line before `main`: `use units::length::to_km;`"]},{"t":"task","md":"A module `counter` with a closed field. Add a method `get` so that `main` can read the value but cannot break it.","code":"mod counter {\n    pub struct Counter {\n        value: u32,\n    }\n\n    impl Counter {\n        pub fn new() -> Self {\n            Counter { value: 0 }\n        }\n        pub fn inc(&mut self) {\n            self.value += 1;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut c = counter::Counter::new();\n    c.inc();\n    c.inc();\n    println!(\"{}\", c.get());\n}","solution":"mod counter {\n    pub struct Counter {\n        value: u32,\n    }\n\n    impl Counter {\n        pub fn new() -> Self {\n            Counter { value: 0 }\n        }\n        pub fn inc(&mut self) {\n            self.value += 1;\n        }\n        pub fn get(&self) -> u32 {\n            self.value\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut c = counter::Counter::new();\n    c.inc();\n    c.inc();\n    println!(\"{}\", c.get());\n}","expect":"2","hints":["The method only reads — `&self`. And it must be `pub`.","`pub fn get(&self) -> u32 { self.value }`"]},{"t":"quiz","md":"Which command builds the fast version of a program for users?","options":["`cargo run`","`cargo check`","`cargo build --release`"],"answer":2,"why":["This is debug mode: it builds fast but runs slower.","`check` builds nothing; it only checks.","Correct. The finished file appears in `target/release`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Everything in a module is closed until it is marked `pub`.","`use path;` shortens long paths.","Closed fields + public methods protect data from being broken."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Organize code into modules","Fix visibility errors"]}]},{"id":"m12","title":"Generics and traits","sub":"One piece of code for many types","lessons":[{"id":"m12-1","title":"Generics: <T>","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Do not repeat yourself","md":"Suppose you need a function \"first element\" for a list of numbers. Then for a list of strings. Then for a list of fractional numbers. Three identical functions?\n\nNo. We write one **generic** function with a type parameter `T`:\n\n```\nfn first<T>(list: &[T]) -> &T {\n    &list[0]\n}\n```\n`<T>` reads as \"for any type T\"."},{"t":"code","code":"fn first<T>(list: &[T]) -> &T {\n    &list[0]\n}\n\nstruct Pair<T> {\n    a: T,\n    b: T,\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", first(&[3, 1, 2]));\n    println!(\"{}\", first(&[\"cat\", \"dog\"]));\n    let p = Pair { a: 1.5, b: 2.5 };\n    println!(\"{} {}\", p.a, p.b);\n}","out":"3\ncat\n1.5 2.5","after":"You have already met generics: `Vec<T>`, `Option<T>`, `Result<T, E>`."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `T` mean in `Vec<T>`?","options":["The type \"text\"","The type of the elements, any type","The number of elements"],"answer":1,"why":["It is not text, it is \"some type\".","Correct. `Vec<i32>` and `Vec<String>` are the same `Vec<T>`.","The number is the length, and `T` is the type."]},{"t":"deep","title":"Generics cost nothing","md":"When you call `first(&[3, 1, 2])` and `first(&[\"cat\"])`, the compiler creates **two separate copies** of the function: one for `i32`, another for `&str`. This is called **monomorphization**.\n\nThat is why generic code runs as fast as if you had written two functions by hand. The price: the program builds a bit longer, and the file is a bit bigger."},{"t":"recap","items":["`<T>` is a type parameter: \"any type\".","It works for functions, structs, enums.","`Vec<T>` and `Option<T>` are generics too."]}],"goals":["Write functions for any type","Read the notation `<T>`"]},{"id":"m12-2","title":"Traits are skills of a type","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A trait is a skill certificate","md":"A **trait** describes a **skill**: which methods a type must have.\n\n```\ntrait Speak {\n    fn speak(&self) -> String;\n}\n```\n> Analogy: a driving license. It does not matter who you are — if you have a license, you can drive. The trait `Speak` means \"can speak\"."},{"t":"code","code":"trait Speak {\n    fn speak(&self) -> String;\n\n    fn greet(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"I say: {}\", self.speak())\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Dog;\nstruct Cat;\n\nimpl Speak for Dog {\n    fn speak(&self) -> String {\n        \"Woof\".to_string()\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Speak for Cat {\n    fn speak(&self) -> String {\n        \"Meow\".to_string()\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Dog.greet());\n    println!(\"{}\", Cat.greet());\n}","out":"I say: Woof\nI say: Meow","after":"`greet` is a **default** method: it is written right in the trait. The types only need to implement `speak`."},{"t":"fill","explain":"The pattern: `impl Trait for Type { ... }`.","md":"Complete the trait implementation for the type `Robot`.","code":"___ Speak ___ Robot {\n    fn speak(&self) -> String {\n        \"Beep\".to_string()\n    }\n}","answers":[["impl"],["for"]]},{"t":"deep","title":"Whose trait and whose type","md":"There is a rule about **what** you may implement a trait for. An implementation `impl Trait for Type` is allowed if either the **trait** or the **type** is yours:\n- your trait for your type — allowed;\n- your trait for someone else's type (for example `impl Speak for i32`) — allowed;\n- someone else's trait for your type (for example `impl Display for Dog`) — allowed;\n- someone else's trait for someone else's type (`impl Display for Vec<i32>`) — **not allowed**.\n\nThis rule is called the **orphan rule**. It prevents two libraries from writing two different implementations of the same thing."},{"t":"recap","items":["`trait Name { fn method(&self); }` describes a skill.","`impl Trait for Type { ... }` teaches a type.","A trait can have default methods."]}],"goals":["What a trait is","How to implement a trait for your own type"]},{"id":"m12-3","title":"A trait as a requirement","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Not any type, but a skilled one","md":"A generic function often needs `T` to be **able** to do something. For example, to be compared.\n\n```\nfn largest<T: PartialOrd>(list: &[T]) -> &T { ... }\n```\n`T: PartialOrd` means \"any T that can be compared with `<` and `>`\". This is called a **trait bound**."},{"t":"code","code":"fn largest<T: PartialOrd>(list: &[T]) -> &T {\n    let mut best = &list[0];\n    for item in list {\n        if item > best {\n            best = item;\n        }\n    }\n    best\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", largest(&[3, 9, 4]));\n    println!(\"{}\", largest(&['y', 'b', 'z']));\n}","out":"9\nz"},{"t":"text","title":"impl Trait — shorter","md":"If a type is needed only once, there is a shortcut:\n\n```\nfn announce(x: &impl Speak) {\n    println!(\"{}\", x.speak());\n}\n```\nThis is the same as `fn announce<T: Speak>(x: &T)`.\n\nA long list of requirements is conveniently moved into `where`:\n\n```\nfn show<T>(x: T) where T: std::fmt::Debug + Clone { ... }\n```"},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `T: PartialOrd` mean?","options":["T is a number","T must be comparable","T can be copied"],"answer":1,"why":["Not necessarily a number. Letters and strings can also be compared.","Correct. It is a requirement on the type.","Copying is the trait `Copy` or `Clone`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Write a function `print_all` that prints each element of a slice with `{:?}`. Any `T` that can do `Debug` fits.","code":"use std::fmt::Debug;\n\n// write print_all\n\nfn main() {\n    print_all(&[1, 2]);\n    print_all(&[\"a\", \"b\"]);\n}","solution":"use std::fmt::Debug;\n\nfn print_all<T: Debug>(items: &[T]) {\n    for x in items {\n        println!(\"{:?}\", x);\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    print_all(&[1, 2]);\n    print_all(&[\"a\", \"b\"]);\n}","expect":"1\n2\n\"a\"\n\"b\"","hints":["The header: `fn print_all<T: Debug>(items: &[T])`","Inside, the loop `for x in items { println!(\"{:?}\", x); }`"]},{"t":"deep","title":"impl Trait in the return type","md":"`impl Trait` can also be written in the result type. This way a function says: \"I will return something that can do this. Which exact type is my business\".\n\nThe real type here is long and awkward. `impl Iterator` hides it. Iterators are in module 14.","code":"fn evens() -> impl Iterator<Item = u32> {\n    (0..10).filter(|n| n % 2 == 0)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for n in evens() {\n        print!(\"{n} \");\n    }\n    println!();\n}","out":"0 2 4 6 8"},{"t":"recap","items":["`<T: Trait>` means T must be able to do this.","`impl Trait` is the short form.","`where` is for long lists of requirements."]}],"goals":["Require a skill from `T`: `T: Trait`","The short form `impl Trait`"]},{"id":"m12-4","title":"Useful traits of the standard library","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"derive — traits for free","md":"The compiler can write many traits by itself:\n\n```\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Default)]\nstruct Point { x: i32, y: i32 }\n```\n- `Debug` — printing with `{:?}`;\n- `Clone` — the method `.clone()`;\n- `PartialEq` — comparing with `==`;\n- `Default` — a \"default\" value (zeros)."},{"t":"code","md":"`Display` — printing with `{}` — is written by hand. You decide how the type looks.","code":"use std::fmt;\n\nstruct Money {\n    dollars: u32,\n    cents: u32,\n}\n\nimpl fmt::Display for Money {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter) -> fmt::Result {\n        write!(f, \"${}.{:02}\", self.dollars, self.cents)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let price = Money { dollars: 99, cents: 5 };\n    println!(\"Price: {price}\");\n}","out":"Price: $99.05","after":"`{:02}` means \"at least two digits, with zeros on the left\"."},{"t":"text","title":"From and Into — conversions","md":"The trait `From` describes how to make one type from another.\n\n```\nlet s = String::from(\"text\");    // String from &str\nlet n: i64 = i64::from(5i32);    // i64 from i32\nlet s2: String = \"more\".into();  // into is the same From, from the other side\n```\nIf you implemented `From<A> for B`, then `into()` appears on `A` automatically."},{"t":"text","title":"dyn Trait — different types in one list","md":"You can put **different** types into one vector if they share a trait:\n\n```\nlet animals: Vec<Box<dyn Speak>> = vec![Box::new(Dog), Box::new(Cat)];\nfor a in &animals {\n    println!(\"{}\", a.speak());\n}\n```\n`dyn` means \"some type with this skill\". `Box` is explained in the module about smart pointers."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Which trait is needed to print a type with `{}`?","options":["`Debug`","`Display`","`Clone`"],"answer":1,"why":["`Debug` is for `{:?}`.","Correct. It is implemented by hand.","`Clone` is about copying, not printing."]},{"t":"deep","title":"Your own operators: the Add trait","md":"The `+` sign in Rust is a call to a method of the trait `std::ops::Add`. If you implement it for your type, `+` works for it too:\n\n`-` (`Sub`), `*` (`Mul`), `==` (`PartialEq`), `<` (`PartialOrd`) work the same way.","code":"use std::ops::Add;\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]\nstruct Vec2 {\n    x: i32,\n    y: i32,\n}\n\nimpl Add for Vec2 {\n    type Output = Vec2;\n    fn add(self, other: Vec2) -> Vec2 {\n        Vec2 { x: self.x + other.x, y: self.y + other.y }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a = Vec2 { x: 1, y: 2 };\n    let b = Vec2 { x: 10, y: 20 };\n    println!(\"{:?}\", a + b);\n}","out":"Vec2 { x: 11, y: 22 }"},{"t":"recap","items":["`#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Default)]` gives traits in one line.","`Display` is written by hand to print with `{}`.","`From`/`Into` convert types.","`Box<dyn Trait>` holds different types with a shared skill."]}],"goals":["Display: printing with `{}`","derive: traits in one line","From/Into: type conversions"]},{"id":"m12-p","title":"Practice: generics and traits","min":14,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Write a generic function `swap_pair` that swaps two values of any type.","code":"// write swap_pair\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", swap_pair(1, 2));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", swap_pair(\"a\", \"b\"));\n}","solution":"fn swap_pair<T>(a: T, b: T) -> (T, T) {\n    (b, a)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", swap_pair(1, 2));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", swap_pair(\"a\", \"b\"));\n}","expect":"(2, 1)\n(\"b\", \"a\")","hints":["The header: `fn swap_pair<T>(a: T, b: T) -> (T, T)`","The body: `(b, a)`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Describe a trait `Area` with a method `area(&self) -> f64` and implement it for `Square` and `Circle` (the area of a circle: `3.14 × r × r`).","code":"struct Square(f64);\nstruct Circle(f64);\n\n// the trait Area and two implementations\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Square(3.0).area());\n    println!(\"{}\", Circle(2.0).area());\n}","solution":"struct Square(f64);\nstruct Circle(f64);\n\ntrait Area {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64;\n}\n\nimpl Area for Square {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64 {\n        self.0 * self.0\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Area for Circle {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64 {\n        3.14 * self.0 * self.0\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Square(3.0).area());\n    println!(\"{}\", Circle(2.0).area());\n}","expect":"9\n12.56","hints":["`trait Area { fn area(&self) -> f64; }`","`impl Area for Square { fn area(&self) -> f64 { self.0 * self.0 } }` — and the same for `Circle`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Implement `Display` for a point so that it prints as `(3, -1)`.","code":"use std::fmt;\n\nstruct Point {\n    x: i32,\n    y: i32,\n}\n\n// impl fmt::Display for Point\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = Point { x: 3, y: -1 };\n    println!(\"Point {p}\");\n}","solution":"use std::fmt;\n\nstruct Point {\n    x: i32,\n    y: i32,\n}\n\nimpl fmt::Display for Point {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter) -> fmt::Result {\n        write!(f, \"({}, {})\", self.x, self.y)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = Point { x: 3, y: -1 };\n    println!(\"Point {p}\");\n}","expect":"Point (3, -1)","hints":["The method header: `fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter) -> fmt::Result`","The body: `write!(f, \"({}, {})\", self.x, self.y)`"]},{"t":"task","md":"A trait with a default method. Implement `Greet` for `Robot` by giving only `name`. The method `hello` is already written in the trait.","code":"trait Greet {\n    fn name(&self) -> String;\n    fn hello(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"Hello, I am {}!\", self.name())\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Robot;\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Robot.hello());\n}","solution":"trait Greet {\n    fn name(&self) -> String;\n    fn hello(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"Hello, I am {}!\", self.name())\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Robot;\n\nimpl Greet for Robot {\n    fn name(&self) -> String {\n        String::from(\"Robot\")\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Robot.hello());\n}","expect":"Hello, I am Robot!","hints":["`impl Greet for Robot { fn name(&self) -> String { ... } }`","Inside: `String::from(\"Robot\")`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Implement `From<(i32, i32)> for Point` so that a point can be made from a tuple with `.into()`.","code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Point {\n    x: i32,\n    y: i32,\n}\n\n// impl From<(i32, i32)> for Point\n\nfn main() {\n    let p: Point = (5, 7).into();\n    println!(\"{:?}\", p);\n}","solution":"#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Point {\n    x: i32,\n    y: i32,\n}\n\nimpl From<(i32, i32)> for Point {\n    fn from(t: (i32, i32)) -> Self {\n        Point { x: t.0, y: t.1 }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let p: Point = (5, 7).into();\n    println!(\"{:?}\", p);\n}","expect":"Point { x: 5, y: 7 }","hints":["The method of the trait `From` is called `from`: `fn from(t: (i32, i32)) -> Self`","The body: `Point { x: t.0, y: t.1 }`. The method `into` appears automatically."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`<T>` is a function for any type, `<T: Trait>` for types with a skill.","`trait` + `impl Trait for Type` teach a type something new.","`Display` prints with `{}`, `From` converts types."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Write generic functions","Describe and implement traits","Implement `Display` and `From`"]}]},{"id":"m13","title":"Lifetimes","sub":"How long a reference lives","lessons":[{"id":"m13-1","title":"Why lifetimes are needed","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Remember dangling references","md":"We already know: a reference cannot outlive its value.\n\nUsually the compiler sees by itself how long everything lives. But sometimes it **cannot tell**. Then it asks for a hint — a **lifetime label**: `'a`.\n\n!> A label does not extend anything. It only **describes** a connection: \"this reference lives no longer than that one\"."},{"t":"code","fails":true,"md":"The function returns a reference to one of two strings. Run it.","code":"fn longest(a: &str, b: &str) -> &str {\n    if a.len() > b.len() { a } else { b }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", longest(\"cat\", \"hippopotamus\"));\n}","after":"Error `E0106`: \"missing lifetime specifier\". The compiler does not know **which** of the two strings the result depends on."},{"t":"text","title":"Giving the compiler a hint","md":"```\nfn longest<'a>(a: &'a str, b: &'a str) -> &'a str {\n    if a.len() > b.len() { a } else { b }\n}\n```\nRead it like this: \"there is a lifetime `'a`. Both strings live at least `'a`. The result also lives `'a`\".\n\nSimpler: **the result lives as long as the shorter-living of the two strings**."},{"t":"code","code":"fn longest<'a>(a: &'a str, b: &'a str) -> &'a str {\n    if a.len() > b.len() { a } else { b }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let first = String::from(\"cat\");\n    let result;\n    {\n        let second = String::from(\"hippopotamus\");\n        result = longest(&first, &second);\n        println!(\"Longer: {result}\");\n    }\n}","out":"Longer: hippopotamus","after":"Try moving `println!` after the closing `}`. The compiler does not allow it: `second` is already gone, and `result` may point to it."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does the label `'a` do in `fn f<'a>(x: &'a str) -> &'a str`?","options":["It makes `x` live longer","It says: the result lives no longer than `x`","It makes the reference mutable"],"answer":1,"why":["Labels do not extend anything.","Correct. It is a description of a connection for the compiler.","Mutability is about `mut`."]},{"t":"deep","title":"How the compiler thinks about lifetimes","md":"For each reference the compiler finds the **part of the code** where it is used: from creation to the last use. For each value — the part where it is alive.\n\nThen it checks one rule: the part of the reference must fit **entirely** inside the part of the value.\n\nThe label `'a` is a name for such a part. In `longest<'a>` we say: \"the result is used only where both strings are alive\". The compiler checks this at every call."},{"t":"recap","items":["A lifetime is how long a reference lives.","The label `'a` describes a connection between references; it does not extend life.","The compiler asks for labels when it cannot work out the connection by itself."]}],"goals":["What the lifetime of a reference is","Why the compiler sometimes asks for `'a`"]},{"id":"m13-2","title":"When labels are not needed","min":5,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"The compiler often guesses by itself","md":"The guessing rules are called **elision**. The main cases:\n\n- One input reference → the result lives as long as it does.\n- A method with `&self` → the result lives as long as `self`.\n\nThat is why `fn first_word(s: &str) -> &str` works without labels. Labels are needed when there are **several** input references and it is unclear which one matters."},{"t":"text","title":"'static — lives for the whole program","md":"`'static` is a special lifetime: \"until the end of the program\".\n\nText in quotes is `&'static str`. It is stored right inside the program and always lives.\n\n```\nlet s: &'static str = \"I live forever\";\n```"},{"t":"quiz","md":"Does `fn trim(s: &str) -> &str` need a lifetime label?","options":["No, there is one input reference, the compiler guesses","Yes, always when a reference is returned"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct. This is an elision rule.","Only if the compiler cannot guess by itself."]},{"t":"text","title":"The three guessing rules","md":"In full, the elision rules are:\n1. Each input reference gets **its own** label.\n2. If there is **one** input reference, the result gets its label.\n3. If one of the inputs is `&self` or `&mut self`, the result gets the label of `self`.\n\nIf after these rules the result still has no label, the compiler asks you to write it by hand. That is error `E0106`."},{"t":"recap","items":["With one input reference or `&self`, labels are usually not needed.","`'static` lives for the whole program. Strings in quotes are `&'static str`."]}],"goals":["The rules the compiler uses to guess","What `'static` is"]},{"id":"m13-3","title":"References in structs","min":5,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A struct with a reference","md":"A struct may not own its data, but look at someone else's. Then it needs a label: the struct must not outlive what it looks at.\n\n```\nstruct Quote<'a> {\n    text: &'a str,\n}\n```"},{"t":"code","code":"struct Highlight<'a> {\n    part: &'a str,\n}\n\nimpl<'a> Highlight<'a> {\n    fn show(&self) {\n        println!(\"Key point: \\\"{}\\\"\", self.part);\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let book = String::from(\"Rust is reliable. And fast.\");\n    let first = book.split('.').next().unwrap();\n    let h = Highlight { part: first };\n    h.show();\n}","out":"Key point: \"Rust is reliable\""},{"t":"text","title":"Advice for beginners","md":"Not sure? Store a `String` in the struct, not a `&str`. The struct will own the data, and no labels are needed.\n\nReferences in structs are an optimization. You can come back to it later."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What is the simplest thing to do if labels in a struct are confusing?","options":["Store a `String` instead of a `&str`","Put `'static` everywhere","Delete the struct"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct. An owning type needs no labels.","Then only eternal strings could go into the struct. That is inconvenient.","Too radical. It is enough to change the field type."]},{"t":"deep","title":"'static in type requirements","md":"The requirement `T: 'static` on a type does not mean \"lives forever\". It means \"**contains no** temporary references\". `String`, `i32`, `Vec<u8>` fit: they own their data.\n\nFor example, `thread::spawn` has this requirement: a thread can live as long as it wants, so it cannot take temporary references with it. Threads are in module 17."},{"t":"recap","items":["A struct with a reference: `struct S<'a> { x: &'a str }`.","A struct cannot outlive the data it looks at.","Storing a `String` instead of a `&str` is an easy way out."]}],"goals":["Store a reference inside a struct","Understand the notation `struct Name<'a>`"]},{"id":"m13-p","title":"Practice: lifetimes","min":10,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Fix error `E0106`: add a lifetime label.","code":"fn shorter(a: &str, b: &str) -> &str {\n    if a.len() < b.len() { a } else { b }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", shorter(\"cat\", \"hippopotamus\"));\n}","solution":"fn shorter<'a>(a: &'a str, b: &'a str) -> &'a str {\n    if a.len() < b.len() { a } else { b }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", shorter(\"cat\", \"hippopotamus\"));\n}","expect":"cat","hints":["Declare the label after the function name: `fn shorter<'a>(...)`.","`fn shorter<'a>(a: &'a str, b: &'a str) -> &'a str`"]},{"t":"task","md":"The function always returns the first string. So the second string can live as long as it wants. Describe this with two different labels.","code":"fn pick_first(a: &str, b: &str) -> &str {\n    println!(\"Second: {b}\");\n    a\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let x = String::from(\"first\");\n    let r;\n    {\n        let y = String::from(\"second\");\n        r = pick_first(&x, &y);\n    }\n    println!(\"{r}\");\n}","solution":"fn pick_first<'a, 'b>(a: &'a str, b: &'b str) -> &'a str {\n    println!(\"Second: {b}\");\n    a\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let x = String::from(\"first\");\n    let r;\n    {\n        let y = String::from(\"second\");\n        r = pick_first(&x, &y);\n    }\n    println!(\"{r}\");\n}","expect":"Second: second\nfirst","hints":["Two labels: `fn pick_first<'a, 'b>(a: &'a str, b: &'b str) -> &'a str`","The result is connected only to `a`, so `y` may die before `r` is used."]},{"t":"task","md":"A struct with a reference. Complete the method `word_count` that counts the words in the text.","code":"struct Note<'a> {\n    text: &'a str,\n}\n\nimpl<'a> Note<'a> {\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let s = String::from(\"references in structs work\");\n    let n = Note { text: &s };\n    println!(\"{}\", n.word_count());\n}","solution":"struct Note<'a> {\n    text: &'a str,\n}\n\nimpl<'a> Note<'a> {\n    fn word_count(&self) -> usize {\n        self.text.split_whitespace().count()\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let s = String::from(\"references in structs work\");\n    let n = Note { text: &s };\n    println!(\"{}\", n.word_count());\n}","expect":"4","hints":["The method only reads: `fn word_count(&self) -> usize`.","`self.text.split_whitespace().count()`"]},{"t":"quiz","md":"Which function **needs** an explicit lifetime label?","options":["`fn len(s: &str) -> usize`","`fn first(s: &str) -> &str`","`fn pick(a: &str, b: &str) -> &str`"],"answer":2,"why":["The result is not a reference. No label is needed.","There is one input reference; the compiler guesses by itself.","Correct. Two input references — it is unclear which one the result is connected to."]},{"t":"recap","items":["E0106 — add a label `'a` and connect the input and the result with it.","Different labels mean the references live independently.","A struct with a reference: `struct S<'a>` and `impl<'a> S<'a>`."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Fix error E0106","Store references in structs"]}]},{"id":"m14","title":"Closures and iterators","sub":"A conveyor belt for data","lessons":[{"id":"m14-1","title":"Closures","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A function made on the spot","md":"A **closure** is a small function without a name that you write right where you need it. The parameters go between bars `| |`.\n\n```\nlet double = |x| x * 2;\nprintln!(\"{}\", double(5)); // 10\n```\nYou usually do not need to write the types: the compiler works them out."},{"t":"text","title":"A closure sees the variables around it","md":"Unlike `fn`, a closure **captures** variables from its surroundings:\n\n```\nlet bonus = 10;\nlet add_bonus = |x| x + bonus;\nprintln!(\"{}\", add_bonus(5)); // 15\n```\n> Analogy: a note with the address already written on it. You do not have to pass the address every time."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut count = 0;\n    let mut click = || {\n        count += 1;\n        println!(\"Click number {count}\");\n    };\n    click();\n    click();\n    println!(\"Total: {count}\");\n}","out":"Click number 1\nClick number 2\nTotal: 2","after":"The closure changes `count`, so the closure itself must be `mut`."},{"t":"text","title":"move — take it along","md":"The word `move` before a closure makes it **take** the variables into ownership instead of borrowing them.\n\n```\nlet name = String::from(\"Anna\");\nlet hello = move || println!(\"Hello, {name}\");\n```\nThis is needed in the module about threads."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `let f = |a, b| a * b; println!(\"{}\", f(3, 4));` print?","options":["7","12","An error: the closure has no name"],"answer":1,"why":["`*` is multiplication, not addition.","Correct.","It does not need one: we put it into the variable `f`."]},{"t":"deep","title":"Three kinds of closures","md":"The compiler sorts closures into three kinds by what they do with the captured variables:\n- `Fn` — only **reads**. It can be called any number of times.\n- `FnMut` — **changes**. The closure itself must be `mut`.\n- `FnOnce` — **takes** ownership. It can be called only once.\n\nA function can require the kind it needs:","code":"fn twice<F: Fn()>(f: F) {\n    f();\n    f();\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let name = String::from(\"Rust\");\n    twice(|| println!(\"Hello, {name}\"));\n}","out":"Hello, Rust\nHello, Rust"},{"t":"recap","items":["`|parameters| expression` is a closure.","A closure sees the variables around it.","`move` takes them into ownership."]}],"goals":["What a closure is","How it \"captures\" variables"]},{"id":"m14-2","title":"Iterators and the conveyor belt","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"An iterator is a conveyor belt","md":"An **iterator** gives out elements one by one. You can attach operations to it.\n\n> Analogy: a conveyor belt. Items ride on it; along the way they are washed (`map`), rejected (`filter`), and at the end they are packed into a box (`collect`).\n\n```\nlet v = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5];\nlet even_squares: Vec<i32> = v.iter()\n    .filter(|x| *x % 2 == 0)\n    .map(|x| x * x)\n    .collect();\n// [4, 16]\n```"},{"t":"text","title":"The main operations","md":"- `map(|x| ...)` — transform each element.\n- `filter(|x| ...)` — keep the matching ones.\n- `collect()` — collect into a collection (usually a `Vec`).\n- `sum()`, `count()`, `max()`, `min()` — compute a total.\n- `enumerate()` — give each element a number.\n- `zip(other)` — walk over two lists in pairs."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    let prices = vec![120, 45, 300, 80];\n    let total: i32 = prices.iter().sum();\n    let cheap = prices.iter().filter(|p| **p < 100).count();\n    let with_tax: Vec<i32> = prices.iter().map(|p| p * 12 / 10).collect();\n    println!(\"Sum: {total}, cheap: {cheap}\");\n    println!(\"With tax: {:?}\", with_tax);\n    for (i, p) in prices.iter().enumerate() {\n        println!(\"{}. {p}\", i + 1);\n    }\n}","out":"Sum: 545, cheap: 2\nWith tax: [144, 54, 360, 96]\n1. 120\n2. 45\n3. 300\n4. 80","after":"`**p` is two dereferences. `iter()` gives references, and `filter` gives a reference to a reference. If the stars are confusing, write `|&&p| p < 100`, or just follow the compiler's hints."},{"t":"order","md":"Build the conveyor: the sum of the squares of the numbers from 1 to 3.","lines":["let total: i32 = (1..=3)","    .map(|x| x * x)","    .sum();"]},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `vec![1, 2, 3].iter().map(|x| x + 10).collect::<Vec<i32>>()` return?","options":["`[11, 12, 13]`","`[1, 2, 3]`","`36`"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct. `map` changes each element.","`map` does not just go through the elements; it transforms them.","That would be `sum()`, not `collect()`."]},{"t":"text","title":"More useful operations","md":"- `take(n)` takes the first `n`; `skip(n)` skips the first `n`.\n- `any(|x| ...)` — is there at least one matching element; `all(|x| ...)` — do all elements match.\n- `find(|x| ...)` — the first matching element, in an `Option`.\n- `position(|x| ...)` — the index of the first matching element, in an `Option`.\n- `fold(start, |acc, x| ...)` — fold everything into one value.\n\n```\nlet v = vec![3, 8, 1, 9];\nv.iter().any(|&x| x > 8);           // true\nv.iter().find(|&&x| x > 5);         // Some(&8)\nv.iter().fold(0, |acc, x| acc + x); // 21\n```"},{"t":"recap","items":["`.iter()` gives references to the elements.","`map`, `filter`, `enumerate`, `zip` work along the way.","`collect`, `sum`, `count`, `max` work at the end."]}],"goals":["What an iterator is","The main operations: `map`, `filter`, `collect`, `sum`"]},{"id":"m14-3","title":"Laziness and kinds of iteration","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"The conveyor does not move without a command","md":"Iterators are **lazy**. Until you ask for the result (`collect`, `sum`, `for`), nothing happens.\n\n```\nlet v = vec![1, 2, 3];\nv.iter().map(|x| println!(\"{x}\")); // prints nothing!\n```\nThe compiler even warns you: \"iterators are lazy and do nothing unless consumed\".\n\n> This saves work: if you take only the first 3 elements with `.take(3)`, the rest are not even processed."},{"t":"text","title":"Three ways to iterate","md":"- `iter()` — look: gives `&T`.\n- `iter_mut()` — change: gives `&mut T`.\n- `into_iter()` — take: gives `T`; the vector is gone afterwards.\n\n`for x in &v` is the same as `v.iter()`. `for x in v` is the same as `v.into_iter()`."},{"t":"task","md":"From a list of words, build a new vector: only the words longer than 3 letters, in capital letters.","code":"fn main() {\n    let words = vec![\"cat\", \"doggy\", \"ox\", \"parrot\"];\n    let result: Vec<String> = words\n        .iter()\n        // your conveyor\n        .collect();\n    println!(\"{:?}\", result);\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let words = vec![\"cat\", \"doggy\", \"ox\", \"parrot\"];\n    let result: Vec<String> = words\n        .iter()\n        .filter(|w| w.chars().count() > 3)\n        .map(|w| w.to_uppercase())\n        .collect();\n    println!(\"{:?}\", result);\n}","expect":"[\"DOGGY\", \"PARROT\"]","hints":["Count the length in letters with `w.chars().count()`. `len()` counts bytes, which differs for non-English letters.","`.filter(|w| w.chars().count() > 3).map(|w| w.to_uppercase())`"]},{"t":"deep","title":"Your own iterator","md":"Any type with a method `next` that returns an `Option` becomes an iterator. This is the trait `Iterator`:\n\nWrite one `next` — and get `map`, `filter`, `sum` and everything else for free.\n\nBy the way, iterator chains run as fast as a hand-written loop: the compiler turns them into a normal loop.","code":"struct Countdown(u32);\n\nimpl Iterator for Countdown {\n    type Item = u32;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u32> {\n        if self.0 == 0 {\n            None\n        } else {\n            self.0 -= 1;\n            Some(self.0 + 1)\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v: Vec<u32> = Countdown(3).collect();\n    println!(\"{:?}\", v);\n    println!(\"{}\", Countdown(4).map(|x| x * 10).sum::<u32>());\n}","out":"[3, 2, 1]\n100"},{"t":"recap","items":["Iterators are lazy: the work starts at `collect`/`sum`/`for`.","`iter` looks, `iter_mut` changes, `into_iter` takes."]}],"goals":["Why iterators are lazy","The difference between `iter`, `iter_mut`, `into_iter`"]},{"id":"m14-p","title":"Practice: iterators","min":14,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Calculate the sum of the squares of the odd numbers from 1 to 9 in one chain.","code":"fn main() {\n    let total: i32 = (1..=9)\n        // your chain\n        .sum();\n    println!(\"{total}\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let total: i32 = (1..=9)\n        .filter(|x| x % 2 == 1)\n        .map(|x| x * x)\n        .sum();\n    println!(\"{total}\");\n}","expect":"165","hints":["First `filter` for the odd ones, then `map` for the squares.","`.filter(|x| x % 2 == 1).map(|x| x * x)`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Find the first word longer than 5 letters. Use `find`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let words = [\"code\", \"ferrous\", \"crab\", \"compiler\"];\n    let long = words.iter().find(|w| true);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", long);\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let words = [\"code\", \"ferrous\", \"crab\", \"compiler\"];\n    let long = words.iter().find(|w| w.chars().count() > 5);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", long);\n}","expect":"Some(\"ferrous\")","hints":["The length in letters is `w.chars().count()`.","`.find(|w| w.chars().count() > 5)`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Check a list of marks: is there at least one 2, and are all marks positive. Print `true` and `true`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let marks = [5, 4, 2, 5, 3];\n    let has_two = false;\n    let all_positive = false;\n    println!(\"{has_two} {all_positive}\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let marks = [5, 4, 2, 5, 3];\n    let has_two = marks.iter().any(|&m| m == 2);\n    let all_positive = marks.iter().all(|&m| m > 0);\n    println!(\"{has_two} {all_positive}\");\n}","expect":"true true","hints":["`any` means \"at least one\", `all` means \"all\".","`marks.iter().any(|&m| m == 2)` and `marks.iter().all(|&m| m > 0)`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Calculate the product of the numbers from 1 to 6 with `fold`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let product = (1..=6).fold(0, |acc, x| acc);\n    println!(\"{product}\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let product = (1..=6).fold(1, |acc, x| acc * x);\n    println!(\"{product}\");\n}","expect":"720","hints":["For multiplication the start value must be `1`, not `0`.","`(1..=6).fold(1, |acc, x| acc * x)`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Write a function `above` that returns a vector of the numbers greater than `limit`. The closure inside `filter` must capture `limit`.","code":"fn above(nums: &[i32], limit: i32) -> Vec<i32> {\n    Vec::new()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", above(&[4, 10, 7, 1, 12], 6));\n}","solution":"fn above(nums: &[i32], limit: i32) -> Vec<i32> {\n    nums.iter().filter(|&&x| x > limit).copied().collect()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", above(&[4, 10, 7, 1, 12], 6));\n}","expect":"[10, 7, 12]","hints":["`nums.iter().filter(...)` gives references. `.copied()` turns `&i32` into `i32`.","`nums.iter().filter(|&&x| x > limit).copied().collect()`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Number the tasks starting from one with `enumerate` and collect the lines into a vector.","code":"fn main() {\n    let tasks = [\"buy bread\", \"learn Rust\"];\n    let lines: Vec<String> = Vec::new();\n    for l in &lines {\n        println!(\"{l}\");\n    }\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let tasks = [\"buy bread\", \"learn Rust\"];\n    let lines: Vec<String> = tasks\n        .iter()\n        .enumerate()\n        .map(|(i, t)| format!(\"{}. {}\", i + 1, t))\n        .collect();\n    for l in &lines {\n        println!(\"{l}\");\n    }\n}","expect":"1. buy bread\n2. learn Rust","hints":["`enumerate()` gives pairs `(number, element)`, numbered from zero.","`.map(|(i, t)| format!(\"{}. {}\", i + 1, t))`"]},{"t":"recap","items":["`filter` → `map` → `sum`/`collect` is the main conveyor.","`find`, `any`, `all`, `position` search and check.","`fold` folds everything into one value."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Build conveyors from `filter`, `map`, `sum`","Search with `find`, `any`, `all`","Write closures"]}]},{"id":"m15","title":"Tests","sub":"How to make sure code works","lessons":[{"id":"m15-1","title":"Your first test","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A test is an automatic check","md":"A **test** is a small function that checks another function. Write it once — and check a thousand times with one command.\n\n> Analogy: a pilot's checklist before takeoff. Boring, but it saves lives."},{"t":"text","title":"What a test looks like","md":"```\n#[test]\nfn two_plus_two() {\n    assert_eq!(add(2, 2), 4);\n}\n```\n- `#[test]` means \"this is a test\".\n- `assert_eq!(a, b)` means \"a must equal b\". If not — the test fails.\n- `assert!(condition)` means \"the condition must be true\"."},{"t":"code","tests":true,"md":"There is no `main` here. The \"Run\" button runs the tests.","code":"pub fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {\n    a + b\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n\n    #[test]\n    fn adds_positive() {\n        assert_eq!(add(2, 3), 5);\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn adds_negative() {\n        assert_eq!(add(-2, -3), -5);\n    }\n}","after":"The output says `test result: ok. 2 passed`. Change `a + b` to `a - b` and run it again: you will see the failure and exactly what did not match."},{"t":"text","title":"What mod tests is","md":"- `#[cfg(test)]` means \"build this module only when testing\". It does not go into the finished program.\n- `use super::*;` means \"take everything from the module above\". Otherwise the tests do not see `add`.\n\nOn your own computer, tests are run with the command `cargo test`."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `assert_eq!(x, 10)` do?","options":["It assigns 10 to `x`","It fails the test if `x` is not equal to 10","It prints `x`"],"answer":1,"why":["It is a check, not an assignment.","Correct.","It prints only on failure, to show the difference."]},{"t":"text","title":"More checks and your own messages","md":"- `assert_ne!(a, b)` means \"a must **not** equal b\".\n- You can add your own message to any check. It appears on failure:\n\n```\nassert!(age >= 18, \"the age {age} is less than 18\");\nassert_eq!(total, 100, \"the total must be 100\");\n```\nA good message saves time: you see right away what went wrong."},{"t":"recap","items":["`#[test] fn name() { ... }` is a test.","`assert_eq!(a, b)`, `assert!(condition)` are checks.","Tests go into `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` and run with `cargo test`."]}],"goals":["Write a test with `#[test]`","Check a result with `assert_eq!`"]},{"id":"m15-2","title":"Tests for errors","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"should_panic","md":"Sometimes a panic is the correct behavior. This is checked with the attribute `#[should_panic]`:\n\n```\n#[test]\n#[should_panic]\nfn divide_by_zero_panics() {\n    divide(1, 0);\n}\n```"},{"t":"text","title":"A test can return a Result","md":"Then you can use `?` inside it. An error means the test failed.\n\n```\n#[test]\nfn parses() -> Result<(), std::num::ParseIntError> {\n    let n: i32 = \"42\".parse()?;\n    assert_eq!(n, 42);\n    Ok(())\n}\n```"},{"t":"task","tests":true,"mustContain":["#[test]"],"explain":"The tests passed. This is how reliable programs are written: a function + checks.","md":"Write a function `is_adult(age: u32) -> bool` (adult means 18 or more) and two tests for it: for 20 and for 10.","code":"pub fn is_adult(age: u32) -> bool {\n    // your code\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n\n    // your tests\n}","solution":"pub fn is_adult(age: u32) -> bool {\n    age >= 18\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n\n    #[test]\n    fn twenty_is_adult() {\n        assert!(is_adult(20));\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn ten_is_not_adult() {\n        assert!(!is_adult(10));\n    }\n}","hints":["The function body is one expression: `age >= 18`.","A test: `#[test] fn twenty() { assert!(is_adult(20)); }`. For 10 — `assert!(!is_adult(10));`"]},{"t":"deep","title":"Tests in documentation and the tests folder","md":"Rust has two more kinds of tests.\n\n**Documentation tests.** A code example in a `///` comment is also run by `cargo test`. That way the documentation never goes out of date:\n\n```\n/// Doubles a number.\n///\n/// ```\n/// assert_eq!(my_crate::double(2), 4);\n/// ```\npub fn double(x: i32) -> i32 { x * 2 }\n```\n\n**Integration tests.** Files in the folder `tests/` next to `src/`. They see the library the same way other programs will see it: only the public functions.\n\nIn total: unit tests (`mod tests`) check the details, integration tests check the whole thing, documentation tests check that the examples are correct."},{"t":"recap","items":["`#[should_panic]` — the test expects a panic.","A test can return a `Result` and use `?`.","Write tests for normal cases and for edge cases: 0, empty, very many."]}],"goals":["Check that a function panics","Check a `Result` in tests"]},{"id":"m15-p","title":"Practice: tests","min":12,"cards":[{"t":"task","tests":true,"mustContain":["#[test]"],"md":"Write two tests for `max_of`: for `[3, 9, 2]` the answer is `9`, for `[-5, -1]` the answer is `-1`.","code":"pub fn max_of(items: &[i32]) -> i32 {\n    let mut best = items[0];\n    for &x in items {\n        if x > best {\n            best = x;\n        }\n    }\n    best\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n}","solution":"pub fn max_of(items: &[i32]) -> i32 {\n    let mut best = items[0];\n    for &x in items {\n        if x > best {\n            best = x;\n        }\n    }\n    best\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n\n    #[test]\n    fn positive() {\n        assert_eq!(max_of(&[3, 9, 2]), 9);\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn negative() {\n        assert_eq!(max_of(&[-5, -1]), -1);\n    }\n}","hints":["Each test is `#[test] fn name() { assert_eq!(...); }`","`assert_eq!(max_of(&[3, 9, 2]), 9);`"]},{"t":"task","tests":true,"md":"The tests are already written, but the function has a bug. Find the bug from the failure message and fix the function.","code":"pub fn average(items: &[f64]) -> f64 {\n    let mut sum = 0.0;\n    for x in items {\n        sum += x;\n    }\n    sum / 2.0\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n\n    #[test]\n    fn three_items() {\n        assert_eq!(average(&[1.0, 2.0, 6.0]), 3.0);\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn two_items() {\n        assert_eq!(average(&[4.0, 6.0]), 5.0);\n    }\n}","solution":"pub fn average(items: &[f64]) -> f64 {\n    let mut sum = 0.0;\n    for x in items {\n        sum += x;\n    }\n    sum / items.len() as f64\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n\n    #[test]\n    fn three_items() {\n        assert_eq!(average(&[1.0, 2.0, 6.0]), 3.0);\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn two_items() {\n        assert_eq!(average(&[4.0, 6.0]), 5.0);\n    }\n}","hints":["The test with two numbers passes, but the one with three does not. What in the function works only for two numbers?","Divide by the count: `sum / items.len() as f64`."]},{"t":"task","tests":true,"mustContain":["should_panic"],"md":"The function `first_char` panics on an empty string. Write a test with `#[should_panic]` that checks this.","code":"pub fn first_char(s: &str) -> char {\n    s.chars().next().expect(\"empty string\")\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n}","solution":"pub fn first_char(s: &str) -> char {\n    s.chars().next().expect(\"empty string\")\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n\n    #[test]\n    #[should_panic]\n    fn empty_panics() {\n        first_char(\"\");\n    }\n}","hints":["Two attributes in a row: `#[test]` and `#[should_panic]`.","Inside the test just call `first_char(\"\");`"]},{"t":"quiz","md":"A test failed with the message `left: 4, right: 5`. What does it mean?","options":["The function returned 4, and the test expected 5","The test has 4 errors out of 5","The test ran 4 times"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct. `left` is the first value in `assert_eq!`, `right` is the second.","It is not a number of errors, but the two compared values.","The numbers are the values that did not match."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Tests are written next to the code, in `#[cfg(test)] mod tests`.","The failure message shows `left` and `right` — this makes the bug easier to find.","`#[should_panic]` checks that a panic happened."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Write tests for your functions","Find bugs with tests"]}]},{"id":"m16","title":"Smart pointers","sub":"Box, Rc and RefCell","lessons":[{"id":"m16-1","title":"Box — a value in the heap","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A smart pointer","md":"A **pointer** is the address of a value in memory. A **smart** pointer is an address plus rules: who owns it, when to delete it.\n\nYou have already used smart pointers: `String` and `Vec` are like that too."},{"t":"text","title":"Box — a box in the storage room","md":"`Box<T>` puts a value into the heap, and you keep a small address.\n\n```\nlet b = Box::new(5);\nprintln!(\"{}\", b); // 5\n```\nWhen you need Box:\n- a type refers to itself (a tree, a list);\n- a value is very big and you do not want to copy it;\n- you need `Box<dyn Trait>` — different types in one list."},{"t":"code","md":"A type that contains itself. Without `Box` its size would be infinite.","code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nenum List {\n    Node(i32, Box<List>),\n    End,\n}\n\nuse List::{End, Node};\n\nfn sum(list: &List) -> i32 {\n    match list {\n        Node(value, next) => value + sum(next),\n        End => 0,\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let list = Node(1, Box::new(Node(2, Box::new(Node(3, Box::new(End))))));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", list);\n    println!(\"Sum: {}\", sum(&list));\n}","out":"Node(1, Node(2, Node(3, End)))\nSum: 6","after":"`sum` calls itself. This is called **recursion**."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why is there a `Box` in `Node(i32, Box<List>)`?","options":["To make the list faster","So the size of the type is known: a Box is just an address","So the list can be changed"],"answer":1,"why":["Speed has nothing to do with it.","Correct. Without Box the list would contain itself endlessly.","Changing needs `mut`, not Box."]},{"t":"deep","title":"How Box<dyn Trait> works","md":"`Box<dyn Trait>` stores **two** addresses:\n1. the address of the data itself in the heap;\n2. the address of the **method table** (vtable) for this type.\n\nWhen you call a method, the program looks in the table and finds the right function. This is called **dynamic dispatch**. It is a bit slower than a normal call, but it lets you keep different types in one list.\n\nA normal `Box` is 8 bytes, `Box<dyn ...>` is 16: two addresses.","code":"fn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", std::mem::size_of::<Box<i32>>());\n    println!(\"{}\", std::mem::size_of::<Box<dyn std::fmt::Debug>>());\n}","out":"8\n16"},{"t":"recap","items":["A smart pointer is an address plus ownership rules.","`Box::new(x)` puts `x` into the heap.","Box is needed for recursive types and `dyn Trait`."]}],"goals":["What a smart pointer is","When you need `Box`"]},{"id":"m16-2","title":"Rc — a shared owner","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Sometimes there are several owners","md":"The rule \"one owner\" sometimes gets in the way. For example, two rooms in a game use the same texture.\n\n`Rc<T>` (reference counting) allows **several owners**. It counts how many there are. When the last one leaves, the value is deleted.\n\n> Analogy: a shared TV in a dormitory. There is a counter of viewers. When the last one leaves, the TV is turned off."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::rc::Rc;\n\nfn main() {\n    let song = Rc::new(String::from(\"Favorite song\"));\n    println!(\"Owners: {}\", Rc::strong_count(&song));\n    let anna = Rc::clone(&song);\n    let bob = Rc::clone(&song);\n    println!(\"Owners: {}\", Rc::strong_count(&song));\n    drop(anna);\n    println!(\"Owners: {}\", Rc::strong_count(&song));\n    println!(\"{bob}\");\n}","out":"Owners: 1\nOwners: 3\nOwners: 2\nFavorite song","after":"`Rc::clone` does not copy the song; it only increases the counter. This is cheap. `drop` deletes a variable early."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `Rc::clone(&x)` do?","options":["Copies all the data","Adds one more owner of the same data","Deletes `x`"],"answer":1,"why":["No, the data is the same. Only the counter grows.","Correct.","The opposite: there are more owners."]},{"t":"deep","title":"Cycles and Weak","md":"If two values hold an `Rc` to each other, their counters never reach zero. The memory is never freed — this is a **leak**.\n\nFor such links there is `Weak` — a \"weak\" reference. It does not increase the main counter. You get the value from it with `.upgrade()`: it returns `Some` if the value is still alive, and `None` if it is not.\n\nA typical example: in a tree a parent holds its children with `Rc`, and a child holds its parent with `Weak`.","code":"use std::rc::{Rc, Weak};\n\nfn main() {\n    let strong = Rc::new(5);\n    let weak: Weak<i32> = Rc::downgrade(&strong);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", weak.upgrade());\n    drop(strong);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", weak.upgrade());\n}","out":"Some(5)\nNone"},{"t":"recap","items":["`Rc<T>` gives one value several owners.","`Rc::clone` is cheap: only counter +1.","`Rc` is read-only and works in one thread only."]}],"goals":["How to give a value several owners","What `Rc` counts"]},{"id":"m16-3","title":"RefCell — changing through shared access","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"The check happens at run time, not at compile time","md":"`Rc` only lets you read. What if several owners need to **change** the value?\n\n`RefCell<T>` moves the borrowing check from compile time **to run time**. The rule is the same: many readers or one writer. Break it — and you get a panic.\n\n- `.borrow()` — take for reading;\n- `.borrow_mut()` — take for writing."},{"t":"code","md":"A shared counter that two players change:","code":"use std::cell::RefCell;\nuse std::rc::Rc;\n\nfn main() {\n    let score = Rc::new(RefCell::new(0));\n    let player1 = Rc::clone(&score);\n    let player2 = Rc::clone(&score);\n\n    *player1.borrow_mut() += 10;\n    *player2.borrow_mut() += 5;\n\n    println!(\"Shared score: {}\", score.borrow());\n}","out":"Shared score: 15"},{"t":"code","md":"Now let us break the rule: two writers at the same time. Run it.","code":"use std::cell::RefCell;\n\nfn main() {\n    let data = RefCell::new(vec![1, 2, 3]);\n    let a = data.borrow_mut();\n    let b = data.borrow_mut();\n    println!(\"{:?} {:?}\", a, b);\n}","after":"The compiler allows it, but the program panics: `already borrowed`. This is the price of flexibility: the error is caught by running, not by the compiler."},{"t":"quiz","md":"When does `RefCell` check the borrowing rules?","options":["At compile time","While the program runs"],"answer":1,"why":["Normal references do that. RefCell checks later.","Correct. Breaking the rule is a panic."]},{"t":"text","title":"Cell — for simple values","md":"For `Copy` types (numbers, `bool`) there is the simpler `Cell`. It gives out no references; it just puts and takes a value:\n\n```\nuse std::cell::Cell;\nlet counter = Cell::new(0);\ncounter.set(counter.get() + 1);\n```\n`Cell` never panics: there are no references, so there is nothing to break."},{"t":"recap","items":["`RefCell<T>` allows changing through shared access, with the check at run time.","`Rc<RefCell<T>>` is a shared changeable value in one thread.","Try normal references first. Use RefCell when there is no other way."]}],"goals":["What interior mutability is","The combination `Rc<RefCell<T>>`"]},{"id":"m16-p","title":"Practice: smart pointers","min":14,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"A binary tree: a node has a number and two optional children. Complete the function `sum` that adds up all numbers of the tree.","code":"struct Node {\n    value: i32,\n    left: Option<Box<Node>>,\n    right: Option<Box<Node>>,\n}\n\nfn sum(node: &Option<Box<Node>>) -> i32 {\n    0\n}\n\nfn leaf(v: i32) -> Option<Box<Node>> {\n    Some(Box::new(Node { value: v, left: None, right: None }))\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let tree = Some(Box::new(Node { value: 1, left: leaf(2), right: leaf(3) }));\n    println!(\"{}\", sum(&tree));\n}","solution":"struct Node {\n    value: i32,\n    left: Option<Box<Node>>,\n    right: Option<Box<Node>>,\n}\n\nfn sum(node: &Option<Box<Node>>) -> i32 {\n    match node {\n        Some(n) => n.value + sum(&n.left) + sum(&n.right),\n        None => 0,\n    }\n}\n\nfn leaf(v: i32) -> Option<Box<Node>> {\n    Some(Box::new(Node { value: v, left: None, right: None }))\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let tree = Some(Box::new(Node { value: 1, left: leaf(2), right: leaf(3) }));\n    println!(\"{}\", sum(&tree));\n}","expect":"6","hints":["Check `node` with `match`: for `None` return 0.","`Some(n) => n.value + sum(&n.left) + sum(&n.right),`"]},{"t":"task","md":"A shared log. Two \"modules\" of a program write into one list through `Rc<RefCell<Vec<String>>>`. Complete the function `log`.","code":"use std::cell::RefCell;\nuse std::rc::Rc;\n\nfn log(journal: &Rc<RefCell<Vec<String>>>, msg: &str) {\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let journal = Rc::new(RefCell::new(Vec::new()));\n    let network = Rc::clone(&journal);\n    let disk = Rc::clone(&journal);\n    log(&network, \"network connected\");\n    log(&disk, \"disk ready\");\n    println!(\"{:?}\", journal.borrow());\n}","solution":"use std::cell::RefCell;\nuse std::rc::Rc;\n\nfn log(journal: &Rc<RefCell<Vec<String>>>, msg: &str) {\n    journal.borrow_mut().push(msg.to_string());\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let journal = Rc::new(RefCell::new(Vec::new()));\n    let network = Rc::clone(&journal);\n    let disk = Rc::clone(&journal);\n    log(&network, \"network connected\");\n    log(&disk, \"disk ready\");\n    println!(\"{:?}\", journal.borrow());\n}","expect":"[\"network connected\", \"disk ready\"]","hints":["To change what is inside a `RefCell`, you need `borrow_mut()`.","`journal.borrow_mut().push(msg.to_string());`"]},{"t":"task","md":"The program panics with `already borrowed`. Fix it: end the first borrow before the second one starts.","code":"use std::cell::RefCell;\n\nfn main() {\n    let data = RefCell::new(vec![1, 2, 3]);\n    let mut first = data.borrow_mut();\n    first.push(4);\n    let mut second = data.borrow_mut();\n    second.push(5);\n    drop(second);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", data.borrow());\n}","solution":"use std::cell::RefCell;\n\nfn main() {\n    let data = RefCell::new(vec![1, 2, 3]);\n    {\n        let mut first = data.borrow_mut();\n        first.push(4);\n    }\n    let mut second = data.borrow_mut();\n    second.push(5);\n    drop(second);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", data.borrow());\n}","expect":"[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]","hints":["A borrow ends when the variable `first` disappears.","Put the two lines with `first` into a separate block `{ ... }`, or write `drop(first);` after `push(4)`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Add up the areas of shapes of different types that are stored in one vector `Vec<Box<dyn Shape>>`.","code":"trait Shape {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64;\n}\n\nstruct Sq(f64);\nstruct Rect(f64, f64);\n\nimpl Shape for Sq {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64 { self.0 * self.0 }\n}\nimpl Shape for Rect {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64 { self.0 * self.1 }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let shapes: Vec<Box<dyn Shape>> = vec![Box::new(Sq(2.0)), Box::new(Rect(3.0, 5.0))];\n    let total = 0.0;\n    println!(\"{total}\");\n}","solution":"trait Shape {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64;\n}\n\nstruct Sq(f64);\nstruct Rect(f64, f64);\n\nimpl Shape for Sq {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64 { self.0 * self.0 }\n}\nimpl Shape for Rect {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64 { self.0 * self.1 }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let shapes: Vec<Box<dyn Shape>> = vec![Box::new(Sq(2.0)), Box::new(Rect(3.0, 5.0))];\n    let total: f64 = shapes.iter().map(|s| s.area()).sum();\n    println!(\"{total}\");\n}","expect":"19","hints":["Go through the vector and call `area()` on each shape.","`let total: f64 = shapes.iter().map(|s| s.area()).sum();`"]},{"t":"recap","items":["`Box` is for recursive types and `dyn Trait`.","`Rc<RefCell<T>>` is shared changeable data in one thread.","A `RefCell` borrow ends when its variable disappears."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Build recursive types with `Box`","Share data with `Rc` and `RefCell`","Store different types with `Box<dyn Trait>`"]}]},{"id":"m17","title":"Threads","sub":"Doing several things at the same time","lessons":[{"id":"m17-1","title":"Your first thread","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A thread is one more worker","md":"A **thread** is a separate line of execution. Several threads run at the same time, on different processor cores.\n\n> Analogy: several cooks in one kitchen. The work goes faster, but they must agree on who takes which knife.\n\nRust is famous for the phrase \"fearless concurrency\": it catches many thread bugs at compile time."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let handle = thread::spawn(|| {\n        for i in 1..=3 {\n            println!(\"helper: step {i}\");\n        }\n        42\n    });\n    println!(\"the main thread waits\");\n    let answer = handle.join().unwrap();\n    println!(\"the helper returned {answer}\");\n}","after":"- `thread::spawn(closure)` starts a thread.\n- `join()` waits for it to finish and takes the result.\n\nThe order of lines can change from run to run: the threads run at the same time."},{"t":"text","title":"move — give data to the thread","md":"A thread may live longer than the function that created it. So it cannot borrow a variable — it can only **take** it:\n\n```\nlet name = String::from(\"Anna\");\nlet h = thread::spawn(move || {\n    println!(\"Hello from the thread, {name}\");\n});\nh.join().unwrap();\n```\nWithout `move` there is error `E0373`."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why do you need `join()`?","options":["To start the thread","To wait until the thread finishes","To merge two threads into one"],"answer":1,"why":["`spawn` starts it. `join` waits.","Correct. Otherwise `main` may end earlier and cut the thread off.","The name is misleading, but no: it means \"wait\"."]},{"t":"deep","title":"Send and Sync — how the compiler watches threads","md":"The compiler knows which types can be given to another thread. For this there are two marker traits:\n- `Send` — a value can be **passed** to another thread;\n- `Sync` — a value can be **looked at** from several threads at once.\n\nAlmost all types are like this automatically. Exceptions: `Rc` is not `Send` (its counter is not protected), `RefCell` is not `Sync`. If you try to send an `Rc` into `thread::spawn`, you get a compile error, not a random failure at run time."},{"t":"recap","items":["`thread::spawn(|| ...)` starts a new thread.","`handle.join()` waits and returns the result.","`move` gives the variables to the thread."]}],"goals":["Run code in a separate thread","Wait for it with `join`"]},{"id":"m17-2","title":"Channels: threads write to each other","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A channel is a pneumatic tube","md":"A **channel** is a pipe between threads. Messages go in at one end and come out at the other.\n\n```\nlet (tx, rx) = mpsc::channel();\n```\n- `tx` is the transmitter; you send with it: `tx.send(x)`.\n- `rx` is the receiver; you receive with it: `rx.recv()`.\n\n`mpsc` means \"multiple producers, single consumer\"."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::sync::mpsc;\nuse std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let (tx, rx) = mpsc::channel();\n    thread::spawn(move || {\n        for fruit in [\"apple\", \"pear\", \"plum\"] {\n            tx.send(fruit).unwrap();\n        }\n    });\n    for got in rx {\n        println!(\"Got: {got}\");\n    }\n    println!(\"The channel is closed\");\n}","out":"Got: apple\nGot: pear\nGot: plum\nThe channel is closed","after":"The loop `for got in rx` ends by itself when the transmitter disappears together with its thread."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What is passed with `send`?","options":["Ownership of the value","A copy of the value","A reference to the value"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct. Once sent, you no longer have it. This way threads do not fight over data.","Not necessarily a copy: the value moves.","A reference could dangle. Rust sends the value itself."]},{"t":"text","title":"Several senders","md":"The transmitter `tx` can be cloned. Then several threads write into one channel:\n\n```\nlet tx2 = tx.clone();\nthread::spawn(move || tx2.send(\"from the second\").unwrap());\n```\nThe receiver ends the `for` loop when **all** transmitters are gone."},{"t":"recap","items":["`mpsc::channel()` creates a channel `(tx, rx)`.","`tx.send(x)` sends; `rx.recv()` or `for x in rx` receives.","Sending passes ownership."]}],"goals":["Pass data between threads through a channel"]},{"id":"m17-3","title":"Shared data: Arc and Mutex","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A lock on the door","md":"`Mutex` (mutual exclusion) lets threads to the data **one at a time**.\n\n> Analogy: the key to the bathroom in a cafe. You take the key and go in. The others wait until you return it.\n\n`lock()` takes the key. The key is returned automatically when the variable holding it disappears."},{"t":"text","title":"Arc — Rc for threads","md":"`Rc` cannot be passed between threads: its counter is not protected. For threads there is `Arc` — an **atomic** Rc.\n\nThe classic: `Arc<Mutex<T>>` — a shared changeable value for many threads."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};\nuse std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let counter = Arc::new(Mutex::new(0));\n    let mut handles = vec![];\n    for _ in 0..10 {\n        let c = Arc::clone(&counter);\n        handles.push(thread::spawn(move || {\n            let mut n = c.lock().unwrap();\n            *n += 1;\n        }));\n    }\n    for h in handles {\n        h.join().unwrap();\n    }\n    println!(\"Result: {}\", *counter.lock().unwrap());\n}","out":"Result: 10","after":"Ten threads, each one added 1. Without a Mutex the result could be smaller: threads would overwrite each other."},{"t":"text","title":"thread::scope — simpler for short tasks","md":"If threads are needed only inside one block, `thread::scope` lets them **borrow** data. `move` and `Arc` are not needed:\n\n```\nlet data = vec![1, 2, 3, 4];\nlet (left, right) = data.split_at(2);\nthread::scope(|s| {\n    s.spawn(|| println!(\"{}\", left.iter().sum::<i32>()));\n    s.spawn(|| println!(\"{}\", right.iter().sum::<i32>()));\n});\n```"},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why do threads need `Arc` instead of `Rc`?","options":["`Arc` is faster","The `Arc` counter is safely changed from different threads","`Rc` cannot be cloned"],"answer":1,"why":["The opposite: it is a bit slower. But it is safe.","Correct. The compiler does not let you send an `Rc` into a thread.","It can, but only in one thread."]},{"t":"deep","title":"Deadlock and other tools","md":"**Deadlock**: thread A holds lock 1 and waits for lock 2, while thread B holds lock 2 and waits for lock 1. Both wait forever. Rust does **not** protect against this. The rule: always take several locks in the same order.\n\nOther tools from `std::sync`:\n- `RwLock` — many readers **or** one writer. Useful when reading is frequent and writing is rare.\n- **Atomic numbers** (`AtomicUsize` and others) — a counter without a lock. Faster than `Mutex` for simple numbers.","code":"use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering};\nuse std::sync::Arc;\nuse std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let hits = Arc::new(AtomicUsize::new(0));\n    let handles: Vec<_> = (0..4).map(|_| {\n        let h = Arc::clone(&hits);\n        thread::spawn(move || {\n            for _ in 0..1000 {\n                h.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);\n            }\n        })\n    }).collect();\n    for t in handles {\n        t.join().unwrap();\n    }\n    println!(\"{}\", hits.load(Ordering::Relaxed));\n}","out":"4000"},{"t":"recap","items":["`Mutex` gives access one at a time; `lock()` takes the key.","`Arc` is a shared owner for threads.","`Arc<Mutex<T>>` is shared changeable data.","`thread::scope` runs threads that borrow data."]}],"goals":["Change one value from many threads","Why `Arc` and `Mutex` are needed"]},{"id":"m17-p","title":"Practice: threads","min":14,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Fix error `E0373`: the thread cannot borrow `name`.","code":"use std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let name = String::from(\"Rust\");\n    let h = thread::spawn(|| {\n        println!(\"Hello from the thread, {name}\");\n    });\n    h.join().unwrap();\n}","solution":"use std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let name = String::from(\"Rust\");\n    let h = thread::spawn(move || {\n        println!(\"Hello from the thread, {name}\");\n    });\n    h.join().unwrap();\n}","expect":"Hello from the thread, Rust","hints":["The compiler suggests: to force the closure to take ownership, use the `move` keyword.","`thread::spawn(move || { ... })`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Start three threads. Each one returns the square of its number (1, 2, 3). Add up the results in the main thread.","code":"use std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut handles = Vec::new();\n    for i in 1..=3 {\n        // start a thread that returns i * i\n    }\n    let mut total = 0;\n    for h in handles {\n        total += h.join().unwrap();\n    }\n    println!(\"{total}\");\n}","solution":"use std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut handles = Vec::new();\n    for i in 1..=3 {\n        handles.push(thread::spawn(move || i * i));\n    }\n    let mut total = 0;\n    for h in handles {\n        total += h.join().unwrap();\n    }\n    println!(\"{total}\");\n}","expect":"14","hints":["`handles.push(thread::spawn(move || i * i));`","`move` is needed so that each thread takes its own copy of `i`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Two threads send numbers into one channel. The main thread adds up everything that arrives.","code":"use std::sync::mpsc;\nuse std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let (tx, rx) = mpsc::channel();\n    let tx2 = tx.clone();\n    thread::spawn(move || {\n        for n in [1, 2, 3] {\n            tx.send(n).unwrap();\n        }\n    });\n    thread::spawn(move || {\n        for n in [10, 20] {\n            tx2.send(n).unwrap();\n        }\n    });\n    let mut sum = 0;\n    // receive all numbers\n    println!(\"{sum}\");\n}","solution":"use std::sync::mpsc;\nuse std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let (tx, rx) = mpsc::channel();\n    let tx2 = tx.clone();\n    thread::spawn(move || {\n        for n in [1, 2, 3] {\n            tx.send(n).unwrap();\n        }\n    });\n    thread::spawn(move || {\n        for n in [10, 20] {\n            tx2.send(n).unwrap();\n        }\n    });\n    let mut sum = 0;\n    for n in rx {\n        sum += n;\n    }\n    println!(\"{sum}\");\n}","expect":"36","hints":["The loop `for n in rx` receives messages while the transmitters are alive.","Inside: `sum += n;`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Five threads add their number to a shared vector. At the end, sort the vector and print it.","code":"use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};\nuse std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let list = Arc::new(Mutex::new(Vec::new()));\n    let mut handles = Vec::new();\n    for i in 0..5 {\n        let l = Arc::clone(&list);\n        handles.push(thread::spawn(move || {\n            // add i to the shared vector\n        }));\n    }\n    for h in handles {\n        h.join().unwrap();\n    }\n    let mut v = list.lock().unwrap().clone();\n    v.sort();\n    println!(\"{:?}\", v);\n}","solution":"use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};\nuse std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let list = Arc::new(Mutex::new(Vec::new()));\n    let mut handles = Vec::new();\n    for i in 0..5 {\n        let l = Arc::clone(&list);\n        handles.push(thread::spawn(move || {\n            l.lock().unwrap().push(i);\n        }));\n    }\n    for h in handles {\n        h.join().unwrap();\n    }\n    let mut v = list.lock().unwrap().clone();\n    v.sort();\n    println!(\"{:?}\", v);\n}","expect":"[0, 1, 2, 3, 4]","hints":["Take the lock and add the number: `l.lock().unwrap().push(i);`","Sorting is needed because threads finish in a random order."]},{"t":"task","md":"With `thread::scope`, calculate the sums of the two halves of a vector in two threads, then add them up.","code":"use std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let data: Vec<u64> = (1..=100).collect();\n    let (left, right) = data.split_at(50);\n    let mut a = 0;\n    let mut b = 0;\n    thread::scope(|s| {\n        // two threads: one sums left, the other right\n    });\n    println!(\"{}\", a + b);\n}","solution":"use std::thread;\n\nfn main() {\n    let data: Vec<u64> = (1..=100).collect();\n    let (left, right) = data.split_at(50);\n    let mut a = 0;\n    let mut b = 0;\n    thread::scope(|s| {\n        s.spawn(|| a = left.iter().sum());\n        s.spawn(|| b = right.iter().sum());\n    });\n    println!(\"{}\", a + b);\n}","expect":"5050","hints":["Inside `scope`, threads are started with `s.spawn(|| ...)`.","`s.spawn(|| a = left.iter().sum());` and the same for `b`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`move` gives variables to a thread; `join` returns the result.","A channel: `tx.clone()` for several senders.","`Arc<Mutex<T>>` for shared data, `thread::scope` for threads that borrow."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Start threads and collect results","Pass data through channels","Share data with `Arc<Mutex<T>>`"]}]},{"id":"m18","title":"Async and macros","sub":"An overview of advanced topics","lessons":[{"id":"m18-1","title":"Async: waiting without idling","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A waiter instead of a cook for every table","md":"A program often **waits**: for an answer from a server, for a file to be read. While it waits, the processor is idle.\n\n> Analogy: threads are like hiring a cook for every table. Async is one waiter: he takes an order, brings it to the kitchen, and while the dish is cooking he serves other tables.\n\nAsync pays off when there are very many tasks and each has little work: web servers, chats, network programs."},{"t":"text","title":"async and .await","md":"- `async fn` is a function that returns not a result, but a **promise** of a result (a future).\n- `.await` means \"wait for the promise, and meanwhile let others work\".\n\n```\nasync fn load_user() -> String {\n    String::from(\"Anna\")\n}\n\nasync fn hello() {\n    let name = load_user().await;\n    println!(\"Hello, {name}\");\n}\n```"},{"t":"text","title":"An executor is needed","md":"A future is lazy by itself, like an iterator. Someone must \"drive\" it. This is done by a **runtime** — an executor.\n\nThe standard library has no runtime. Real projects use the crate **tokio**:\n\n```text\n#[tokio::main]\nasync fn main() {\n    hello().await;\n}\n```"},{"t":"code","md":"For the curious: a tiny executor using only the standard library. You do not need to understand every line.","code":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::pin;\nuse std::sync::Arc;\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Wake};\n\nstruct Noop;\nimpl Wake for Noop {\n    fn wake(self: Arc<Self>) {}\n}\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> F::Output {\n    let waker = Arc::new(Noop).into();\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(&waker);\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nasync fn load_user() -> String {\n    String::from(\"Anna\")\n}\n\nasync fn hello() -> String {\n    let name = load_user().await;\n    format!(\"Hello, {name}\")\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", block_on(hello()));\n}","out":"Hello, Anna"},{"t":"quiz","md":"What happens if you call an `async fn` without `.await` and without an executor?","options":["The function runs immediately","Nothing: a future is created, but it does not run","The program panics"],"answer":1,"why":["A future is lazy: without an executor nothing happens.","Correct. Like an iterator without `collect`.","There is no panic; the code just does not run. The compiler warns about it."]},{"t":"text","title":"What to choose: threads or async","md":"- **Threads** — when there are few tasks and each one **computes** a lot: image processing, compression, calculations.\n- **Async** — when there are very many tasks and each one mostly **waits**: thousands of network connections, a chat, a web server.\n\nIf you are not sure and there are few tasks — start with threads. They are simpler."},{"t":"deep","title":"What a Future is inside","md":"The compiler turns an `async fn` into a **state machine**: a struct that remembers at which `.await` it stopped.\n\nA future has one main method — `poll`. The executor calls `poll`:\n- if the result is ready, `poll` returns `Poll::Ready(value)`;\n- if not — `Poll::Pending`, and the future promises to \"wake up\" the executor when it is worth trying again.\n\nWhile one future waits, the executor polls others. This is how one thread serves thousands of tasks."},{"t":"recap","items":["Async is for many tasks that mostly wait.","`async fn` returns a future, `.await` waits for it.","A runtime is needed. Real projects use tokio."]}],"goals":["How async differs from threads","What `async` and `.await` mean"]},{"id":"m18-2","title":"Macros","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A macro writes code for you","md":"You have used macros the whole course: `println!`, `vec!`, `format!`, `assert_eq!`. The exclamation mark is their sign.\n\nA **macro** is code that **writes code** before compilation. That is why `println!` can take any number of arguments, and a normal function cannot."},{"t":"code","md":"A simple macro with `macro_rules!`:","code":"macro_rules! square {\n    ($x:expr) => {\n        $x * $x\n    };\n}\n\nmacro_rules! hello_all {\n    ($($name:expr),*) => {\n        $( println!(\"Hello, {}!\", $name); )*\n    };\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", square!(7));\n    hello_all!(\"Anna\", \"Bob\", \"Vic\");\n}","out":"49\nHello, Anna!\nHello, Bob!\nHello, Vic!","after":"- `$x:expr` means \"put any expression here\".\n- `$(...),*` means \"repeat any number of times, separated by commas\"."},{"t":"text","title":"derive is a macro too","md":"`#[derive(Debug)]` is a **procedural macro**. It reads your struct and adds an implementation of the trait to it.\n\nPeople rarely write their own procedural macros. But it is useful to know that this is what they are."},{"t":"text","title":"unsafe — one line of truth","md":"Rust has the word `unsafe`. Inside such a block you can do things the compiler cannot check: work with raw pointers, call code written in C.\n\nA beginner does not need `unsafe`. Most Rust programs do not contain it at all."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why is `println!` a macro and not a function?","options":["It takes a different number of arguments and checks the format at compile time","Macros run faster than functions","It is historical; there is no difference"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct. A normal function in Rust cannot do that.","It is not about speed.","There is a difference: a macro expands into code before compilation."]},{"t":"text","title":"Useful macros of the standard library","md":"- `vec![...]`, `format!(...)`, `println!`, `print!`, `eprintln!` (print to the error stream);\n- `assert!`, `assert_eq!`, `assert_ne!`;\n- `dbg!(x)` — print the expression, its value and the line of code. Handy when debugging;\n- `todo!()` — \"not written yet\". It compiles, but panics when run;\n- `matches!(x, pattern)` — `true` if `x` fits the pattern.\n\n```\nlet c = 'z';\nlet is_letter = matches!(c, 'a'..='z' | 'A'..='Z'); // true\n```"},{"t":"deep","title":"Procedural macros","md":"`macro_rules!` substitutes code by a template. There is a second, more powerful kind — **procedural macros**. They are separate small Rust programs that receive code as a set of tokens and return new code.\n\nThree kinds:\n- `#[derive(...)]` — add a trait implementation;\n- attributes like `#[tokio::main]` — rework a function;\n- function-like ones, like `sql!(...)` — parse a mini-language of your own.\n\nThey are written in a separate crate marked `proc-macro = true`. A beginner does not need to write them, but it is useful to know where `#[derive]` and `#[tokio::main]` come from."},{"t":"recap","items":["Macros with `!` generate code before compilation.","`macro_rules!` makes your own simple macros.","`#[derive]` is a macro too.","`unsafe` exists, but a beginner does not need it."]}],"goals":["How a macro differs from a function","Write a simple macro with `macro_rules!`"]},{"id":"m18-p","title":"Practice: macros and async","min":12,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Write a macro `max!(a, b)` that returns the larger of two expressions.","code":"// write macro_rules! max\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", max!(3, 8));\n    println!(\"{}\", max!(10 - 1, 2 * 2));\n}","solution":"macro_rules! max {\n    ($a:expr, $b:expr) => {\n        if $a > $b { $a } else { $b }\n    };\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", max!(3, 8));\n    println!(\"{}\", max!(10 - 1, 2 * 2));\n}","expect":"8\n9","hints":["The template: `($a:expr, $b:expr) => { ... };`","The body: `if $a > $b { $a } else { $b }`"]},{"t":"task","md":"Write a macro `sum!` that adds up any number of numbers: `sum!(1, 2, 3)`.","code":"// write macro_rules! sum\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", sum!(1, 2, 3));\n    println!(\"{}\", sum!(10));\n}","solution":"macro_rules! sum {\n    ($($x:expr),*) => {\n        0 $(+ $x)*\n    };\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", sum!(1, 2, 3));\n    println!(\"{}\", sum!(10));\n}","expect":"6\n10","hints":["Repetition: `($($x:expr),*)` in the template and `$( ... )*` in the body.","The body: `0 $(+ $x)*` — it becomes `0 + 1 + 2 + 3`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Count the vowels in a string with `matches!`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let text = \"macro rules\";\n    let n = text.chars().filter(|c| false).count();\n    println!(\"{n}\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let text = \"macro rules\";\n    let n = text.chars().filter(|c| matches!(c, 'a' | 'e' | 'i' | 'o' | 'u')).count();\n    println!(\"{n}\");\n}","expect":"4","hints":["`matches!(c, 'a' | 'e' | 'i' | 'o' | 'u')`","Put this condition inside `filter`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Complete the async function `total` that gets two prices with `.await` and returns their sum. The small executor `block_on` is already written.","code":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::pin;\nuse std::sync::Arc;\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Wake};\n\nstruct Noop;\nimpl Wake for Noop {\n    fn wake(self: Arc<Self>) {}\n}\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> F::Output {\n    let waker = Arc::new(Noop).into();\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(&waker);\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nasync fn price(item: &str) -> u32 {\n    if item == \"tea\" { 100 } else { 250 }\n}\n\nasync fn total() -> u32 {\n    0\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", block_on(total()));\n}","solution":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::pin;\nuse std::sync::Arc;\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Wake};\n\nstruct Noop;\nimpl Wake for Noop {\n    fn wake(self: Arc<Self>) {}\n}\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> F::Output {\n    let waker = Arc::new(Noop).into();\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(&waker);\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nasync fn price(item: &str) -> u32 {\n    if item == \"tea\" { 100 } else { 250 }\n}\n\nasync fn total() -> u32 {\n    let a = price(\"tea\").await;\n    let b = price(\"cake\").await;\n    a + b\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", block_on(total()));\n}","expect":"350","hints":["The result of an async function is taken with `.await`: `let a = price(\"tea\").await;`","Return `a + b`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`macro_rules!` + `$x:expr` + `$(...)*` make your own simple macros.","`matches!`, `dbg!`, `todo!` are handy built-in macros.","`.await` takes the result out of an async function."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Write simple macros","Use macros of the standard library","Write async functions"]}]},{"id":"m19","title":"Final project","sub":"Putting it all together","lessons":[{"id":"m19-1","title":"Project: a to-do list. Step 1 — the data","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"What we are building","md":"A small task manager. It can:\n- add tasks;\n- mark tasks as done;\n- show the list;\n- run commands from text like `add buy bread`.\n\nWe go step by step. Each step is a separate small win."},{"t":"text","title":"The data types","md":"We start with the data. A task is a struct. Its state is an enum.\n\n```\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]\nenum Status {\n    Todo,\n    Done,\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone)]\nstruct Task {\n    id: u32,\n    title: String,\n    status: Status,\n}\n```"},{"t":"task","md":"Create a struct `TodoList` with the fields `tasks: Vec<Task>` and `next_id: u32`, and a constructor `new()` where the list is empty and `next_id` is 1. The program must print `0 1`.","code":"#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]\nenum Status {\n    Todo,\n    Done,\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone)]\nstruct Task {\n    id: u32,\n    title: String,\n    status: Status,\n}\n\n// your struct TodoList and an impl with new()\n\nfn main() {\n    let list = TodoList::new();\n    println!(\"{} {}\", list.tasks.len(), list.next_id);\n}","solution":"#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]\nenum Status {\n    Todo,\n    Done,\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone)]\nstruct Task {\n    id: u32,\n    title: String,\n    status: Status,\n}\n\nstruct TodoList {\n    tasks: Vec<Task>,\n    next_id: u32,\n}\n\nimpl TodoList {\n    fn new() -> Self {\n        TodoList { tasks: Vec::new(), next_id: 1 }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let list = TodoList::new();\n    println!(\"{} {}\", list.tasks.len(), list.next_id);\n}","expect":"0 1","hints":["`struct TodoList { tasks: Vec<Task>, next_id: u32 }`","`impl TodoList { fn new() -> Self { TodoList { tasks: Vec::new(), next_id: 1 } } }`"]},{"t":"recap","items":["First think about the data, then about the actions.","A struct describes \"what it is\", an enum describes \"which state it is in\"."]}],"goals":["Design the types for a program","Combine a struct and an enum"]},{"id":"m19-2","title":"Step 2 — the actions","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Complete the methods `add` (returns the id of the new task) and `done` (marks a task; if the id is not found — `Err` with a text). The method `show` is ready.","code":"#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]\nenum Status { Todo, Done }\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone)]\nstruct Task { id: u32, title: String, status: Status }\n\nstruct TodoList { tasks: Vec<Task>, next_id: u32 }\n\nimpl TodoList {\n    fn new() -> Self {\n        TodoList { tasks: Vec::new(), next_id: 1 }\n    }\n\n    fn add(&mut self, title: &str) -> u32 {\n        // create a Task, put it into tasks, increase next_id, return the id\n        0\n    }\n\n    fn done(&mut self, id: u32) -> Result<(), String> {\n        // find the task with this id and change its status\n        Err(format!(\"No task {id}\"))\n    }\n\n    fn show(&self) {\n        for t in &self.tasks {\n            let mark = if t.status == Status::Done { \"x\" } else { \" \" };\n            println!(\"[{mark}] {}. {}\", t.id, t.title);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut list = TodoList::new();\n    list.add(\"buy bread\");\n    list.add(\"learn Rust\");\n    list.done(2).unwrap();\n    list.show();\n    println!(\"{:?}\", list.done(99));\n}","solution":"#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]\nenum Status { Todo, Done }\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone)]\nstruct Task { id: u32, title: String, status: Status }\n\nstruct TodoList { tasks: Vec<Task>, next_id: u32 }\n\nimpl TodoList {\n    fn new() -> Self {\n        TodoList { tasks: Vec::new(), next_id: 1 }\n    }\n\n    fn add(&mut self, title: &str) -> u32 {\n        let id = self.next_id;\n        self.tasks.push(Task { id, title: title.to_string(), status: Status::Todo });\n        self.next_id += 1;\n        id\n    }\n\n    fn done(&mut self, id: u32) -> Result<(), String> {\n        match self.tasks.iter_mut().find(|t| t.id == id) {\n            Some(task) => {\n                task.status = Status::Done;\n                Ok(())\n            }\n            None => Err(format!(\"No task {id}\")),\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn show(&self) {\n        for t in &self.tasks {\n            let mark = if t.status == Status::Done { \"x\" } else { \" \" };\n            println!(\"[{mark}] {}. {}\", t.id, t.title);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut list = TodoList::new();\n    list.add(\"buy bread\");\n    list.add(\"learn Rust\");\n    list.done(2).unwrap();\n    list.show();\n    println!(\"{:?}\", list.done(99));\n}","expect":"[ ] 1. buy bread\n[x] 2. learn Rust\nErr(\"No task 99\")","hints":["In `add`: remember `let id = self.next_id;`, then `self.tasks.push(Task { ... })`, then `self.next_id += 1;` and return `id`.","In `done`: `self.tasks.iter_mut().find(|t| t.id == id)` returns `Option<&mut Task>`. Check it with `match`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`iter_mut().find(...)` finds an element in order to change it.","No element — an honest `Err`, not a panic."]}],"goals":["Write the methods add, done and showing the list","Return an error with `Result` if there is no such task"]},{"id":"m19-3","title":"Step 3 — commands","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A command as an enum","md":"The user writes text. Let us turn it into a clear type:\n\n```\nenum Command {\n    Add(String),\n    Done(u32),\n    List,\n}\n```\nThe function `parse` turns a string into `Result<Command, String>`. Input mistakes are not a panic, but an `Err`."},{"t":"task","md":"Complete `parse`: `add text` → `Add`, `done number` → `Done`, `list` → `List`, everything else → `Err`. The rest of the program is ready.","code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nenum Command {\n    Add(String),\n    Done(u32),\n    List,\n}\n\nfn parse(line: &str) -> Result<Command, String> {\n    let (cmd, rest) = match line.trim().split_once(' ') {\n        Some((c, r)) => (c, r.trim()),\n        None => (line.trim(), \"\"),\n    };\n    match cmd {\n        // your branches\n        _ => Err(format!(\"Unknown command \\\"{cmd}\\\"\")),\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for line in [\"add buy bread\", \"done 1\", \"list\", \"done abc\", \"jump\"] {\n        println!(\"{line} -> {:?}\", parse(line));\n    }\n}","solution":"#[derive(Debug)]\nenum Command {\n    Add(String),\n    Done(u32),\n    List,\n}\n\nfn parse(line: &str) -> Result<Command, String> {\n    let (cmd, rest) = match line.trim().split_once(' ') {\n        Some((c, r)) => (c, r.trim()),\n        None => (line.trim(), \"\"),\n    };\n    match cmd {\n        \"add\" if !rest.is_empty() => Ok(Command::Add(rest.to_string())),\n        \"done\" => rest\n            .parse::<u32>()\n            .map(Command::Done)\n            .map_err(|_| format!(\"\\\"{rest}\\\" is not a task number\")),\n        \"list\" => Ok(Command::List),\n        _ => Err(format!(\"Unknown command \\\"{cmd}\\\"\")),\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for line in [\"add buy bread\", \"done 1\", \"list\", \"done abc\", \"jump\"] {\n        println!(\"{line} -> {:?}\", parse(line));\n    }\n}","expect":"add buy bread -> Ok(Add(\"buy bread\"))\ndone 1 -> Ok(Done(1))\nlist -> Ok(List)\ndone abc -> Err(\"\\\"abc\\\" is not a task number\")\njump -> Err(\"Unknown command \\\"jump\\\"\")","hints":["The branch for add: `\"add\" => Ok(Command::Add(rest.to_string())),`","For done, parse the number: `match rest.parse::<u32>() { Ok(n) => Ok(Command::Done(n)), Err(_) => Err(format!(\"\\\"{rest}\\\" is not a task number\")) }`"]},{"t":"text","title":"You built a real program","md":"Look at what it contains:\n- structs and enums;\n- methods and a constructor;\n- `Vec`, iterators, closures;\n- `Option`, `Result`, `match`, errors without panics.\n\nThis is the base of any Rust program. From here on it is only more of the same."},{"t":"recap","items":["Text from the user → your own `enum` through `parse`.","Input mistakes → `Err`, not a panic.","A program = data + actions + parsing commands."]}],"goals":["Parse a text command into an enum","Connect everything with `match`"]},{"id":"m19-4","title":"What next","min":5,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"You finished the course","md":"This is a long road. You worked through ownership — a topic where many experienced programmers stumble.\n\nIt is fine if you forgot something. Look into the **Cheat sheet** and **Review** — that is what they are for."},{"t":"text","title":"What to read and practice","md":"- [The Rust Book](https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/) — the official book. Now it will make sense.\n- [Rustlings](https://rustlings.rust-lang.org/) — 95 small exercises on your own computer. Very much in the spirit of this course.\n- [Rust by Example](https://doc.rust-lang.org/rust-by-example/) — code examples by topic.\n- [Exercism, Rust track](https://exercism.org/tracks/rust) — tasks with checks and feedback from mentors."},{"t":"text","title":"How not to give up","md":"- Write small programs for yourself: an expense calculator, a file renamer, a bot.\n- 15 minutes a day is better than 3 hours once a month.\n- Stuck on a compiler error? Read it fully once more. There is almost always a `help`.\n- Breaks are part of learning, not a failure."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Which path best strengthens your knowledge?","options":["Reread the whole course","Write small programs of your own and solve exercises","Start a big complex project right away"],"answer":1,"why":["Rereading is remembered worse than practice.","Correct. Recalling and applying is the best way to learn.","You may get stuck. Many small wins are better."]},{"t":"deep","title":"How to read the documentation","md":"Almost everything about Rust is in the official documentation:\n- **doc.rust-lang.org/std** — the whole standard library. On the left is the list of modules; each type has a list of methods with examples.\n- **docs.rs** — the documentation of any crate from crates.io.\n- The command `rustup doc` opens the documentation **without internet**.\n\nHow to find a method: open the page of a type (for example `Vec`), press `S` or click the search field and type a word, for example `sort`. Every method has a code example, and you can run it with the Run button."},{"t":"recap","items":["Next: the Rust Book, Rustlings, Rust by Example, Exercism.","Small projects and regularity.","The Cheat sheet and Review are always at hand."]}],"goals":["Where to go after the course","How not to give up"]},{"id":"m19-p","title":"Practice: more mini-projects","min":20,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"How to do this lesson","md":"There are four mini-projects here. Each one uses several topics of the course at once.\n\nIf you are stuck, open a hint. If that does not help, look at the solution and then rewrite it yourself without looking. That is good practice too."},{"t":"task","md":"Text statistics. Count the lines, words and letters (without spaces and line breaks). Print: `lines: 2, words: 6, letters: 25`.","code":"fn main() {\n    let text = \"Rust is fast\\nand very reliable\";\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let text = \"Rust is fast\\nand very reliable\";\n    let lines = text.lines().count();\n    let words = text.split_whitespace().count();\n    let letters = text.chars().filter(|c| !c.is_whitespace()).count();\n    println!(\"lines: {lines}, words: {words}, letters: {letters}\");\n}","expect":"lines: 2, words: 6, letters: 25","hints":["Lines — `text.lines().count()`, words — `split_whitespace().count()`.","Letters without spaces: `text.chars().filter(|c| !c.is_whitespace()).count()`."]},{"t":"task","md":"A temperature converter. Parse strings like `\"36.6C\"` and `\"100F\"` and convert them to the other scale. Wrong input — `error`.","code":"fn convert(input: &str) -> String {\n    String::from(\"error\")\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for s in [\"100C\", \"212F\", \"abc\", \"20X\"] {\n        println!(\"{s} -> {}\", convert(s));\n    }\n}","solution":"fn convert(input: &str) -> String {\n    let Some(unit) = input.chars().last() else {\n        return String::from(\"error\");\n    };\n    let number = &input[..input.len() - unit.len_utf8()];\n    let Ok(value) = number.parse::<f64>() else {\n        return String::from(\"error\");\n    };\n    match unit {\n        'C' => format!(\"{}F\", value * 1.8 + 32.0),\n        'F' => format!(\"{}C\", (value - 32.0) / 1.8),\n        _ => String::from(\"error\"),\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    for s in [\"100C\", \"212F\", \"abc\", \"20X\"] {\n        println!(\"{s} -> {}\", convert(s));\n    }\n}","expect":"100C -> 212F\n212F -> 100C\nabc -> error\n20X -> error","hints":["The last character is the unit: `input.chars().last()`. Everything before it is the number.","The number without the last character: `&input[..input.len() - unit.len_utf8()]`, then `parse::<f64>()` and `match unit { 'C' => ..., 'F' => ..., _ => ... }`."]},{"t":"task","md":"A wallet. There is a list of operations. Calculate the final balance with `fold`, starting from 100.","code":"enum Op {\n    Income(i64),\n    Expense(i64),\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let ops = [Op::Income(500), Op::Expense(120), Op::Expense(80), Op::Income(50)];\n    let balance = 0;\n    println!(\"{balance}\");\n}","solution":"enum Op {\n    Income(i64),\n    Expense(i64),\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let ops = [Op::Income(500), Op::Expense(120), Op::Expense(80), Op::Income(50)];\n    let balance = ops.iter().fold(100, |acc, op| match op {\n        Op::Income(n) => acc + n,\n        Op::Expense(n) => acc - n,\n    });\n    println!(\"{balance}\");\n}","expect":"450","hints":["`ops.iter().fold(100, |acc, op| match op { ... })`","The branches: `Op::Income(n) => acc + n,` and `Op::Expense(n) => acc - n,`"]},{"t":"task","md":"The top 2 most frequent words. Count the words in a `HashMap`, move the pairs into a vector, sort by count in descending order and print the first two.","code":"use std::collections::HashMap;\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = \"cat dog cat fish dog cat\";\n    let mut counts: HashMap<&str, u32> = HashMap::new();\n    for w in text.split_whitespace() {\n        *counts.entry(w).or_insert(0) += 1;\n    }\n    // sort and print the top 2\n}","solution":"use std::collections::HashMap;\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = \"cat dog cat fish dog cat\";\n    let mut counts: HashMap<&str, u32> = HashMap::new();\n    for w in text.split_whitespace() {\n        *counts.entry(w).or_insert(0) += 1;\n    }\n    let mut pairs: Vec<(&str, u32)> = counts.into_iter().collect();\n    pairs.sort_by(|a, b| b.1.cmp(&a.1));\n    for (word, n) in pairs.iter().take(2) {\n        println!(\"{word}: {n}\");\n    }\n}","expect":"cat: 3\ndog: 2","hints":["`let mut pairs: Vec<(&str, u32)> = counts.into_iter().collect();`","Descending: `pairs.sort_by(|a, b| b.1.cmp(&a.1));`, then `pairs.iter().take(2)`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["A big task = several small ones: parse the input, calculate, print.","Input mistakes are handled with `let ... else` and `match`, without panics.","`fold`, `sort_by`, `HashMap` are everyday working tools."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Combine the knowledge of the course in small programs","Practice without theory hints"]}]}],"glossary":[{"term":"Program","en":"","def":"A list of commands for a computer. They run in order, from top to bottom."},{"term":"Compiler","en":"rustc","def":"A program that checks code and translates it into machine commands."},{"term":"Function","en":"fn","def":"A piece of code with a name. Declared with `fn`, called as `name()`."},{"term":"Macro","en":"name!","def":"Code that writes code before compilation. Recognized by `!`: `println!`, `vec!`."},{"term":"Variable","en":"let","def":"A box with a name and a value. Created with `let`."},{"term":"Mutable variable","en":"let mut","def":"A variable that can be changed."},{"term":"Constant","en":"const","def":"A value that never changes: `const MAX: u32 = 10;`."},{"term":"Shadowing","en":"","def":"A new `let` with the same name creates a new variable; the old one is hidden."},{"term":"Type","en":"","def":"What is stored in a variable: a number, text, a `bool` and so on."},{"term":"Expression","en":"","def":"Code that has a value: `2 + 2`, a function call, a block `{}`."},{"term":"Statement","en":"","def":"Code that does something but gives no value. Usually ends with `;`."},{"term":"Scope","en":"","def":"The part of the code where a variable is visible. Usually its block `{}`."},{"term":"Comment","en":"//","def":"A note for people after `//`. The computer skips it."},{"term":"Loop","en":"loop, while, for","def":"Repeating code."},{"term":"Range","en":"1..5, 1..=5","def":"A sequence of numbers: `1..5` (without 5) or `1..=5` (with 5)."},{"term":"Tuple","en":"(a, b)","def":"Several values of different types in parentheses: `(\"Anna\", 25)`."},{"term":"Array","en":"[T; N]","def":"A row of values of one type with a fixed length: `[1, 2, 3]`."},{"term":"Vector","en":"Vec","def":"A list of one type that can grow: `vec![1, 2, 3]`."},{"term":"Index","en":"","def":"The number of an element. Counting starts from zero."},{"term":"Panic","en":"panic!","def":"An emergency stop of the program. For example, when an index is out of bounds."},{"term":"Stack","en":"","def":"Fast memory for data of known size. Like a pile of plates."},{"term":"Heap","en":"","def":"Memory for data that can grow. Like a storage room with tickets."},{"term":"Ownership","en":"","def":"The rules: a value has one owner; when the owner is gone, the value is deleted."},{"term":"Owner","en":"","def":"The variable that is responsible for a value and its deletion."},{"term":"Move","en":"","def":"Passing ownership: `let b = a;` for a `String`. After this `a` cannot be used."},{"term":"Clone","en":".clone()","def":"A full copy of a value."},{"term":"Copy","en":"Copy","def":"The trait of simple types (numbers, `bool`, `char`) that are copied automatically."},{"term":"Reference","en":"&x","def":"Access to a value without ownership."},{"term":"Borrowing","en":"","def":"Creating a reference. Like lending a book."},{"term":"Mutable reference","en":"&mut x","def":"A reference through which you can change the value. Only one can exist at a time."},{"term":"Dangling reference","en":"","def":"A reference to a value that no longer exists. Rust does not allow them."},{"term":"Slice","en":"&v[1..3]","def":"A reference to a part of a sequence."},{"term":"String slice","en":"&str","def":"A reference to text. Text in quotes is also a `&str`."},{"term":"String","en":"String","def":"An owning string in the heap. It can grow."},{"term":"UTF-8","en":"","def":"A way to store text. An English letter takes 1 byte, a Russian letter 2, an emoji 4."},{"term":"Struct","en":"struct","def":"Your own type made of named fields."},{"term":"Method","en":"impl","def":"A function of a type, written in `impl` and called with a dot."},{"term":"Constructor","en":"new()","def":"A function that creates a value. Usually `new()`."},{"term":"Enum","en":"enum","def":"A type whose value is one variant from a list."},{"term":"Pattern matching","en":"match","def":"Choosing a branch by the shape of a value: `match`, `if let`."},{"term":"Option","en":"Some / None","def":"`Some(value)` or `None`. Rust's replacement for `null`."},{"term":"Result","en":"Ok / Err","def":"`Ok(value)` or `Err(error)`. For operations that can fail."},{"term":"Question mark operator","en":"?","def":"Take the `Ok` value, and on an error return it from the function right away."},{"term":"HashMap","en":"HashMap","def":"A dictionary: key → value."},{"term":"Module","en":"mod","def":"A named part of code: `mod name { }`."},{"term":"Crate","en":"","def":"A program or library that is built as a whole."},{"term":"Cargo","en":"cargo","def":"The tool for creating, building, running and testing projects."},{"term":"rustup","en":"rustup","def":"The installer and updater of Rust."},{"term":"Generics","en":"<T>","def":"Code for any type: `fn f<T>(x: T)`."},{"term":"Trait","en":"trait","def":"A description of a skill of a type: a set of methods."},{"term":"Trait bound","en":"T: Trait","def":"A requirement on a type: `T: Display`."},{"term":"derive","en":"#[derive]","def":"Automatic implementation of traits: `#[derive(Debug)]`."},{"term":"Lifetime","en":"'a","def":"How long a reference lives. A label like `'a`."},{"term":"'static","en":"'static","def":"The lifetime \"the whole program\". Text in quotes has it."},{"term":"Closure","en":"closure","def":"A function without a name, made on the spot: `|x| x + 1`. It sees the variables around it."},{"term":"Iterator","en":"Iterator","def":"Gives out elements one by one. Works with `map`, `filter`, `collect`."},{"term":"Laziness","en":"","def":"An iterator does nothing until the result is requested."},{"term":"Test","en":"#[test]","def":"A function with `#[test]` that checks code."},{"term":"Smart pointer","en":"","def":"The address of a value plus ownership rules: `Box`, `Rc`, `Arc`."},{"term":"Box","en":"Box","def":"Puts a value into the heap."},{"term":"Rc","en":"Rc","def":"Several owners of one value in one thread."},{"term":"RefCell","en":"RefCell","def":"Lets you change a value through shared access. The check happens at run time."},{"term":"Thread","en":"thread::spawn","def":"A separate line of execution of a program."},{"term":"Channel","en":"mpsc::channel","def":"A pipe for messages between threads."},{"term":"Mutex","en":"Mutex","def":"A lock: lets threads to the data one at a time."},{"term":"Arc","en":"Arc","def":"Like `Rc`, but safe for threads."},{"term":"async","en":"async / .await","def":"A way to write code that waits a lot without taking up threads."},{"term":"Future","en":"Future","def":"A promise of a result. It is run by an executor after `.await`."},{"term":"Runtime","en":"","def":"An executor of async code. Real projects use tokio."},{"term":"unsafe","en":"unsafe","def":"A block where the compiler does not check some rules. A beginner does not need it."},{"term":"Edition","en":"edition","def":"A version of the language rules. The current one is 2024."},{"term":"Rust Playground","en":"","def":"A website that runs Rust code. This course uses it."},{"term":"Dereference","en":"*r","def":"Getting the value behind a reference: `*r`."},{"term":"Recursion","en":"","def":"A function calls itself."},{"term":"Escaping","en":"\\","def":"Writing special characters inside text with `\\`: `\\\"`, `\\n`."},{"term":"Doc comment","en":"///","def":"A `///` comment before a function. The editor shows it as a hint."},{"term":"Call stack","en":"","def":"The chain of called functions: who called whom. On a panic, a backtrace shows it."},{"term":"Unit","en":"()","def":"The empty value `()`. Functions without a result return it."},{"term":"Tuple struct","en":"","def":"A struct without field names: `struct Meters(f64);`."},{"term":"Match guard","en":"if","def":"A condition in a `match` branch: `n if n < 0 => ...`."},{"term":"Monomorphization","en":"","def":"The compiler makes a separate copy of a generic function for each type. That is why generics are fast."},{"term":"Dynamic dispatch","en":"dyn","def":"Calling a method through a method table, like with `Box<dyn Trait>`."},{"term":"Weak","en":"Weak","def":"A \"weak\" reference from `Rc`. It does not keep the value alive and helps avoid leaks in cycles."},{"term":"Cell","en":"Cell","def":"Simple interior mutability for `Copy` types: `get` and `set`."},{"term":"Send and Sync","en":"Send, Sync","def":"Markers of types: can a value be passed to another thread, can it be looked at from several threads."},{"term":"Deadlock","en":"","def":"Two threads wait for each other's locks and stand still forever."},{"term":"Atomic number","en":"AtomicUsize","def":"A number that can be safely changed from several threads without a lock."},{"term":"SemVer","en":"","def":"The version rule `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`. A major version may break compatibility."},{"term":"Cargo.lock","en":"Cargo.lock","def":"A file with the exact versions of the libraries the project was built with."},{"term":"Release","en":"--release","def":"The build mode with optimization: `cargo build --release`. The program runs faster."},{"term":"State machine","en":"","def":"What the compiler turns an `async fn` into: a struct that remembers where it stopped."},{"term":"Procedural macro","en":"","def":"A macro that is a program: it receives code and returns new code. For example `#[derive]`."},{"term":"Integration test","en":"tests/","def":"A test in the folder `tests/` that checks a library from the outside."}]};
